{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\nsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\nexhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n\n**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\nhappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\nappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\nidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\nno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\ncorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n\n**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\na timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\nto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n\n**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\nadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\ntimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\nwhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\nthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\nto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\ndeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\nmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\nnot an upward callback.\n\nHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\nplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\ntransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\nadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\nevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\nfor facts it owns.\n\nWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\nadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\ntransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\nthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\nconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n\nA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\nnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\nhappen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\nevent. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\ndoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\nnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\ninterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n\nTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\ncall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\nappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\ncallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\nthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\ndirections wearing the shape of an event.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git status --short; rg --files collab | rg 'phase7-plan|7[a-c]-(report|review)|7d-stage-e.report'; cat collab/phase7-plan.md collab/7a-report.md collab/7a-review.md collab/7b-report.md collab/7c-report.md collab/7c-review.md collab/1792500000000-engineer-7d-stage-e.report.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git status --short; rg --files collab | rg 'phase7-plan|7[a-c]-(report|review)|7d-stage-e.report'; cat collab/phase7-plan.md collab/7a-report.md collab/7a-review.md collab/7b-report.md collab/7c-report.md collab/7c-review.md collab/1792500000000-engineer-7d-stage-e.report.md\"","aggregated_output":"?? collab/1792450000000-engineer-7b-holder-export.report.md\n?? collab/1792480000000-engineer-7c-compose-fencing.report.md\n?? collab/1792500000000-engineer-7d-stage-e.report.md\n?? collab/1792530000000-engineer-7e-docs.codex.stdout.log\n?? collab/1792530000000-engineer-7e-docs.prompt.md\n?? collab/7a-report.md\n?? collab/7c-review.md\n?? collab/phase7-plan.md\ncollab/phase7-plan.md\ncollab/7c-review.md\ncollab/1792500000000-engineer-7d-stage-e.report.md\ncollab/7a-report.md\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-13 16:10 +07\n# Phase 7 plan: Stage D/E rerun over the row carrier (Architect, plan only)\n\nAuthor: claude-fable-5-1. Worktree `srvm-phase7-plan` at `781a9ba8` (phases 1-6 merged). Read: the four design documents named in the brief, `rows.cljc`, `handoff.cljc` (export-task, the reader, resume-task*, the recovery paths), `holder/{export,driver,writer,reader}.cljc`, `checkpoint.cljc`, `custody.cljc`, `ledger.cljc`, `authority/{grant,completion,admission}.cljc`, `compose.cljc`, the Stage E world, scenarios, peer, process test and rows test, the checkpoint and ledger fixtures and tests, `lift_support.cljc`, `handoff_v2_holder_test.cljc`, the semantic-register safepoint harness, `dao.jing.{file,mem}` put contracts, `yin.repl`'s composition wiring. No code or doc was edited.\n\n## Summary for the owner (15 lines)\n\n1. Every Stage D/E test today runs on the **old** `yin.vm.semantic` kernel through `handoff.cljc` bodies (version 0 stream-CBOR, version 1 and 2 jing-CBOR); the row carrier (version 3) runs only on the semantic-register kernel and only with `custody nil`.\n2. Phase 7 is therefore one move, done in four slices: teach the row carrier custody, re-base the holder (export record, recovery object, driver lower) and the authority's inspector on row packs, then rerun the composition and the Stage E crash matrix on the semantic-register kernel, three hosts.\n3. The driver, writer, reader, compose, authority, ledger and front are **kernel-agnostic**: they read machine fields that `yin.vm.engine` owns (`:wait-set`, `:installs`, `:yin.k/gate`, `:yin.k/custody`). They change only where they call `handoff/*` or `checkpoint/inspect` (eight call sites).\n4. Nothing of version 0/1/2 is rewritten; it dies at phase 8. Phase 7 deletes only what it replaces: the version-1 export-recovery object, the holder's use of `export-task`/`resume-task`, the authority's use of the version-1 inspector. The old codec and its direct tests stay green and dead until phase 8.\n5. One hard fact drives the design: `dao.jing` content stores **verify bytes against the address on put**, and a row pack's bytes do not hash to its root id. So the checkpoint has two identities: the pack address (storage, the ledger's `:yin.k/id`) and the root id (semantic, inside the baseline). `emit` must sort its rows so one root gives one pack on every host.\n6. Custody travels as the root's `custody` slot exactly as the draft's §5.5 table says (policy, occurrence, arbitration, next-op-seq, origin); lease, epoch and holder never enter a body (UCF 7.2.1 stands); op-ids already have their slots on `:put`, `:ffi-request` and `:link-request` pendings.\n7. The recovery object loses its second \"snapshot\" body (rows already carry the whole store and every parked record) and keeps only holder-local engine state (gate, issues, issued, origins) beside the pack.\n8. Crash cuts stay meaningful unchanged if the pack is stored as **one** blob; storing rows individually would add a torn-pack cut family and is not recommended.\n9. `:resume-mode` stays out: the version-2 stack lane is frozen until phase 8, and rows carry `:act`/`:tail`, which subsume it. No work.\n10. Three questions need the owner: fork spelling in the custody slot (nil as landed, or the draft's explicit `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}`); the REPL custody gap (the REPL composes the old kernel until phase 8, so its custody path is dead between 7 and 8 unless the kernel switch is pulled forward); whether the Q3 install checks (link-id = parent, image address = manifest) land in 7b.\n11. Slices: 7a custody on rows and the rows inspector with regenerated fixtures; 7b holder export, recovery v2 and the driver's lower; 7c the composition and the compose/fencing row tests; 7d the Stage E world and matrix across processes; 7e docs. 7a → 7b → 7c → 7d, with 7e and the 7d world port overlapping 7c.\n12. Estimate: 12 working days median (9 implementation, the rest 2-4 codex rounds per slice and the slow-tier runs), 17 at the worst; phase 6's three slices landed in three calendar days with the design pre-written, so calendar could be shorter.\n13. Biggest risks: the identity split surprising the ledger's variant rules (mitigated by sorting rows); the fenced-wait rehydration of install children on the semantic-register kernel, exercised only by the harness today; Dart runs only the in-process Stage E form (as today).\n14. Nothing found that forces a redesign of rows or the ledger; the de Bruijn lanes are not Stage E participants (the 61/350 matrix covers only the cross-engine laws).\n15. Verdict at the end: **NOT READY** until the owner answers the three questions in §3.10; everything else is decided below and can start on 7a the moment the fork-spelling answer lands.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Inventory: every Stage D/E scenario and test, its transport today, what it needs on rows\n\nVocabulary (UCF 7.5.3): a *cursor* is the immutable `dao.stream` value; a *cursor cell* is the carrier's box (`:yin.k/cc-<n>` in rows, `:yin.k/c-<n>` in versions 0-2); a *cursor-ref* is the sealed reference a program holds; a *cell* is only the heap box.\n\nTransport codes: **v0** = `handoff/export-task` with a nil header, stream CBOR, fork; **v1** = the same with a header, jing CBOR, exclusive; **v2** = `{:version 2}`, the four-profile body (frozen); **rec1** = the export-recovery object version 1 (`holder.export/freeze`, embeds a v0/v1/v2 body plus a full-census snapshot); **ckpt** = `checkpoint/inspect` over v1/v2 bytes; **ledger** = `yin.vm.ucf.ledger` records (no body); **rows** = `rows/lift-task`/`lower-task`, version 3.\n\n### 1.1 Stage E (the acceptance matrix, `stage_e_*`)\n\nThe world (`stage_e_world.cljc`) builds both machines with `lift-support/new-machine`, which is `yin.vm.semantic/create-vm` (old kernel). Every life drives `compose` only; the export is v1 (`:export-version` nil), the recovery object rec1, the offer carries v1 bytes, the authority inspects with ckpt. Dart runs the in-process form only; JVM and Node also run the separate-process form over the four ordered pairs.\n\n| Scenario (ids) | Count | Parking shape (continuation §5.2 row) | Transport today | Needs on rows |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `[:fork]` | 1 | row 1 (blocked read, `:parked` world) | v0 lift, no bracket | rows lift with no header → root custody nil (or fork spelling, §3.1); the driver's `fork-step` answers pack bytes and address |\n| `[:row-7 cut]` × 5 cuts | 5 | row 1 at the source; successor is the same shape | v1 export, rec1, ckpt on offer and report | rows export under header; recovery v2; rows inspector on offer and report; successor pack address as `:yin.k/result` |\n| `[:row-7 :failure-lower]`, `[:row-7 :lost-authority]` | 2 | row 1 | v1, ckpt | as above; failure-lower is `attach!` returning not-found inside `lower-task` step 11 |\n| `[:row-4 cut]` × 3 | 3 | row 1 then row 2 (`:rows` world: read, write to the enrolled target, write to the side log, halt) | v1 with an op-id on the `:put`, ckpt baseline, admission | rows lift with the enrolled set (new `:unprotected-pending`/unenrolled checks); op-id slot already exists; baseline from the rows inspector |\n| `[:row-3 variant]` × 2 | 2 | rows 1, 2 | v1, admission | as row 4 |\n| `[:row-5 variant]` × 2 | 2 | rows 1, 2; evicted kept cursor (gap) | v1; input records | unchanged protocol; the kept cursor position is a cursor-cell body `[stream position]` in rows |\n| `[:row-2 cut]` × 14 cuts | 14 | row 1 | v1 export phases: mint, serve, store recovery (`store-put-*`), store body (`body-put-*`), fence, offer | the same phases over the pack; see §3.4 for why the cut set stays |\n| `[:row-2-carrier outcome]` × 4 | 4 | row 1 | v1 offer appends, abort | unchanged (`export/abort` is machine-level) |\n| `[:row-1]` | 1 | row 1; a second encoding minted by rewriting `:yin.k/id-counter` in the CBOR body | v1 variant bytes | the variant must be re-emitted: decode pack → change the root's id-counter slot → `emit` → new root id and new pack; equal baseline (§3.3) |\n| `[:row-6 ...]` × 3 | 3 | row 1 | v1; partition and lost reply | unchanged |\n| `[:clause-8 variant]` × 2, `[:clause-8 :closed]` | 3 | rows 1, 2; cross-target | v1, admission, inherited ids read the accepted checkpoint through ckpt | `accepted-baseline` must inspect the stored pack with the rows inspector |\n| `[:clause-3 :regrant]`, `[:clause-3 :carried-put]` | 2 | row 2 with an id assigned before export | v1 carries the id | rows carry `:op-id` on the `:put` pending slot; the successor's counter from the custody slot |\n| `[:journal cut]` × 7, `{:fail ...}` × 2 | 9 | row 1 | v1; inbox retention, journal brackets | unchanged except the fence record's two addresses are recovery v2 and the pack |\n\nTotal: 51 scenarios (`all-scenarios`), 12 `deftest`s in `stage_e_rows_test` (8 slow) and 12 in `stage_e_process_test` (all slow, JVM only, four host pairs, Node peer compiled by shadow with the peer as main).\n\n**What the world itself needs:** `parked-reader-over` and `reading-writer` built with `yin.vm.semantic-register/create-vm` and `sr/load-vector` over `linearize/project` (as the harness's `machine`/`load-program` do), a `:source-index` supplied to the lift (the composition's `{A #{tree}}`, continuation §6.1), `holder-seams` unchanged, and `offer-variant!` re-emitting a pack instead of patching CBOR.\n\n### 1.2 Stage D (the driver, the holder halves, the compose rows)\n\n| Test file (deftests) | Transport today | What it tests | Needs on rows |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `holder/driver_test.cljc` (118) | v0/v1/v2 through `handoff/resume-task`, `export/*`, rec1, ckpt | the whole driver: brackets, inbox, grant, lower, run, exit, reopen, abort, enrollment | machines from a semantic-register support namespace; the three driver call sites (`lower`, `step-validating`, `rebuilt-cell`/`reopen-holder`/`reopen-exit`) over rows; `a-version-two-fork-lift-is-the-composition-choice-test` deleted at 7 (its subject is the frozen v2 lane; nothing else exercises `:export-version 2` through the driver) |\n| `holder/export_test.cljc` (26) | v0/v1/v2 enter/prepare/encode/freeze/rehydrate/abort | the exporting state and recovery | `enter`, `abort`, hold refusals: unchanged (machine-level); prepare/encode/freeze/rehydrate: rewritten over rows and recovery v2; the \"snapshot extends body\" and \"transitive module store fixed point\" tests disappear (rows carry everything once) |\n| `holder/writer_test.cljc` (29) | machines with a hand-set `:yin.k/custody` map; no transport | assign, envelope, retention, discharge, closes | port the machine builders to the semantic-register kernel; logic unchanged (`writer.cljc` reads engine fields only) |\n| `holder/reader_test.cljc` (24) | same | held observations, replay, frontier | same |\n| `holder/evidence_test.cljc` (10) | ledger | binding evidence | none |\n| `fencing_rows_test.cljc` (4) | v1 lift + writer + reader + authority, no compose | rows 2, 4, 5 fencing halves | rows lift under header; `lower-task` with the grant opts |\n| `compose_test.cljc` (13) | v0/v1 through compose | open!, fork label, substrate checks, reopen, the plain composition | the `:receiver` and `:machine` seams become semantic-register machines; `the-plain-composition-loads-and-drives-with-no-yin-repl-test` reads the REPL's wiring (see §3.10 Q2) |\n| `compose_rows_test.cljc` (41, 1 slow) | v1 through compose; rec1; ckpt | rows 1-8 same host, completion recovery, journal cuts around freeze/storage/fencing, the content-store cuts | as compose; the content-store cut tests must see two puts (recovery v2, pack) as today |\n| `lift_v1_test.cljc` (29) | v1 `export-task` | header rules, enrolled refusals, canonical bytes, cursor cells | **kept until phase 8** (tests the old codec directly); its custody rules are re-pinned on rows in a new `rows_custody_test` (7a) |\n| `handoff_v2_custody_test.cljc` (5), `handoff_v2_holder_test.cljc` (6), the other `handoff_v2_*` (8 files), `handoff_test`, `handoff_v1_test`, `scalar_round_trip_test` | v0/v1/v2 codec | the old codec and layouts | kept until phase 8, except `a-version-2-recovery-snapshot-freezes-and-rehydrates-fenced` (tests rec1 freeze, deleted at 7) and `a-poisoned-receiver-behaves-as-a-clean-one` (goes through `resume-task` only, keep) |\n\n### 1.3 Stage C (the authority), where a body enters\n\n| Test file (deftests) | Body use today | Needs on rows |\n|---|---|---|\n| `checkpoint_fixtures.cljc`, `checkpoint_test.cljc` (14), `test/resources/.../checkpoint-v1.txt` | five bases lifted by the old semantic kernel through v1, 48 mutations, 3 byte anchors | **kept as the old inspector's test** until phase 8; a new `rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc` + `rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` pins packs lifted by the semantic-register kernel (same five bases, the mutations re-expressed as row edits with rehash, §5.2's \"raw row edit\" rule) |\n| `ledger_fixtures.cljc`, `ledger_fixture_test.cljc` (6), `ledger-v1.txt` | `park`/`halted` bodies derived from the checkpoint fixtures and offered/reported through `grant/offer!` and `completion/report!` | regenerate over packs (the projection digest and every frame change); file renamed `ledger-v2.txt` |\n| `crash_cut_test.cljc` (2) | `lf/world`, `lf/park` bodies | follows the ledger fixtures |\n| `authority/grant_test.cljc` (16), `completion_test.cljc` (21), `inherited_test.cljc` (18), `front_test.cljc` (29), `admission_test.cljc` (24), `input_test.cljc` (20), `reclaim_test.cljc` (21), `completion_fold_test.cljc` (8) | bodies through `checkpoint-fixtures`/`ledger-fixtures` where an offer, report or inherited id is admitted; otherwise ledger only | the fixture switch above; the inspector call in `grant/offer!`, `completion/report!`, `admission/accepted-baseline` becomes the rows inspector |\n| `ledger_test` (14), `custody_test` (4), `durability_test` (10), `evidence_test` | ledger only | none |\n\n### 1.4 Phase 6 assets phase 7 builds on (no change expected)\n\n`rows_test.cljc` (31), `semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc` (27; rows 1-24 of §5.2 on three lanes, laws 1-6), `frame_test`, `layout_test`. The harness's `machine`, `load-program`, `project`/`source-index` and `canonical-under` are the templates for the new support namespace.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Old transport paths: deleted at phase 7 versus kept until phase 8\n\n**Deleted or replaced at phase 7** (their subject is replaced, so keeping them would test a dead path):\n\n- `holder.export/prepare`, `encode`, `freeze`, `rehydrate-fenced` as written over `handoff/export-task`, `inspect-recovery-body` and `rehydrate-fenced-body`: rewritten over rows. `enter` and `abort` survive unchanged.\n- The export-recovery object version 1 (`:yin.k/export-recovery`, `:yin.k/version 1`, `:yin.k/snapshot-*`): replaced by version 2 (§3.2). No reader of version 1 remains.\n- The driver's calls: `handoff/resume-task` in `lower` and `step-validating`; `checkpoint/inspect` in `step-validating`, `rebuilt-cell`, `reopen-holder`; `export/rehydrate-fenced` in `rebuilt-cell`; `:export-version` as a seam (compose's `holder-config-keys` and `driver-config` drop it). The `fork-step` keeps its shape over the rows lift.\n- The authority's three inspector calls (`grant/offer!`, `completion/report!`, `admission/accepted-baseline`) switch to the rows inspector; the ledger's `:yin.k/baseline` shape gains `:yin.k/root` (§3.3).\n- `lift-support`'s role as the D/E machine source: superseded by a semantic-register support namespace; `lift-support` itself stays for the old codec's tests.\n- Tests deleted at 7: `driver_test/a-version-two-fork-lift-is-the-composition-choice-test`, `handoff_v2_holder_test/a-version-2-recovery-snapshot-freezes-and-rehydrates-fenced`, `export_test`'s rec1-specific rows (`complete-export-recovery-is-fenced-and-reproducible`, `module-store-dependencies-are-censused-to-a-fixed-point`, `version-one-recovery-retains-operation-ids-issues-and-local-enrollment`, `snapshot`-shaped assertions) are replaced by their rows equivalents in the same file.\n\n**Kept until phase 8, untouched** (the brief's rule: no new work on the old kernels and compilers):\n\n- `handoff.cljc` whole (version 0/1/2 lift, reader, `validate-body`, `rehydrate-fenced-body`, `inspect-recovery-body`; the last two become dead code with no caller).\n- `checkpoint.cljc` (the version-1/2 inspector) and `checkpoint_test`/`checkpoint_fixtures`/`checkpoint-v1.txt`.\n- `remote.cljc`'s version 0-2 pending lift/lower (`lift-frame`, `lower-frame`, `lift-pending`, `lower-pending`); rows uses only `lift-marker`/`lower-marker`/`cursor-profiles`.\n- `lift_v1_test`, `handoff_test`, `handoff_v1_test`, every `handoff_v2_*` test but the one deftest above, `scalar_round_trip_test`, `v2_support`, `lift_support`, `handoff-v2.txt`, `scalars-v1.*`.\n- `custody.cljc`, `ledger.cljc`, `authority.cljc`, `authority/*`, `holder/{driver,writer,reader,inbox,evidence}.cljc`, `compose.cljc`: not old transport; they change only at the call sites listed above.\n\n**Owner rulings honoured:** A is the only code identity (rows carry `{A vector}` and `:yin.k/trees`; the inspector names code by A only); the AST is never lost (`:source-index` is a lift input in every slice; the Stage E composition must supply it); cursor-cell sharing survives transport (the census, unchanged); BLAKE3 (row ids, pack address, intent digest, recovery address all under Jing's default); no backward compatibility (no version dispatch in the driver, fixtures regenerated, files renamed); native lanes excluded from Stage E.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Design questions phase 7 forces, with recommendation, cost, and who decides\n\n### 3.1 Exclusive and holder custody on rows: which rows carry lease, fence and holder state\n\n**Finding.** The root row's slot 16 `custody` is plain data (draft §5.5 table). UCF 7.2.1 is operative for version 3: \"no body carries an epoch; the epoch is learned only from the grant binding\". The holder state the driver needs at run time (`:yin.k/custody` on the machine: occurrence, lease, holder, epoch, arbitration, next-op-seq, `:protection`, `:input {:next :prefix}`, `:tenure`) is built by the lower from the **grant evidence**, never from the body (`accept-grant!` today). The fence (op-ids) already has slots: `:put [stream V op-id]`, `:ffi-request [... op-id]`, `:link-request [... envelope op-id]`.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides).**\n- The root `custody` slot carries exactly the draft's table for `:blocked`/`:parked` exclusive roots: `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence O :yin.k/arbitration {...} :yin.k/next-op-seq n}` plus `:yin.k/origin` on a successor; a halted exclusive root carries `{:yin.k/origin ...}` only; children nil (as now). Lease, epoch, holder, enrolled set, protection classes: **never** in a row.\n- `lift-task` gains a `:header` option (the same header `holder.export/prepare` takes today). With a header it writes the slot by kind, refuses `:op-seq-exhausted` at 2^52-1 on a blocked or parked root, `:unprotected-pending` for a retained `:put`/`:ffi-request`/`:link-request` on an enrolled target without an op-id (root or child), and `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream for an op-id on an unenrolled target; a halted root with a header but no origin is refused (not downgraded). These are the version-1 rules, moved (`handoff.cljc:623-657`, `:1513-1523`).\n- `lower-task` replaces `check-custody!` (nil only) by the version-1 custody stage: fork root under `:exclusive` → `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; exclusive root of kind `:blocked`/`:parked` requires `:grant` + `:protection` opts and runs the seven grant checks of `accept-grant!` (`handoff.cljc:3343-3420`, ported verbatim, keyed off the slot instead of body keys); every marker's protection class must match enrollment in both directions; op-id presence must equal enrollment per retained write; the restored machine gets `:yin.k/custody` from the evidence and `:yin.k/next-op-seq` from the slot **exactly** (no max); a halted exclusive root lowers with no grant, gated `:ended`. A `:fenced` option (recovery, §3.2) skips the grant and gates `:exporting`.\n- The custody stage keeps its place: all four stages over the whole tree before the first `attach!` (continuation §7.2, codex F1/F2/F4/F7).\n\n**Fork spelling (OWNER decides, §3.10 Q1).** Phase 6 writes `custody nil` for a fork root; the draft's table spells `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}` on blocked/parked fork roots and nil on halted ones. Both decide the policy from the root alone. Nil is landed and every pinned root id in `rows_test` and the harness depends on it; the explicit spelling costs about half a day of pin regeneration and makes the slot's reader one `case` on `:yin.k/policy`. I recommend **keeping nil** and amending the draft's table (fork = nil in every row), on the minimal-diff principle; the owner may prefer the draft's spelling.\n\n**Cost.** 1.5 days in 7a (lift option, three refusals, the ported grant checks, the protection stage, tests on three hosts).\n\n### 3.2 The export-recovery object re-based on rows\n\n**Finding.** Version 1 (`holder.export/freeze`) stores two bodies: the published body and a \"snapshot\" lifted with `::recovery true` (full census), because version-0 bodies omitted transitive module stores and the recovery must rehydrate everything. It also stores `:yin.k/header`, `:yin.k/descriptors` (the served set) and `:yin.k/state` (`gate`, `origins`, `issues` per wait, `issued`, children recursively). `rehydrate-fenced` restores the snapshot with `rehydrate-fenced-body` (gate `:exporting`, waits in `:yin.k/recovery-waits`, parked table emptied into the record, `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids` mapping resource ids back to carrier cursor cells), re-prepares from the retained served table and proves the published bytes are reproduced.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides).** Version 2:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/export-recovery true\n :yin.k/version         2\n :yin.k/pack-address    a        ; the pack's own address (§3.3)\n :yin.k/root            B        ; the root id inside it\n :yin.k/pack-bytes      base64\n :yin.k/header          h        ; nil for a fork\n :yin.k/descriptors     #{...}   ; the served set, as today\n :yin.k/state           {:gate g :origins n :issued n :issues [..] :children {m state}}}\n```\n\nOne body: the pack already carries the whole root store, every parked record and the module stores by reachability (continuation §2.5), so the \"snapshot extends body\" check and the fixed-point repair disappear. `rehydrate-fenced` = decode, verify `pack-address` and `root`, `lower-task` with `{:fenced true :header h}` (no grant, gate `:exporting`, waits moved to `:yin.k/recovery-waits`, parked records moved into the record, `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids` from the census), then `prepare` from the retained served table and `encode` must reproduce `pack-bytes` exactly. Engine-local state that is not canonical content (`issues`, `issued`, `origins`, `gate`) stays in `:yin.k/state` as today; `:yin.k/issue` on lowered `:put` waits is assigned sequentially in carried order (what `resume-task*` does, `handoff.cljc:3643-3650`) and overwritten from `:issues` on rehydration. The recovery address stays the BLAKE3 of the recovery bytes.\n\n**Cost.** 1.5 days in 7b (export.cljc rewrite, `:fenced` lower mode, export_test re-based).\n\n### 3.3 Checkpoint and ledger embedding of a row body root\n\n**Finding.** The authority stores the body bytes under `:yin.k/id` with `(:put-bytes-fn store)`, and both `dao.jing.mem` and `dao.jing.file` **refuse a put whose bytes do not hash to the address** (`mem.cljc:38`, `file.cljc:351`). The pack `{:yin.k/handoff true :yin.k/version 3 :yin.k/root B :yin.k/rows [...]}` does not hash to `B`. Further, `emit` builds `:yin.k/rows` as `(vec (vals rows))` of a hash map, so the pack's byte order is host-dependent today; `B` is not.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides; the draft already allows it, §10 answer 2: \"when a pack is addressed it gets its own address, distinct from the semantic root id\").**\n- `emit` sorts `:yin.k/rows` by row id (bytewise). One root → one pack on every host. Root ids do not change, so no pin moves.\n- Two identities, each used where it belongs: the **pack address** `a = BLAKE3(pack bytes)` is the storage key and the ledger's `:yin.k/id` (a snapshot variant is an encoding, as today); the **root id** `B` is the semantic identity and enters the baseline as `:yin.k/root`. Two packs with one root are variants of equal baseline (impossible after sorting, but harmless). The offer request, the fence record and the report's `:yin.k/result` carry `a`; `offer-decision`'s `(jing/segment-address? (:yin.k/id fact))` holds for `a`.\n- The **rows inspector** (`yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint/inspect address bytes`): address matches bytes (`jing/segment-bytes-match?`), bytes decode through `rows/decode` (every row hash, canonical numbering, frames), the root's custody slot by kind (the version-1 header rules of `checkpoint.cljc:183-232` on the slot's map; children nil), every op-id on `:put`/`:ffi-request`/`:link-request` pendings in root and children against the root's occurrence, origin and counter (`check-op-id!` verbatim), duplicate ids refused, and the baseline `{:yin.k/kind :yin.k/root :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/origin :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq :yin.k/ops {op-id digest}}` where the intent digest is `BLAKE3(cbor [:yin.k/append target-identity payload-slot])` with the payload **as the wire slot holds it** (`[:lit x]` or `[:row id]`, both canonical). A fork pack answers `:yin.k/fork true` as `inspect-v2` does. Every refusal is data; nothing throws (as today).\n- `admission/accepted-baseline` and `completion/successor-defect` read `:yin.k/next-op-seq` and `:yin.k/origin` from the baseline, unchanged. `ledger/offer-defect` is unchanged (`:yin.k/kind` ∈ `#{:blocked :parked}`, the baseline compared whole).\n- Halted exit: the report's `:yin.k/result` is the halted pack's address; the terminal edge names it.\n\n**Cost.** 1.5 days in 7a (inspector, sorted emit, fixtures regenerated, the three authority call sites, `ledger-v2.txt`).\n\n### 3.4 Crash cuts: which cuts are meaningful when the body is a Merkle row set\n\n**Finding.** The named cuts (`stage_e_world/cuts`) cut the progress journal around mint, serve, store intent/ack, two content-store puts (recovery first, body second), fence, offer; the authority's commit; the reply journal; and composition ticks. They are meaningful at the granularity of **one store put per object**.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides).** Store the pack as **one** content-store object. Then every cut keeps its meaning: `:export/store-put-*` cuts the recovery v2 put, `:export/body-put-*` the pack put, and a pack is either whole or absent; a torn pack is impossible by the store's atomic insert. Storing rows individually (one object per row, Merkle-addressed) would make partial checkpoints possible and require a new cut family (`:pack/missing-row-before-fence`) and a new refusal path (`rows/decode` `:missing-row` at the authority), with no benefit in phase 7. Row-level storage is a phase-8+ option for deduplication across checkpoints and is out of scope. One new cut is worth adding cheaply: `:export/pack-put-after-recovery-missing` is already covered by `store-put-before` (recovery absent, nothing fenced); no addition needed.\n\n**Cost.** None beyond keeping the two-put order in `export-fence-step`.\n\n### 3.5 Publication of successors\n\n**Finding.** The protocol (`exit-mint` → prepare → freeze → fence → report → release → closure; the successor offer refused `:awaiting-completion` then admitted) is body-agnostic except for three values: the successor header (built from the machine's `:yin.k/custody`), the report's result address and the stored bytes.\n\n**Recommendation (decided).** No protocol change. The successor's header takes `:yin.k/next-op-seq` from the machine's custody map as today; the lift writes it into the slot; the inspector's `:counter-regression` check compares slot against baseline. The report carries the successor's pack address; `reopen-exit` rebuilds from the journaled mint and the fence as today. The one wrinkle: `reopen-holder` and `rebuilt-cell` read the kind and occurrence from the stored bytes through the inspector; the rows inspector answers the same keys.\n\n**Cost.** Included in 7b.\n\n### 3.6 Authority and capability tokens versus sealed refs on rows\n\n**Finding.** Rows re-seal every stream and cursor-ref under the receiver's owner on lower (`engine/issue-ref`), authenticate refs on lift, and carry no credential (UCF 7.7.8: \"the envelope carries no credential; the holder is authenticated by the attribution the composition supplies\"). The owner ruled (2026-10-12) that ShiBi tokens are a configurable policy at the `:admit` seam, and (2026-09-27) that ShiBi is a tuple space emerging from index and query interpreters, with `dao.stream.remote` offering only a capability-agnostic seam.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides; owner to confirm scope).** Phase 7 carries **no** capability token in any row and adds no row kind for one. Authority is ledger facts, attribution is the composition's resolver, tenure is lease evidence; sealed refs are the receiver's and are minted on lower. A token-bearing admission is ShiBi work at the admission seam, after phase 8.\n\n**Cost.** None.\n\n### 3.7 `:resume-mode` under version 2\n\n**Finding.** The owner's ruling (orchestrator log, 2026-10-11) is that the version-2 stack lane stays frozen and does not carry `:resume-mode`. Rows carry `:act` and `:tail` rows; a `:tail` is the register kernel's `:return-result` delivery and an `:act` the `:write-result` one (continuation §4.2, §4.3), so the information `:resume-mode` encodes is structural in version 3.\n\n**Recommendation (decided).** No work. Phase 7 does not touch version 2; version 3 needs no such key.\n\n### 3.8 Two gaps the brief did not list but phase 7 meets\n\n- **Continuation §11 Q4 (op-id rules on lower).** Closed by the rows inspector running inside `lower-task`'s custody stage for exclusive roots (below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent without origin, never the body's own occurrence). Fork roots refuse any op-id (`:fork-op-id`), as `inspect-v2` does. Decided, in 7a.\n- **Continuation §11 Q3 (install verification).** `check-install!` verifies status, manifest name, exports and that the image loads, not link-id = parent nor image address = the manifest's. Adding both is about a quarter day in 7b. **OWNER decides** (§3.10 Q3); recommend yes, because the recovery object now trusts the pack for install children.\n- **Continuation §11 Q1/Q10 (the `:running` wire phase).** Unchanged by phase 7; Stage E's installer scenarios use phase `:parked` only. Leave open.\n\n### 3.9 Which kernels take part\n\nStage E runs the **semantic-register kernel only** (the reference lane). The de Bruijn kernels are covered by the cross-engine laws of the harness (61/350, accepted) and are not Stage E holders or sources; the holder driver never lowers through a layout. This follows the owner's matrix ruling and avoids three times the process matrix.\n\n### 3.10 The owner's questions (blocking)\n\n- **Q1, fork spelling in the custody slot.** nil (landed; recommended) or the draft's `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}` (half a day of pin regeneration; the draft is amended either way).\n- **Q2, the REPL custody gap.** `yin.repl` composes the **old** `yin.vm.semantic` kernel (`repl.cljc:144`) and wires `compose` (`repl/main.cljc:979-1011`). After phase 7 the driver reads only packs, so the REPL's custody path cannot lift its machine until phase 8 renames the semantic-register kernel into `yin.vm.semantic`. Options: (a) accept the gap until 8 (recommended: `the-plain-composition-loads-and-drives-with-no-yin-repl-test` is re-based on a semantic-register receiver, and the REPL's `:custody` branch is marked inert until 8); (b) pull the REPL's kernel switch into phase 7 (an extra slice, two to three days, touching `yin.repl` and its tests, which 4b/5 did not plan).\n- **Q3, the install checks of continuation §11 Q3** in 7b (recommended yes, a quarter day).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Slicing\n\nEach slice: files, dependencies, acceptance tests, hosts, days (opus implementation + review rounds at the observed 2-4 codex rounds per slice, about half a day per round including fixes).\n\n### 7a: custody on rows, the rows inspector, the authority's fixtures\n\n**Create.** `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows/checkpoint.cljc` (the inspector of §3.3); `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_support.cljc` (semantic-register machine builders mirroring `lift_support`: `new-machine` over `sr/create-vm` with `tu/make-stream`, deterministic one-slot streams, `parked-reader`, `parked-writer`, `parked-explicit`, `parked-installer`, `parked-linker`, `halted-machine`, `halted-with-cursors`, `parked-module-store-cursors`, `with-op-id`, `serve-table`, `header`, `source-index` capture through `linearize/project`); `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc` + `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` (the five bases lifted by `rows/lift-task` under a header, the mutations as raw row edits with rehash where they touch a row and as root-slot edits otherwise, three anchors with full pack bytes); `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_test.cljc`; `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc` (the lift and lower custody rules of §3.1: header by kind, the three lift refusals, the seven grant checks with zero attachments, protection in both directions, exact counter restore, halted exclusive lowers ended, fork under `:exclusive` refused).\n\n**Change.** `rows.cljc` (`emit` sorts rows by id; `lift-task` `:header`; `lower-task` custody stage with `:grant`/`:protection`/`:exclusive`/`:fenced` opts; `check-custody!` replaced); `authority/grant.cljc`, `authority/completion.cljc`, `authority/admission.cljc` (inspector call); `ledger.cljc` (none expected; `:yin.k/root` in the baseline is opaque to the fold); `ledger_fixtures.cljc` (`park`/`halted` from rows packs; `path` → `ledger-v2.txt`), `ledger_fixture_test`, `crash_cut_test`, `grant_test`, `completion_test`, `inherited_test`, `front_test` (fixture bodies only).\n\n**Depends on.** Nothing; owner's Q1.\n\n**Acceptance.** `rows_checkpoint_test` (every base accepted with its baseline; every mutation refused at its path; the file equals `render` on three hosts), `rows_custody_test`, `ledger_fixture_test` (every host rebuilds every frame byte for byte; the projection digest pinned), `crash_cut_test`, the eight authority tests green, `rows_test` and the safepoint harness unchanged (no root id moves), `store_write_audit_test` allowlist updated if the row table changes.\n\n**Hosts.** JVM, Node, Dart.\n\n**Days.** 2.5 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) = **4**.\n\n### 7b: the holder's export record, recovery v2, the driver's lower\n\n**Change.** `holder/export.cljc` (`prepare`/`encode` over `rows/lift-task` with `:header` and a `:serve-keyed` adaptor; `freeze` → recovery v2; `rehydrate-fenced` over `lower-task {:fenced true}`); `holder/driver.cljc` (`lower`, `step-validating`, `rebuilt-cell`, `reopen-holder`, `reopen-exit`, `source-mint`/`exit-mint` unchanged in shape; `:export-version` removed); `compose.cljc` (`holder-config-keys`/`driver-config` drop `:export-version`); `holder/export_test`, `holder/driver_test`, `holder/writer_test`, `holder/reader_test` (machines from `rows_support`; rec1 rows replaced; the v2 fork-lift deftest deleted); `handoff_v2_holder_test` (one deftest deleted).\n\n**Depends on.** 7a; owner's Q3 for the install checks (a quarter day inside this slice).\n\n**Acceptance.** `export_test` (recovery v2 freezes and rehydrates fenced with zero program IO, the published pack reproduced byte for byte, aliases and distinct cursor cells preserved, install children rehydrated without initialization or IO, a restarted abort refused), `driver_test` whole (117 deftests), `writer_test`, `reader_test`, `evidence_test`; the law \"zero attachments before the custody stage refuses\" pinned by attach counts.\n\n**Hosts.** JVM, Node, Dart.\n\n**Days.** 3 implementation + 2 review (4 rounds; the driver is 2.9k lines and its tests 4.3k) = **5**.\n\n### 7c: the composition and the compose and fencing rows\n\n**Change.** `fencing_rows_test` (rows lift under header, `lower-task` with grant opts), `compose_test` (semantic-register `:receiver`/`:machine`; the plain-composition test per Q2), `compose_rows_test` (41 deftests; `offer-variant`-style helpers re-emit packs; content-store cuts see recovery v2 then the pack); `yin.repl/main.cljc` only if Q2 answers (b).\n\n**Depends on.** 7b; owner's Q2.\n\n**Acceptance.** All three files green on three hosts; the slow deftest `completion-recovery-unknown-policy-transaction-is-reconciled-once-test` under `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1` on each lane.\n\n**Days.** 1.5 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) = **3**.\n\n### 7d: Stage E on the semantic-register kernel, three hosts, four process pairs\n\n**Change.** `stage_e_world.cljc` (`parked-reader-over`, `reading-writer` over `rows_support`; `holder-seams` supplies `:source-index`; `offer-variant!` re-emits; `snapshot`'s `:fences` reads pack digests), `stage_e_scenarios.cljc` (checks that name `:yin.k/id-counter` or body keys adjusted; row 1's \"two encodings\" assertion keeps its meaning), `stage_e_peer.cljc` (none expected), `stage_e_process_test.clj` (none expected; the Node peer build picks up the new namespaces), `stage_e_rows_test.cljc` (none expected).\n\n**Depends on.** 7c (the world port can begin on 7b and run in-process while 7c is in review).\n\n**Acceptance.** `stage_e_rows_test` on JVM, Node and Dart (slow tier, `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`); `stage_e_process_test` on the JVM over `[[:jvm :jvm] [:jvm :node] [:node :jvm] [:node :node]]`, every life its own process, every cut a SIGKILL, `target/stage-e-durability.edn` appended; the fast-lane gate `the-cross-host-handoff-across-processes-test` first. Row 10's canonical-byte agreement across hosts now holds for packs as well (sorted rows).\n\n**Hosts.** JVM, Node, Dart (in-process); JVM↔Node (processes).\n\n**Days.** 2 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) + 0.5 for the slow matrix runs (the process matrix is about an hour per full pass on the remote hosts, per build-n-test) = **4**.\n\n### 7e: documentation\n\n**Change.** `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §2.5, §2.6 (custody slot by kind), §7.1-7.2 (the `:header` lift, the custody and fenced lower stages), §8.4 (phase 7 met), §10 (a 10.7 inventory), §11 (Q3, Q4 closed; Q1/Q10 left); `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` §5.5 (the custody table as ruled), §7 (pack address versus root id, sorted rows), §9 (the fenced lower); `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` 7.7.4 (recovery v2 paragraph replacing the version-1 one), 7.11.1 clauses 1-5 and 10 (stage letters updated); `docs/orchestrator-log.md` entry.\n\n**Depends on.** The owner's answers; concurrent with 7c/7d.\n\n**Days.** 1 + 1 review round = **1.5**.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Risks and what could force a redesign\n\n1. **The identity split (pack address versus root id).** If the owner or a reviewer insists the ledger's `:yin.k/id` be the root id, the content store contract must change (a verifier-taking put) or the authority must store under the pack address and record both; the second is what §3.3 recommends anyway, so the redesign cost is in naming, not mechanics. Sorting rows removes the host-order variance that would otherwise make JVM and Node packs distinct variants.\n2. **Fenced rehydration on the semantic-register kernel.** `rehydrate-fenced-body` relies on the version-0/1/2 lower's `:fenced?` arm (`handoff.cljc:3688-3699`, `:3722-3725`): gate `:exporting`, waits parked in `:yin.k/recovery-waits`, `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids`, the FFI pair resources for `:ffi-request` pendings, `id-counter` carried exactly. `lower-task` has none of this; porting it is routine but the install-child case (children exporting, their parked records moved to the child's record) is exercised only by `export_test`. Medium.\n3. **Install children on the semantic-register kernel under the holder.** The harness proves rows 7-12 (4b stage 2) for lift/lower; the holder's reader (held observations, replay through child call ids) over semantic-register install children has no test today. If `reader.cljc`'s walk assumes a field the new kernel spells differently, 7b finds it. Low-medium: both kernels share `engine.cljc` for `:installs`, `:wait-set`, `:yin.k/held`.\n4. **The REPL gap (Q2).** If the owner wants the REPL's custody demo live between 7 and 8, phase 7 grows by a slice and touches the 4b/5 \"no REPL edit\" boundary.\n5. **Stage E's pinned row-5 behaviour** (\"the exposed gap marker is the first and only commit\", pending an Architect ruling) is inherited unchanged; a ruling during phase 7 would change `row5`'s check, not the transport.\n6. **Dart.** Stage E runs in-process only on Dart (as today); the numbering budget (`default-budget`) is far above Stage E's task sizes. The known Dart traps (reader whitespace, `for` over chunked seqs, multi-key `assoc` on nil, duplicate protocol params) apply to the new support namespace; the harness already avoids them.\n7. **Review load.** Phase 6b took three codex rounds on a 1.8k-line file; 7b touches the 2.9k-line driver through eight call sites plus `export.cljc` whole. Budgeted at four rounds.\n8. **What would force a redesign of rows:** a requirement that the body's identity be a byte address (contradicts Merkle rows; §3.3 resolves it with two identities); a requirement to carry lease or epoch in the body (contradicts UCF 7.2.1, not proposed); row-level storage with partial checkpoints (deferred, §3.4). None is live.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Order of work and concurrency\n\n```\n7a (custody, inspector, fixtures)        ──────────┐\n                                                   ▼\n7b (export record, recovery v2, driver)  ──────────┐\n                                                   ▼\n7c (compose, compose/fencing rows)       ──────┐   ├── 7d world port may start here\n                                               ▼   ▼\n7d (Stage E, in-process three hosts, then the process matrix)\n7e (docs) ─ starts after the owner's answers, lands beside 7c/7d\n```\n\n- 7a is the only slice that needs an owner answer before it starts (Q1). Q2 gates one deftest in 7c and possibly an extra slice; Q3 gates a quarter day in 7b.\n- Concurrent work: 7e with 7c/7d; the 7d world port (`stage_e_world` over `rows_support`) with 7c's review, since it depends on 7b's `export`/`driver` only. The old-codec tests are never touched, so no conflict with other worktrees; `rows.cljc` is the one file 7a and the harness share, and 7a keeps every pinned root id.\n- Landing cadence (owner's tiers): quick tier at every checkpoint; the slow tier (Stage E, the completion-recovery slow deftest, the process matrix) before each merge of 7c and 7d and once at the phase boundary; one lane set per machine, Dart in its own clone on the remote host.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Honest total\n\n| Slice | Implementation (opus) | Review rounds | Days |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 7a | 2.5 | 3 | 4 |\n| 7b | 3 | 4 | 5 |\n| 7c | 1.5 | 3 | 3 |\n| 7d | 2 | 3 (+0.5 matrix runs) | 4 |\n| 7e | 1 | 1 | 1.5 |\n| **Total** | **10** | | **17.5 sequential** |\n\nWith 7e concurrent and the 7d port overlapping 7c, the critical path is 7a → 7b → 7c → 7d: about **14 days** of work; **12 days median** if reviews average the lower end (two rounds on 7a/7c), **17 at the worst** (four rounds everywhere, one redesign loop on the identity split). Phase 6's three slices went from first commit to merge in three calendar days with the design already written and reviews back-to-back; phase 7 has four code slices over twice the surface, so calendar time of one to two weeks is realistic if codex capacity holds.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict\n\n**NOT READY.** Blocking questions for the owner: **Q1** fork spelling in the custody slot (nil, recommended, or the draft's explicit map); **Q2** the REPL custody gap until phase 8 (accept, recommended, or pull the REPL kernel switch into phase 7); **Q3** the install link-id and image-address checks in 7b (recommended yes). Every other design point above is decided by the Architect within the owner's rulings and is ready for an implementation brief for 7a on receipt of Q1.\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-13 +07\n# Slice 7a report: custody on rows, the rows inspector, the authority's fixtures (opus, engineer)\n\nWorktree `srvm-phase7a`, from `23a8caef`. Nothing committed. JVM only, all runs in the foreground (one quick-tier run of every `yin.vm.ucf.*` namespace went past the 10-minute foreground limit, finished in the background and was read from its log).\n\n## Verdict\n\n7a is done as the plan's slice section and §3.1, §3.3 and §3.4 describe it, and its own acceptance holds: the two new suites, the ledger fixture, crash cuts, the eight authority tests and the holder tests that use fixtures all pass. `rows_test` and the safepoint harness pass with **no root id moved**.\n\n**7a cannot land alone (design defect D1 below).** The plan's switch of the authority to the rows inspector makes the authority refuse every version-1/2 body. 141 deftests in six old-codec D/E suites now fail. Those suites are re-based in 7b/7c, and I proved the switch is the only cause. I did not work around it, because a fix would change semantics; the decision belongs to the orchestrator.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Lint: `clojure -M:kondo` needed approval in this session, so I used `clj -M:kondo --lint <files>`. Final run over all 22 changed `.clj[c]` files: 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n- Quick tier, new and touched namespaces plus `rows_test`, the harness, `layout_test`, the old `checkpoint_test` and `lift_v1_test` (20 namespaces): **360 tests, 7188 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors** (`target/7a-final.log`).\n  - `rows_checkpoint_test`: 17 deftests.\n  - `rows_custody_test`: 11 deftests, 107 assertions before the M3 fix added two more.\n- Slow tier (`-i :slow`) on the same namespaces plus `rows_test` and the harness: 4 tests, 2724 assertions, 0 failures.\n- Every `yin.vm.ucf.*` namespace, quick tier (`-r 'yin\\.vm\\.ucf\\..*'`, `target/7a-ucf-quick.log`): 694 tests, 648 failures and 89 errors. They are all in six suites (see D1):\n\n  | Namespace | Failing deftests | Failing assertions |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | `holder.driver-test` | 92 | 294 |\n  | `compose-rows-test` | 35 | 335 |\n  | `compose-test` | 6 | 35 |\n  | `stage-e-rows-test` | 4 (the quick ones) | 35 |\n  | `fencing-rows-test` | 3 | 30 |\n  | `handoff-v2-authority-test` | 1 | 8 |\n\n  No other `yin.vm.ucf` namespace fails.\n- Counterfactual check: I pointed the three authority requires back at `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint` and ran `fencing-rows-test`, `handoff-v2-authority-test` and `compose-test`: 21 tests, 332 assertions, 0 failures. I then restored the files (byte-compare verified).\n- Node and Dart were not run, as instructed. The new code follows the portability rules: no `for`, no private var access, catches with `:cljd` first, start-from-`{}` assoc, no EDN strings. The `version-float` fixture relies on Node keeping a `Float64` carrier and on Dart's `integer?` rejecting a double, both checked in the source (`rows/decode` gates on `code/reg?`), but this is unverified on those hosts.\n\n## Files\n\nCreated:\n\n| File | Lines | What |\n|---|---|---|\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows/checkpoint.cljc` | 102 | The rows inspector: `inspect address bytes`, `inspect-pack wire`. Address check, canonical CBOR, `rows/decode`, a `:task` root, then `rows/custody-baseline`; adds `:yin.k/root` |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_support.cljc` | 238 | Semantic-register machine builders mirroring `lift_support` (it reuses that namespace's AST helpers, one-slot streams, `serve-table`, header parts and `with-op-id`): `new-machine`, `project` with `source-index`, `parked-reader`, `parked-writer`, `parked-explicit`, `parked-linker`, `parked-installer`, `halted-machine`, `halted-with-cursors`, `parked-module-store-cursors`, `lift`, `attacher` (counting), `receiver` |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc` | 452 | Five bases lifted under a header; 50 mutations (task edits re-emitted, raw pack edits where the defect is the carrier's own); `render`/`read-file`/`fixture`; editing helpers the authority tests use (`base`, `pack-of`, `edit-task`, `edit-custody`, `waits`, `update-put`, `set-op-id`, `update-child`, `enc`) |\n| `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` | 917 | 55 pins, 3 anchors with full bytes |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_test.cljc` | 203 | 17 deftests |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc` | 275 | 11 deftests |\n| `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/ledger-v2.txt` | 22 | Replaces `ledger-v1.txt` (deleted with `git rm`, so the deletion is staged) |\n\nChanged:\n\n- `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` (411 lines changed per `git diff --stat`, now 2219 lines):\n  - `pack`: rows sorted by id, used by `emit`, `emit-numbered` and `frame->rows`.\n  - The custody section: `max-exact`, `write-slots`, `custody-baseline`, `pack-address`.\n  - `lift-task :header`: `header-custody` and `protect!`, `:op-seq-exhausted`, and the grammar run before emitting.\n  - `check-custody!` rewritten as the custody stage: grammar, then fork under `:exclusive`, then `accept-grant!` ported verbatim. It runs once after the three per-tree stages and before `attach-streams!`.\n  - `with-custody` in restore: issue numbering, gate, custody state, and the fenced gate.\n- `authority/{grant,completion,admission}.cljc`: the require switched to `yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint` under the same alias, so each call site is unchanged; docstrings updated.\n- Fixture bodies only:\n  - Named in the plan: `ledger_fixtures`, `ledger_fixture_test` (docstring), `grant_test`, `completion_test`, `inherited_test`, `front_test`. `crash_cut_test` needed no edit; it follows `lf/park`.\n  - Also needed (deviation 1): `admission_test`, `input_test`, `reclaim_test`, `holder/evidence_test`, `holder/reader_test`, `holder/writer_test`.\n- `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc`: one expected value (deviation 2).\n\n## What the code does (for review)\n\n- **Custody slot (root slot 16):**\n  - nil for a fork of any kind (owner Q1).\n  - `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq}` plus `:yin.k/origin` for blocked or parked exclusive roots.\n  - `{:yin.k/origin}` alone for a halted exclusive root.\n  - nil on every child.\n- **`custody-baseline`** is the version-1 header and op-id grammar of `checkpoint.cljc`, keyed off the slot. On top of that it:\n  - refuses any key outside the five custody keys (`:custody-key`), so lease, epoch and holder cannot enter a body;\n  - refuses a slot that is not a map (`:malformed-custody`);\n  - checks each task's kind and role, that each `:install` pending names an install row, and that every child slot is nil;\n  - refuses any op-id in a fork tree (`:fork-op-id`, closing continuation §11 Q4 for 7a).\n\n  Paths are relative to the root task: `[:custody k]`, `[:frames i :op-id …]`, and `[:installs <name> …]` inside children. The intent digest is BLAKE3 of the canonical `[:yin.k/append target-identity payload-slot]`, where `[:row n]` becomes `[:row id]`.\n- **The lift** runs the grammar over the abstract task before emitting, the same contract as the version-1 lift running its inspector over its own bytes. It does not decode its own output, which keeps continuation §7.1 step 9 true. A fork lift of a machine that carries op-ids therefore refuses `:fork-op-id`; before 7a the op-ids travelled verbatim.\n- **The lower's custody stage**, in order: grammar; then a fork under `:exclusive` is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; a halted exclusive root needs no grant and is gated `:ended`; `:fenced` skips the grant and gates `:exporting`, with waits moved to `:yin.k/recovery-waits`; otherwise `accept-grant!` runs. The grant's `:checkpoint` must equal `rows/pack-address` of the wire, not the root id; a test pins that the root id is refused as `:not-holder`.\n- **Restore:**\n  - `:yin.k/custody` comes from the evidence, with `:yin.k/next-op-seq` equal to the slot's value exactly.\n  - In an exclusive tree every task numbers its `:put` waits (`:yin.k/issue`, `:yin.k/issued`) and is gated `:running`, as `resume-task*` does.\n- **The two identities:**\n  - The pack address is the ledger's `:yin.k/id`, the content-store key, the grant's checkpoint and the report's result.\n  - The root id is the baseline's `:yin.k/root`.\n  - Tested in `the-pack-address-is-storage-and-the-root-id-is-semantic`: address = BLAKE3 of the bytes; root ≠ address; root = the root row's own hash; rows sorted; re-emitting a decoded pack gives the same bytes. Also tested in `a-pack-variant-of-one-root-shares-its-baseline`.\n\n## Mutation checks\n\nDriver: `target/7a-mut/{mut,run}.py`. Each mutant was applied to a backup-guarded copy, run against `rows-custody-test`, `rows-checkpoint-test`, `authority.grant-test` and `ledger-fixture-test`, then restored. Each restore was byte-compared with a clean copy; all were clean.\n\n| # | Mutant | Caught? | By |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| M1 | Custody header by kind swapped (`header-custody` branches) | yes | `the-lift-writes-the-custody-slot-by-kind`, every base (they no longer lift), 46 deftests |\n| M2 | A grant check skipped (holder vs binding) | yes | `every-grant-check-refuses-before-any-attachment` (\"another holder\") |\n| M3 | Protection, one direction dropped (an enrolled stream classed otherwise accepted) | **survived at first**; yes after the fix | The op-id presence check that follows masked it on the successor. I added the isolating case (a reader pack with no retained write) to `protection-is-checked-in-both-directions` |\n| M4 | Counter restore off by one | yes | `the-counter-is-restored-exactly`, `an-exclusive-root-lowers-under-its-grant`, `install-children-lower-…` |\n| M5 | Fork under `:exclusive` accepted | yes | `a-fork-is-refused-under-exclusive` |\n| M6 | Custody stage after attach | yes | The zero-attach assertions in 4 deftests (30 assertions) |\n| M7 | Unsorted `emit` | yes | `the-file-holds-exactly-the-declared-bytes` (anchors), `the-pack-address-…`, the ledger frames, 14 deftests |\n| M8 | Inspector accepting a mutated row (decode's row-hash check off) | yes | `each-mutation-is-refused-as-it-names` (`row-tampered`) |\n| M9 | Baseline root ignored | yes | 11 deftests, including `a-conflicting-variant-is-refused` (`variant-other-root`) and the ledger projection digest |\n| M10 (extra) | The lift skips the grammar | yes | `the-lift-refuses-what-custody-forbids` |\n\n## Deviations\n\n1. **Six more test files rebased.** `authority/{admission,input,reclaim}_test` and `holder/{evidence,reader,writer}_test` offer fixture bodies through `grant/offer!` and break without the switch. Each change is a three-to-five-line `body` helper.\n2. **One harness assertion changed.** `carrier-steps-run-in-order-over-the-tree` expected the phase-6 refusal `[:yin.k/unsupported :custody]` for slot `{:custody :exclusive}`. The plan replaces `check-custody!`, so it now expects `[:yin.k/undecodable :custody-key]`. Nothing else in the harness changed, and no root id moved.\n3. **`:fenced` is minimal.** It skips the grant and protection, gates `:exporting` on every task and moves the waits to `:yin.k/recovery-waits`. `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids`, the exact id counter, the recovery header check and moving parked records into the record are left to 7b (plan §3.2 costs the fenced mode there).\n4. **Refusal kinds and paths are mine:**\n   - New kinds: `:custody-key`, `:malformed-custody`, `:policy` (the old inspector gave a policy mismatch no kind), `:kind`, `:role`, `:child-header`, `:fork-op-id`, `:incomplete-install`.\n   - Paths: `[:custody …]`, `[:frames i …]`, `[:installs <name> …]`.\n   - Carrier-level refusals keep `rows/decode`'s own: a missing tag is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch :version` on rows, not `:undecodable :body` as on version 1.\n5. **No op-id-on-variant fixture.** It is structurally impossible on rows: the pending table gives `:next` no op-id slot. I added fixtures the old set lacked: `frame-root`, `row-tampered`, `root-role`, `custody-epoch`, `custody-lease`, `custody-not-map`, `fork-op-id`, `variant-other-root`.\n6. **The grammar lives in `rows.cljc`** (`custody-baseline`). `rows.checkpoint` is a thin wrapper around it, because `lower-task` must run the grammar too and `rows.checkpoint` requires `rows` (a cycle otherwise). Behaviour is what plan §3.3 assigns to the inspector.\n7. **Fixture shape.** `rows_checkpoint_fixtures` takes the `refusal` map a mutation must contain, instead of the old `expected` and `place` pair.\n8. **Accessor alias kept.** The authority still calls the inspector through the alias `checkpoint`.\n\n## Design defects found (separate list)\n\n- **D1. 7a cannot land alone.** The authority switch (plan §2, §4 7a \"Change\") refuses version-1/2 bodies at `offer!`, `report!` and `accepted-baseline`. The holder, compose and Stage E paths still offer such bodies until 7b/7c, so 141 deftests in six suites fail (table above; proven by the counterfactual). The plan's 7a acceptance never mentions them.\n  - Options for the orchestrator:\n    1. Merge 7a only together with 7b and 7c.\n    2. Move the three-line authority switch out of 7a into 7c.\n    3. A temporary version dispatch in the authority. This contradicts \"no version dispatch\" and is not recommended.\n  - `handoff_v2_authority_test` (an old-codec test the plan keeps until phase 8) also breaks permanently under any option. It needs deleting or re-basing, which the plan does not list.\n- **D2. Plan §1.1 row-1 contradicts §3.3.** Row-1 wants a re-emitted id-counter variant with \"a new root id and new pack; equal baseline\". With `:yin.k/root` in the baseline, which this brief requires, such a variant's baseline differs, and the ledger refuses it as `:variant-conflict` (pinned by `grant_test` `variant-other-root`). Under sorted `emit`, the only equal-baseline variant is the same root in another row order. I used that for `variant-equal`, `completion_test` and `inherited_test`.\n  - Stage E row-1 (7d) needs a ruling: either the variant is a conflict, or the root leaves the baseline.\n  - Related: `rows/decode` accepts rows in any order, so \"one root, one pack\" holds for what `emit` makes, not for what a reader accepts. Should decode refuse unsorted packs? I left decode unchanged.\n- **D3. Draft amendments to list (no doc edited):**\n  - transport-tuples §5.5 table: fork is nil in every row (owner Q1), and the slot admits only the five custody keys.\n  - continuation §2.5, §2.6, §7.1 step 8 and §7.2's stage table: custody is not phase-6 nil-only any more; the step-10 refusal `:yin.k/unsupported :custody` is replaced by the grammar's kinds and the grant refusals; the stage runs once for the tree after the three per-tree stages.\n  - continuation §11 Q4: closed for the op-id rules (correlation agreement is still not implemented).\n  - UCF 7.11.1: the inspector citation moves from `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint` to `yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint`.\n- **D4. The lift serves streams before custody refusals.** v1 refused `:op-seq-exhausted` before lifting; rows refuses it after the walk has called `serve!`. Harmless to the lower's zero-attach law, but a holder that counts serves will see them (7b's export record).\n\n## What 7b needs from me\n\n- `rows/lift-task` with `{:header h :source-index …}` is `holder.export/prepare` and `encode`'s new path; `h` is the same header shape (`:yin.k/enrolled` stays local).\n- `rows/pack-address wire` is the storage key to store and fence. The grant's `:checkpoint` must be that address.\n- `rows/lower-task recv wire attach! {:grant g :protection p}` is the driver's `lower`; `{:exclusive true}` refuses a fork; `{:fenced true}` is the rehydration entry. Finishing fenced is 7b's: recovery-cell-ids, exact id counter, parked records into the record, the header check against `:yin.k/state`.\n- `rows.checkpoint/inspect` answers the old inspector's keys plus `:yin.k/root`; `reopen-holder` and `rebuilt-cell` can read `:yin.k/kind`, `:yin.k/occurrence` and `:yin.k/next-op-seq` from it.\n- `rows_support` builders for the driver, writer and reader tests, and `rows_checkpoint_fixtures`' editing helpers for packs.\n- D1 decides whether 7b lands together with 7a.\ncat: collab/7a-review.md: No such file or directory\ncat: collab/7b-report.md: No such file or directory\ncat: collab/7c-report.md: No such file or directory\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-14 00:40 +07\n# Review findings: slice 7c, composition and fencing suites over row packs\n\nReviewer: claude-fable-5-1 (non-author; codex wrote 7c). Worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7c`, branch `srvm-phase7c`, commit `05d3a25f` over `srvm-phase7` (7a, 7b merged). Method: full read of `git diff srvm-phase7..HEAD` (six test files, no production change, confirmed by `--name-only`), the brief, codex's report and mutant artifacts (`target/7c-mutants/`), `7a-report.md` (D1, D2), `7b-review.md` (F1-F3), the plan (§1.1, §3.3-3.6, slice 7c); the production seams the suites exercise (`compose.cljc` check!/holder-config-keys/driver-config, `driver.cljc` export-prepare/fence/offer/lower/step-validating, `export.cljc` prepare/encode/freeze, `rows.cljc` check-custody!/accept-grant!/with-custody/lower-task/check-install!, `rows/checkpoint.cljc`, `authority/completion.cljc`); the quick and slow lanes; 16 mutants of my own with byte-level restore. JVM only (Node/Dart by reading). Nothing in the repo edited; artifacts under `target/7c-review/` (ignored).\n\n## Verification I ran\n\n| Lane | Command | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Quick, every UCF ns | `clojure -M:test -e :slow -r 'yin\\.vm\\.ucf\\..*'` (foreground, logged) | 700 tests, 9340 assertions, 0 failures, **5 errors**, all in `yin.vm.ucf.stage-e-rows-test` (`row-1-candidates-and-encodings`, `row-7-failure-lower-and-lost-authority`, `row-3-reclaim`, `fork-per-host`, `row-5-replay-divergence`), each `The custody composition needs a source index` thrown at `compose.cljc:104` from `open!`: `stage_e_world/composition-config` supplies no `:source-index`. No other namespace fails. Matches codex exactly. |\n| Slow, the six suites | `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n` the six namespaces | 2 tests, 435 assertions, 0 failures: `completion-recovery-unknown-policy-transaction-is-reconciled-once-test` and the driver's slow journal-cut case. Note: `dao.test-slow/enabled?` is `true` on the JVM unconditionally (`test_slow.cljc:17`), so `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1` is a Node/Dart switch only; the JVM result is identical with or without it. |\n| Lint | `clj -M:kondo --lint` the six files | 0 errors, 0 warnings |\n| Restore | `git diff --quiet -- src`, `git status --short -- src test` after the mutant pass | clean |\n\n## Item by item\n\n1. **F1, F2, F3 are real.** F1: `driver_test.cljc:3196-3201` maps the journal's `:yin.k/store` intents in order to `[(:yin.k/record fence) (:yin.k/address fence)]`; my R01 (the two `store-object` calls swapped) fails it and, independently, the content-store cuts test (8 failures). F2: `rows_custody_test.cljc:294-296` changes only the record's profile, keeping the honest input and output; my R10 (the record-address conjunct replaced by `contains?`) fails exactly that deftest (2 failures), so the conjunct is now isolated. F3: `compose_test.cljc:619` is `:source-index (fn [] {})`; the refusal message `\":source-index is a composition seam\"` matches `check-holder-config-keys!`.\n2. **The re-based suites test what they claim.**\n   - `compose_rows_test` content-store cuts (`:2514`): the cut fires on a freeze put (`some? cut-address` asserted); the expected addresses come from a deterministic re-`freeze` of the stalled export cell, so position 1 = recovery v2 address and position 2 = pack address is asserted against production, not against itself; `:after` cuts decode the stored object (version 2 naming the pack; the pack whose `rows/pack-address` is the store key and whose root the inspector answers); intents = position, acks = position − 1; no fence, no offer; the rebuilt composition stalls `:incomplete-preparation`, never re-mints, never fences, zero attaches. Cut names unchanged (positions 1/2 × before/after here; the named `store-put-*`/`body-put-*` cuts live in 7d's world).\n   - Pack address as store key, root inside the baseline: asserted at `:2540-2543` and throughout (`:address` of the fence is what the authority admits as a variant, `:yin.k/root` only inside `inspect`'s answer).\n   - Two admitted encodings: `variant-of` (`:429`) reverses `:yin.k/rows`; different pack address, same root, same baseline, admitted as a second variant and lowered by the winner (`row-1-…-two-encodings`). A counter change (`f/edit-task … [:slots 4] 9`, slot 4 is the task's id counter, `rows.cljc:1682`) is a different root and a conflicting baseline (`completion-recovery-requires-the-exact-origin-lease-body-and-successor` `:body` case). This is exactly 7a's `variant-equal` / `variant-other-root` fixtures (`rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc:230-232`, `grant_test` `:variant-conflict`), so 7c agrees with 7a D2 and with the code. I agree with the semantics: the root is the semantic identity of the continuation, a changed counter is a different continuation state; the plan's §1.1 row-1 line (\"id-counter variant, equal baseline\") is the stale text (F3 below).\n   - No vacuous test found: every cut asserts it fired; the new source-index test asserts `pos? calls`, the refusal status and reason, no machine, no offer; the new unfenced-report test asserts the exact refusal map and an unchanged projection; the fencing lower asserts `pos?` attaches on success and `zero?` on the fail-closed case.\n   - `fencing_rows_test`: lift under header with `@rs/source-index`; `lower-task` with `:exclusive`, `:protection`, `:grant` (evidence, binding, tenure, prefix); op-ids read from the pending row's slots at `write-slots`' indices (3/6/7, `rows.cljc:1170`), preserved through park → pack → lower → fenced retry, counter restored exactly; `markers` reads `[id kind & slots]` rows (layout at `rows.cljc:874`). Correct.\n3. **`handoff_v2_authority_test`.** Deleted: `authority-for`, the `offer!` of a version-2 fork (`:not-offerable`) and exclusive (`:committed`), and the `report!` of a halted version-2 fork. All three exercise the authority over version-2 bytes, a path that no longer exists (7a D1). Kept, renamed: the inspector's fork/exclusive baselines and the halted result's origin, on all four engines; `a-fork-tree-refuses-a-carried-operation-id` untouched. Rows-side coverage of the deleted claims exists: `grant_test` `offers-the-authority-cannot-hold-are-refused` (`:not-offerable`, `:uninspectable` for a fork pack) and `completion_test` `a-halted-body-must-name-this-occurrence-and-lease` (`:wrong-origin`), plus the new `a-composed-authority-refuses-an-unfenced-result-report-test`. Nothing that remains lost coverage; nothing deleted was about remaining code. The ns docstring misnames the covering suite (F2).\n4. **Stage E errors**: see the quick lane row; the five are the only red UCF tests and the cause is the missing `:source-index` at `open!`, 7d's.\n5. **Slow deftests**: green, see the lane table.\n6. **Portability read.** No `for`, no `#'`/private access across namespaces (everything used from `rows-support`, `rows-checkpoint-fixtures`, `rows`, `export`, `rows.checkpoint` is public), catches `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)` in the three new try sites, `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)` order kept on the subprocess test, base64 only through `jing/base64->bytes` with `(vec …)` equality (driver_test), `(comp vec reverse)` on the rows vector, `(str (random-uuid))` was already in the file. Unverified on Node/Dart by instruction.\n7. **Mutants**: table below.\n\n## Mutants (mine)\n\nDriver `target/7c-review/mut.py`; per-mutant diff, log and backup under `target/7c-review/mutants/`; each restore byte-compared and `git diff --quiet` on the file. Scoped to the named suites, quick tier.\n\n| Mutant | File | Killed? | By |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| R01 recovery stored after the pack | driver | yes (8) | `the-source-half-mints-fences-and-offers-before-candidacy-test` (F1), `the-wired-compositions-content-store-cuts-test` |\n| R02 custody stage after attach | rows | yes (41) | four zero-attach deftests of `rows_custody_test`, `row-2`, `row-7-a-successor-whose-lower-fails`, the fencing fail-closed case |\n| R03a grant `:not-holder` check removed | rows | yes (24) | `every-grant-check-refuses-before-any-attachment` only |\n| R03b `accept-grant!` skipped (nil state) in the compose path | rows | yes (304) | 40 deftests across compose-rows, fencing, rows-custody |\n| R04 fenced lower gates `:running` | rows | yes (1) | `an-exclusive-blocked-root-under-fenced-skips-the-grant` only (as 7b F6 predicted; export/driver mask it via `extract-recovered`) |\n| R05 offer carries the recovery address instead of the pack address | driver | yes (560) | 66 deftests across compose, compose-rows, driver |\n| R06 store ack journaled before the put | driver | yes (6) | `serving-and-storage-have-write-ahead-brackets-test`, `the-wired-compositions-content-store-cuts-test` |\n| R06b test substrate: cut `:before`/`:after` meaning swapped | compose_rows_test | yes (1+1) | `the-wired-compositions-content-store-cuts-test` (codex's M6 confirmed) |\n| R07a compose hands the driver an empty source index | compose | yes (337) | 47 deftests incl. `the-source-index-is-read-from-the-composition-test`, the no-REPL subprocess test |\n| R07b `open!`'s `check!` for `:source-index` removed | compose | **no** (0) | none: every composition in the suites supplies the index; no test pins the open-time refusal (F1) |\n| R08a `:wrong-origin` never | completion | yes (13) | `a-composed-authority-refuses-an-unfenced-result-report-test`, `a-halted-body-must-name-this-occurrence-and-lease`, `the-report-verifies-its-successor` |\n| R08b halted result skips the origin check | completion | yes (5) | the same first two |\n| R09a fence record not journaled | driver | yes (98) | 45 deftests |\n| R09b fence journaled without storing either object | driver | yes (28+26) | 31 deftests incl. both cut suites and `failed-recovery-store-does-not-attempt-the-body-store-test` |\n| R10 Q3 record-address conjunct → `contains?` | rows | yes (2) | `the-install-response-is-verified-before-any-attachment` (F2) |\n| R11 lift stops refusing missing trees | rows | yes (4) | `the-lift-takes-trees-from-the-composition-source-index-test`, `the-source-index-is-read-from-the-composition-test` |\n| R12 inspector drops `:yin.k/root` from the baseline | rows/checkpoint | yes (4+13) | `a-conflicting-variant-is-refused`, the content-store cuts, 13 compose-rows exit/recovery tests |\n\nCodex's nine mutants are consistent with mine where they overlap (M1↔R01, M2↔R02, M3↔R03b, M4↔R04, M5↔R05, M6↔R06b, M7↔R07a, M8↔R08a, M9↔R10). Codex's claim that all eight requested mutants are caught holds; the one survivor here is a mutant outside the brief's list.\n\n## Findings\n\n| id | severity | file:line | finding | fix |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| F1 | low (test gap) | `test/yin/vm/ucf/compose_test.cljc:682` | No test pins that `compose/open!` refuses a config without a function `:source-index` (R07b: removing the `check!` at `compose.cljc:188` passes compose-test and compose-rows-test). The refusal is exercised only by the red Stage E suite, so once 7d supplies the index nothing will guard it. | Add one case beside `a-journal-substrate-is-checked-at-open-test`: `(is (thrown-with-msg? … #\"source index\" (compose/open! (dissoc cfg :source-index))))` for both modes, and one with `:source-index {}` (not a fn). Six lines; can land with 7d or as a follow-up. |\n| F2 | info | `test/yin/vm/ucf/handoff_v2_authority_test.cljc:3` | The ns docstring says the authority paths \"are covered by rows-custody-test\"; that suite covers lift/lower custody, not the authority. The rows-side coverage of the deleted assertions is `authority/grant_test` (`:not-offerable`, `:uninspectable`), `authority/completion_test` (`:wrong-origin`) and compose-rows' new unfenced-report test. | Name those suites in the docstring. |\n| F3 | info (plan amendment, 7d/7e) | `collab/phase7-plan.md` §1.1 row `[:row-1]` | The plan still says a re-emitted id-counter variant has \"a new root id and new pack; equal baseline\". With `:yin.k/root` in the baseline (§3.3, 7a) that variant is `:variant-conflict`; the only equal-baseline encoding is the same root in another row order, which 7a's fixtures, 7c's `variant-of` and the ledger all agree on. Codex reports this as deviation 2 but lists \"no design mismatch\"; it is 7a D2, unchanged. 7d's `offer-variant!` and `stage_e_scenarios` row-1 check must use the reversed-row encoding, and 7e amends the plan/continuation text. Also inherited from 7a D2: `rows/decode` accepts unsorted packs, which is what makes the second encoding admissible at all; \"one root, one pack\" is a property of `emit`, not of readers. Owner/Architect to confirm that stays so. | 7d: reversed rows for row-1's second encoding. 7e: amend §1.1 row-1 and note the decode stance. |\n| F4 | info | `test/yin/vm/ucf/compose_rows_test.cljc:2541` | The position-2 `:after` check compares the stored wire's `:yin.k/root` with `inspect`'s `:yin.k/root`, which `inspect-pack` copies from the same wire (`rows/checkpoint.cljc:80`); the assertion's real content is that `inspect` accepts the pack. Harmless. | Optional: compare with `(:root frozen)` instead, which ties the stored pack's root to the frozen record. |\n| F5 | info | `test/yin/vm/ucf/compose_test.cljc:1118` | The source-index test can assert only `:export-refused`, not the lift's `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete` detail, because the driver's `unsatisfied` keeps `status/reason/kind/path` only. R11 (missing trees not refused) is still killed because the holder then reaches `:lifted`. | None required. |\n\nNo production code changed in 7c and none of my mutants found a production defect. The owner's rulings hold in the tests as written: the composition's source index is the only route for trees (R07a, R11), A names code, pack address is storage and the root is semantic (R12), no version dispatch or shim appeared, lease/epoch/holder stay out of packs (7a grammar, unchanged), `yin.repl` untouched and the no-REPL subprocess test green on the semantic-register receiver, the de Bruijn kernels untouched.\n\nVERDICT: READY_TO_MERGE (into the integration branch `srvm-phase7`). F1 is a six-line test addition, non-blocking; F3 is a 7d/7e note, not a 7c defect.\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-14 +07\n# Slice 7d: Stage E over semantic-register row packs\n\nWorktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7d`, branch `srvm-phase7d`. No commit. JVM verification only; commands ran serially in the foreground, with logs under `target/7d-*`. No production code, REPL, old codec, row grammar, layout, or harness change remains.\n\n## Result\n\nStage E's source and receiver machines now come from `rows-support`: changing the world’s support require ports both `parked-reader-over` and `reading-writer` to the semantic-register kernel without duplicating their AST builders. Both composition opens supply the function `:source-index (fn [] @s/source-index)`, including the exclusive-refusal control in the fork scenario. Code and trees take the existing composition/linker seam.\n\n`offer-variant!` reverses the pack’s row vector, retains its root and custody baseline, computes its BLAKE3 pack address, stores it and offers it through the holder’s attributed inbound. Occurrence discovery uses the rows inspector. Row 1 now explicitly compares the two encodings’ complete inspected baselines and distinct pack addresses, as well as asserting two admitted variants and exactly one running holder. A missing winner reaches assertion failures instead of throwing from `val nil`.\n\nSnapshot `:fences` already reads the addressed stored bytes; those objects are now packs, so its existing BLAKE3 digests retain their meaning. New `:packs` observations inspect the stored pack, check sorted row ids, and decode/re-emit it on the current host. Every reported final or named snapshot compares that host’s re-emitted canonical byte digest to the actual fenced pack digest. This is a portable Row 10 assertion: on the process matrix, receiver hosts re-emit bytes produced by source hosts. It excludes the intentionally unsorted alternative encoding, which is offered directly rather than fenced.\n\nEvery life with a requested cut must report killed and exactly one event naming that cut. This common runner assertion covers both isolated runtimes and the unchanged process transport. Existing checks for unfenced exports, successor eligibility, input replay/divergence, operation-id assignment, reclaim, lost authority, and fork labels remain.\n\n7c-review F1 is added beside the journal-substrate test: absent and non-function (`{}`) source indexes refuse at open in both fork and exclusive modes. F2's version-2 authority docstring now names `authority.grant-test`, `authority.completion-test`, and `compose-rows-test`.\n\n## Files and line counts\n\nFinal line counts and additions/deletions against this worktree’s HEAD:\n\n| File | Lines | Added | Deleted |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/stage_e_world.cljc` | 1410 | 26 | 8 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/stage_e_scenarios.cljc` | 1326 | 20 | 1 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/compose_test.cljc` | 1139 | 18 | 0 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/handoff_v2_authority_test.cljc` | 66 | 2 | 1 |\n| This report | 88 | — | — |\n\nThe Stage E rows suite has 13 deftests (5 quick, 8 slow); the process suite has 13 slow deftests. `stage_e_peer`, `stage_e_rows_test`, and `stage_e_process_test` need no lasting edit. The Node peer build sets `yin.vm.ucf.stage-e-peer/main` on the `:vm-bench` node-script target; it has no old fixture namespace allowlist or regexp to update. Its transitive world require picks up `rows-support`, rows, and the rows inspector.\n\n## Verification\n\n| Gate | Tests | Assertions | Failures / errors | Log |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| Initial touched quick run, Stage E rows + compose | 21 | 237 | 0 / 0 | `target/7d-quick.log` |\n| Stage E rows slow (`DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`, `-i :slow`) | 8 | 632 | 0 / 0 | `target/7d-stage-e-slow.log` |\n| Every `yin.vm.ucf.*` quick (`-e :slow -r 'yin\\.vm\\.ucf\\..*'`) | 701 | 9452 | 0 / 0 | `target/7d-ucf-quick.log` |\n| Every UCF slow except the process matrix | 13 | 2926 | 0 / 0 | `target/7d-ucf-slow.log` |\n| Final touched quick + unchanged rows, semantic-register harness and layout | 88 | 4383 | 0 / 0 | `target/7d-final-quick.log` |\n| Unchanged semantic-register harness/layout slow | 3 | 2716 | 0 / 0 | `target/7d-harness-slow.log` |\n| Named process handoff gate, restricted to JVM/JVM | 1 | 26 | 0 / 0 | `target/7d-process-gate.log` |\n\nLint over all four changed source files: 0 errors, 0 warnings (`target/7d-lint.log`). Final `git diff --check` and `git diff --quiet -- src` pass. No red test observed in the tested UCF quick/slow namespaces. The process matrix is unverified, not claimed green.\n\nThe process gate ran `the-cross-host-handoff-across-processes-test` with its pair vector temporarily changed to `[[:jvm :jvm]]`, exactly as the brief permits. `target/7d-process-gate.py` backs up the file, runs the named test, restores it in `finally`, byte-compares the backup and verifies `git diff --quiet` on that file. The durability record contains `[:row-7 nil]`, pair `[:jvm :jvm]`, two distinct peer pids. This clean-handoff gate requests no crash cut; it is not evidence of a real SIGKILL run. The unchanged process harness blocks the peer on a cut and calls `destroyForcibly`; the orchestrator must run the killed lives on its full matrix.\n\n## Mutants\n\nDriver: `target/7d-mutants.py`. Artifacts: `target/7d-mutants/<name>/` (byte backups, actual diff, test log), plus summaries. Each mutant ran alone, in the foreground, against one named JVM deftest. Backups are restored in `finally`; each restore runs `git diff --no-index --quiet` against its backup and `git diff --quiet -- src`. All seven requested mutants were caught; an additional mutant checks F1.\n\n| Mutant | Change | Catching test | Failures / errors |\n|---|---|---|---:|\n| M1 | Omit the driver’s durable fence record while advancing the world’s export step | Stage E `row-1-candidates-and-encodings-test` | 0 / 1 |\n| M2 | Offer a re-emitted task with id-counter slot 4 changed to 9, and make grant variant comparison ignore `:yin.k/root` | Stage E `row-1-candidates-and-encodings-test` | 8 / 0 |\n| M3 | Call the successor’s offer step where its fence step must run | Stage E `row-7-failure-lower-and-lost-authority-test` | 0 / 1 |\n| M4 | Skip destruction of the permanently lost authority | Stage E `row-7-failure-lower-and-lost-authority-test` | 3 / 0 |\n| M5 | Make `:row7/while-running`’s cut predicate never match | Stage E `row-7-failure-lower-and-lost-authority-test` | 3 / 0 |\n| M6 | Skip the origin check, accepting an unfenced result report | `compose-rows-test/a-composed-authority-refuses-an-unfenced-result-report-test` | 2 / 0 |\n| M7 | Report an exclusive status-policy for the per-host fork | Stage E `fork-per-host-test` | 1 / 0 |\n| M8 (extra) | Remove compose’s open-time function check for `:source-index` | `compose-test/a-source-index-is-checked-at-open-test` | 4 / 0 |\n\nM1 and M3 fail with scenario errors because the required durable checkpoint is absent or an offer is attempted without valid bytes. M2 initially failed with `val nil` when no winner ran; the row-1 check was made nil-safe and the mutant rerun, so it now exposes the failed custody assertions directly. The M2 mutation changes both the world encoding and the authority admission comparison; it does not change the ledger fold, which independently enforces baseline consistency. M6 uses the existing composition authority control from 7c, not a newly invented Stage E scenario. M7 injects a divergent fork observation into the common per-host check; it is a JVM execution of that portable check, not cross-host runtime evidence.\n\n## Not run\n\n- Node or Dart in-process Stage E, including their slow bodies and compiler checks.\n- Node peer compilation/execution, `npm ci`, or the four-pair process matrix on shiva/jupiter. The brief delegates these to the orchestrator; no remote checkout or push was attempted, and no commit was made.\n- The other 12 process deftests, including real SIGKILL cuts. The local process check above is the restricted clean JVM/JVM gate only.\n\nPortability was checked by reading: the additions are ordinary `.cljc` data/sequence operations, no `for`, no cross-namespace private vars, no added host-specific types, no new catches, no unary float minus or EDN string traps. Runtime portability remains unverified until the orchestrator’s Node/Dart runs.\n\n## Deviations and design defects\n\n1. **Source index at composition, not holder.** The brief/plan says `holder-seams` supplies `:source-index`, but current `compose/check-holder-config-keys!` explicitly rejects that key in a holder config. The code wins: both `compose/open!` configs supply it, and compose forwards it to the driver. No holder seam, resolver, or compatibility workaround was added.\n2. **Inherited 7a D2 / 7c F3 remains a design-text defect.** An id-counter change changes the root; the baseline includes that root, so this is `:variant-conflict`. Row 1 uses the owner-ruled equal-root reversed-row encoding. `rows/decode` accepts an unsorted pack while `emit` produces sorted rows. No semantic rule was changed to make the stale plan true.\n3. **The plan’s suite inventory is stale.** Section 1.1 says 12 deftests in each Stage E suite; the current files have 13 each (rows: 5 quick, 8 slow). No suite was removed to match the inventory.\n4. **No new production defect found.** Recovery v2 first, pack second, fence before publication, pack address as storage/ledger id, root as semantic identity, and custody state outside body lease/epoch/holder remain as implemented. Row 5’s pinned gap-marker commitment remains unchanged pending the existing Architect ruling. The accepted REPL custody gap remains untouched.\n\n## What 7e needs to state\n\n- Stage E sources/receivers use only semantic-register machines; de Bruijn kernels remain cross-engine-law backends, not Stage E participants.\n- Code is identified by A, with trees supplied by the composition’s function source-index seam; holder configs cannot override it.\n- Row-1 equal-baseline variants have the same root with another row order. Counter changes conflict. Sorted canonical emission gives one pack for a root; readers still accept the unsorted alternative.\n- Recovery version 2 is stored before the whole pack object; the journal fences both addresses before any source/successor publication. Existing store/body cut names refer to those two object puts.\n- Pack BLAKE3 address is the store key, offer variant id, grant checkpoint and report result; `:yin.k/root` is the baseline’s structural identity. Fork custody slot is nil; lease/epoch/holder remain outside the body.\n- Update UCF/continuation citations to the rows inspector and current custody/fenced lower stages, plus F2’s actual covering suites.\n- Separate local evidence from tri-host acceptance: all local JVM rows tests and the clean JVM/JVM process gate pass, but Node/Dart and the killed four-pair matrix must still be reported before phase 7 integration goes to master. The REPL’s accepted custody gap is not closed by this slice.\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/7a-report.md collab/1792450000000-engineer-7b-holder-export.report.md collab/1792480000000-engineer-7c-compose-fencing.report.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/7a-report.md collab/1792450000000-engineer-7b-holder-export.report.md collab/1792480000000-engineer-7c-compose-fencing.report.md'","aggregated_output":"Created-Local: 2026-10-13 +07\n# Slice 7a report: custody on rows, the rows inspector, the authority's fixtures (opus, engineer)\n\nWorktree `srvm-phase7a`, from `23a8caef`. Nothing committed. JVM only, all runs in the foreground (one quick-tier run of every `yin.vm.ucf.*` namespace went past the 10-minute foreground limit, finished in the background and was read from its log).\n\n## Verdict\n\n7a is done as the plan's slice section and §3.1, §3.3 and §3.4 describe it, and its own acceptance holds: the two new suites, the ledger fixture, crash cuts, the eight authority tests and the holder tests that use fixtures all pass. `rows_test` and the safepoint harness pass with **no root id moved**.\n\n**7a cannot land alone (design defect D1 below).** The plan's switch of the authority to the rows inspector makes the authority refuse every version-1/2 body. 141 deftests in six old-codec D/E suites now fail. Those suites are re-based in 7b/7c, and I proved the switch is the only cause. I did not work around it, because a fix would change semantics; the decision belongs to the orchestrator.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Lint: `clojure -M:kondo` needed approval in this session, so I used `clj -M:kondo --lint <files>`. Final run over all 22 changed `.clj[c]` files: 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n- Quick tier, new and touched namespaces plus `rows_test`, the harness, `layout_test`, the old `checkpoint_test` and `lift_v1_test` (20 namespaces): **360 tests, 7188 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors** (`target/7a-final.log`).\n  - `rows_checkpoint_test`: 17 deftests.\n  - `rows_custody_test`: 11 deftests, 107 assertions before the M3 fix added two more.\n- Slow tier (`-i :slow`) on the same namespaces plus `rows_test` and the harness: 4 tests, 2724 assertions, 0 failures.\n- Every `yin.vm.ucf.*` namespace, quick tier (`-r 'yin\\.vm\\.ucf\\..*'`, `target/7a-ucf-quick.log`): 694 tests, 648 failures and 89 errors. They are all in six suites (see D1):\n\n  | Namespace | Failing deftests | Failing assertions |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | `holder.driver-test` | 92 | 294 |\n  | `compose-rows-test` | 35 | 335 |\n  | `compose-test` | 6 | 35 |\n  | `stage-e-rows-test` | 4 (the quick ones) | 35 |\n  | `fencing-rows-test` | 3 | 30 |\n  | `handoff-v2-authority-test` | 1 | 8 |\n\n  No other `yin.vm.ucf` namespace fails.\n- Counterfactual check: I pointed the three authority requires back at `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint` and ran `fencing-rows-test`, `handoff-v2-authority-test` and `compose-test`: 21 tests, 332 assertions, 0 failures. I then restored the files (byte-compare verified).\n- Node and Dart were not run, as instructed. The new code follows the portability rules: no `for`, no private var access, catches with `:cljd` first, start-from-`{}` assoc, no EDN strings. The `version-float` fixture relies on Node keeping a `Float64` carrier and on Dart's `integer?` rejecting a double, both checked in the source (`rows/decode` gates on `code/reg?`), but this is unverified on those hosts.\n\n## Files\n\nCreated:\n\n| File | Lines | What |\n|---|---|---|\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows/checkpoint.cljc` | 102 | The rows inspector: `inspect address bytes`, `inspect-pack wire`. Address check, canonical CBOR, `rows/decode`, a `:task` root, then `rows/custody-baseline`; adds `:yin.k/root` |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_support.cljc` | 238 | Semantic-register machine builders mirroring `lift_support` (it reuses that namespace's AST helpers, one-slot streams, `serve-table`, header parts and `with-op-id`): `new-machine`, `project` with `source-index`, `parked-reader`, `parked-writer`, `parked-explicit`, `parked-linker`, `parked-installer`, `halted-machine`, `halted-with-cursors`, `parked-module-store-cursors`, `lift`, `attacher` (counting), `receiver` |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc` | 452 | Five bases lifted under a header; 50 mutations (task edits re-emitted, raw pack edits where the defect is the carrier's own); `render`/`read-file`/`fixture`; editing helpers the authority tests use (`base`, `pack-of`, `edit-task`, `edit-custody`, `waits`, `update-put`, `set-op-id`, `update-child`, `enc`) |\n| `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` | 917 | 55 pins, 3 anchors with full bytes |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_test.cljc` | 203 | 17 deftests |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc` | 275 | 11 deftests |\n| `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/ledger-v2.txt` | 22 | Replaces `ledger-v1.txt` (deleted with `git rm`, so the deletion is staged) |\n\nChanged:\n\n- `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` (411 lines changed per `git diff --stat`, now 2219 lines):\n  - `pack`: rows sorted by id, used by `emit`, `emit-numbered` and `frame->rows`.\n  - The custody section: `max-exact`, `write-slots`, `custody-baseline`, `pack-address`.\n  - `lift-task :header`: `header-custody` and `protect!`, `:op-seq-exhausted`, and the grammar run before emitting.\n  - `check-custody!` rewritten as the custody stage: grammar, then fork under `:exclusive`, then `accept-grant!` ported verbatim. It runs once after the three per-tree stages and before `attach-streams!`.\n  - `with-custody` in restore: issue numbering, gate, custody state, and the fenced gate.\n- `authority/{grant,completion,admission}.cljc`: the require switched to `yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint` under the same alias, so each call site is unchanged; docstrings updated.\n- Fixture bodies only:\n  - Named in the plan: `ledger_fixtures`, `ledger_fixture_test` (docstring), `grant_test`, `completion_test`, `inherited_test`, `front_test`. `crash_cut_test` needed no edit; it follows `lf/park`.\n  - Also needed (deviation 1): `admission_test`, `input_test`, `reclaim_test`, `holder/evidence_test`, `holder/reader_test`, `holder/writer_test`.\n- `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc`: one expected value (deviation 2).\n\n## What the code does (for review)\n\n- **Custody slot (root slot 16):**\n  - nil for a fork of any kind (owner Q1).\n  - `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq}` plus `:yin.k/origin` for blocked or parked exclusive roots.\n  - `{:yin.k/origin}` alone for a halted exclusive root.\n  - nil on every child.\n- **`custody-baseline`** is the version-1 header and op-id grammar of `checkpoint.cljc`, keyed off the slot. On top of that it:\n  - refuses any key outside the five custody keys (`:custody-key`), so lease, epoch and holder cannot enter a body;\n  - refuses a slot that is not a map (`:malformed-custody`);\n  - checks each task's kind and role, that each `:install` pending names an install row, and that every child slot is nil;\n  - refuses any op-id in a fork tree (`:fork-op-id`, closing continuation §11 Q4 for 7a).\n\n  Paths are relative to the root task: `[:custody k]`, `[:frames i :op-id …]`, and `[:installs <name> …]` inside children. The intent digest is BLAKE3 of the canonical `[:yin.k/append target-identity payload-slot]`, where `[:row n]` becomes `[:row id]`.\n- **The lift** runs the grammar over the abstract task before emitting, the same contract as the version-1 lift running its inspector over its own bytes. It does not decode its own output, which keeps continuation §7.1 step 9 true. A fork lift of a machine that carries op-ids therefore refuses `:fork-op-id`; before 7a the op-ids travelled verbatim.\n- **The lower's custody stage**, in order: grammar; then a fork under `:exclusive` is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; a halted exclusive root needs no grant and is gated `:ended`; `:fenced` skips the grant and gates `:exporting`, with waits moved to `:yin.k/recovery-waits`; otherwise `accept-grant!` runs. The grant's `:checkpoint` must equal `rows/pack-address` of the wire, not the root id; a test pins that the root id is refused as `:not-holder`.\n- **Restore:**\n  - `:yin.k/custody` comes from the evidence, with `:yin.k/next-op-seq` equal to the slot's value exactly.\n  - In an exclusive tree every task numbers its `:put` waits (`:yin.k/issue`, `:yin.k/issued`) and is gated `:running`, as `resume-task*` does.\n- **The two identities:**\n  - The pack address is the ledger's `:yin.k/id`, the content-store key, the grant's checkpoint and the report's result.\n  - The root id is the baseline's `:yin.k/root`.\n  - Tested in `the-pack-address-is-storage-and-the-root-id-is-semantic`: address = BLAKE3 of the bytes; root ≠ address; root = the root row's own hash; rows sorted; re-emitting a decoded pack gives the same bytes. Also tested in `a-pack-variant-of-one-root-shares-its-baseline`.\n\n## Mutation checks\n\nDriver: `target/7a-mut/{mut,run}.py`. Each mutant was applied to a backup-guarded copy, run against `rows-custody-test`, `rows-checkpoint-test`, `authority.grant-test` and `ledger-fixture-test`, then restored. Each restore was byte-compared with a clean copy; all were clean.\n\n| # | Mutant | Caught? | By |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| M1 | Custody header by kind swapped (`header-custody` branches) | yes | `the-lift-writes-the-custody-slot-by-kind`, every base (they no longer lift), 46 deftests |\n| M2 | A grant check skipped (holder vs binding) | yes | `every-grant-check-refuses-before-any-attachment` (\"another holder\") |\n| M3 | Protection, one direction dropped (an enrolled stream classed otherwise accepted) | **survived at first**; yes after the fix | The op-id presence check that follows masked it on the successor. I added the isolating case (a reader pack with no retained write) to `protection-is-checked-in-both-directions` |\n| M4 | Counter restore off by one | yes | `the-counter-is-restored-exactly`, `an-exclusive-root-lowers-under-its-grant`, `install-children-lower-…` |\n| M5 | Fork under `:exclusive` accepted | yes | `a-fork-is-refused-under-exclusive` |\n| M6 | Custody stage after attach | yes | The zero-attach assertions in 4 deftests (30 assertions) |\n| M7 | Unsorted `emit` | yes | `the-file-holds-exactly-the-declared-bytes` (anchors), `the-pack-address-…`, the ledger frames, 14 deftests |\n| M8 | Inspector accepting a mutated row (decode's row-hash check off) | yes | `each-mutation-is-refused-as-it-names` (`row-tampered`) |\n| M9 | Baseline root ignored | yes | 11 deftests, including `a-conflicting-variant-is-refused` (`variant-other-root`) and the ledger projection digest |\n| M10 (extra) | The lift skips the grammar | yes | `the-lift-refuses-what-custody-forbids` |\n\n## Deviations\n\n1. **Six more test files rebased.** `authority/{admission,input,reclaim}_test` and `holder/{evidence,reader,writer}_test` offer fixture bodies through `grant/offer!` and break without the switch. Each change is a three-to-five-line `body` helper.\n2. **One harness assertion changed.** `carrier-steps-run-in-order-over-the-tree` expected the phase-6 refusal `[:yin.k/unsupported :custody]` for slot `{:custody :exclusive}`. The plan replaces `check-custody!`, so it now expects `[:yin.k/undecodable :custody-key]`. Nothing else in the harness changed, and no root id moved.\n3. **`:fenced` is minimal.** It skips the grant and protection, gates `:exporting` on every task and moves the waits to `:yin.k/recovery-waits`. `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids`, the exact id counter, the recovery header check and moving parked records into the record are left to 7b (plan §3.2 costs the fenced mode there).\n4. **Refusal kinds and paths are mine:**\n   - New kinds: `:custody-key`, `:malformed-custody`, `:policy` (the old inspector gave a policy mismatch no kind), `:kind`, `:role`, `:child-header`, `:fork-op-id`, `:incomplete-install`.\n   - Paths: `[:custody …]`, `[:frames i …]`, `[:installs <name> …]`.\n   - Carrier-level refusals keep `rows/decode`'s own: a missing tag is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch :version` on rows, not `:undecodable :body` as on version 1.\n5. **No op-id-on-variant fixture.** It is structurally impossible on rows: the pending table gives `:next` no op-id slot. I added fixtures the old set lacked: `frame-root`, `row-tampered`, `root-role`, `custody-epoch`, `custody-lease`, `custody-not-map`, `fork-op-id`, `variant-other-root`.\n6. **The grammar lives in `rows.cljc`** (`custody-baseline`). `rows.checkpoint` is a thin wrapper around it, because `lower-task` must run the grammar too and `rows.checkpoint` requires `rows` (a cycle otherwise). Behaviour is what plan §3.3 assigns to the inspector.\n7. **Fixture shape.** `rows_checkpoint_fixtures` takes the `refusal` map a mutation must contain, instead of the old `expected` and `place` pair.\n8. **Accessor alias kept.** The authority still calls the inspector through the alias `checkpoint`.\n\n## Design defects found (separate list)\n\n- **D1. 7a cannot land alone.** The authority switch (plan §2, §4 7a \"Change\") refuses version-1/2 bodies at `offer!`, `report!` and `accepted-baseline`. The holder, compose and Stage E paths still offer such bodies until 7b/7c, so 141 deftests in six suites fail (table above; proven by the counterfactual). The plan's 7a acceptance never mentions them.\n  - Options for the orchestrator:\n    1. Merge 7a only together with 7b and 7c.\n    2. Move the three-line authority switch out of 7a into 7c.\n    3. A temporary version dispatch in the authority. This contradicts \"no version dispatch\" and is not recommended.\n  - `handoff_v2_authority_test` (an old-codec test the plan keeps until phase 8) also breaks permanently under any option. It needs deleting or re-basing, which the plan does not list.\n- **D2. Plan §1.1 row-1 contradicts §3.3.** Row-1 wants a re-emitted id-counter variant with \"a new root id and new pack; equal baseline\". With `:yin.k/root` in the baseline, which this brief requires, such a variant's baseline differs, and the ledger refuses it as `:variant-conflict` (pinned by `grant_test` `variant-other-root`). Under sorted `emit`, the only equal-baseline variant is the same root in another row order. I used that for `variant-equal`, `completion_test` and `inherited_test`.\n  - Stage E row-1 (7d) needs a ruling: either the variant is a conflict, or the root leaves the baseline.\n  - Related: `rows/decode` accepts rows in any order, so \"one root, one pack\" holds for what `emit` makes, not for what a reader accepts. Should decode refuse unsorted packs? I left decode unchanged.\n- **D3. Draft amendments to list (no doc edited):**\n  - transport-tuples §5.5 table: fork is nil in every row (owner Q1), and the slot admits only the five custody keys.\n  - continuation §2.5, §2.6, §7.1 step 8 and §7.2's stage table: custody is not phase-6 nil-only any more; the step-10 refusal `:yin.k/unsupported :custody` is replaced by the grammar's kinds and the grant refusals; the stage runs once for the tree after the three per-tree stages.\n  - continuation §11 Q4: closed for the op-id rules (correlation agreement is still not implemented).\n  - UCF 7.11.1: the inspector citation moves from `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint` to `yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint`.\n- **D4. The lift serves streams before custody refusals.** v1 refused `:op-seq-exhausted` before lifting; rows refuses it after the walk has called `serve!`. Harmless to the lower's zero-attach law, but a holder that counts serves will see them (7b's export record).\n\n## What 7b needs from me\n\n- `rows/lift-task` with `{:header h :source-index …}` is `holder.export/prepare` and `encode`'s new path; `h` is the same header shape (`:yin.k/enrolled` stays local).\n- `rows/pack-address wire` is the storage key to store and fence. The grant's `:checkpoint` must be that address.\n- `rows/lower-task recv wire attach! {:grant g :protection p}` is the driver's `lower`; `{:exclusive true}` refuses a fork; `{:fenced true}` is the rehydration entry. Finishing fenced is 7b's: recovery-cell-ids, exact id counter, parked records into the record, the header check against `:yin.k/state`.\n- `rows.checkpoint/inspect` answers the old inspector's keys plus `:yin.k/root`; `reopen-holder` and `rebuilt-cell` can read `:yin.k/kind`, `:yin.k/occurrence` and `:yin.k/next-op-seq` from it.\n- `rows_support` builders for the driver, writer and reader tests, and `rows_checkpoint_fixtures`' editing helpers for packs.\n- D1 decides whether 7b lands together with 7a.\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-13 +07\n# Slice 7b: holder export, recovery v2, and driver lower\n\nImplemented and verified in `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7b`, branch `srvm-phase7b`. No commit. JVM only; every test process ran in the foreground, sequentially, including the mutation runs. No Node, Dart, remote host, network fetch, or escalation was needed.\n\n## Result\n\n7b's acceptance passes. The holder exports row packs under the composition's source index, freezes recovery format 2 with one pack and holder-local metadata, and rehydrates through fenced row lowering without program IO or child initialization. Exact counters, cursor aliases/distinct cells, all parked records, authentic receiver-owned values, operation IDs and issue stamps survive recovery. The recovered record reproduces the published pack byte for byte and cannot abort into execution. Q3's link/parent and manifest/image checks refuse before attachment. The driver's validation, grant lower and restart paths use rows; `:export-version` is removed from the driver/composition.\n\nThe existing partial edit was reviewed first with `git diff`, then by production-file review and tests. Its row export/driver/recovery implementation was retained. I removed its copied legacy holder export implementation from `lift_support`, moved the five preparation tests to the row holder suite, and added a two-record parked recovery test. The legacy codec is exercised directly through its existing `handoff/export-task`; no compatibility shim or old recovery reader was added.\n\nThis is integration-branch readiness for **7b**, not readiness to land the entire phase on master. The deferred red suites are listed below.\n\n## Files and line counts\n\nCounts are final file lines and added/deleted lines versus this worktree's HEAD, including the retained partial edit.\n\n| File | Final lines | Added | Deleted |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/compose.cljc` | 1009 | 11 | 6 |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc` | 2943 | 82 | 56 |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export.cljc` | 628 | 302 | 242 |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` | 2262 | 66 | 23 |\n| `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` | 946 | 306 | 277 |\n| `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc` | 1939 | 11 | 2 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/handoff_v2_holder_test.cljc` | 217 | 5 | 156 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver_test.cljc` | 4255 | 45 | 45 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export_test.cljc` | 974 | 625 | 563 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/reader_test.cljc` | 1445 | 1 | 1 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/writer_test.cljc` | 1127 | 6 | 5 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/lift_support.cljc` | 454 | 3 | 21 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/lift_v1_test.cljc` | 471 | 2 | 138 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc` | 315 | 40 | 0 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_support.cljc` | 297 | 64 | 5 |\n\n`holder/evidence_test.cljc`, `rows_test.cljc`, and `layout_test.cljc` needed no edit. The harness change only supplies complete linker-style manifest/derivation data for Q3's install fixtures; its existing assertions and root pins were not edited. The rows checkpoint resource was regenerated for the extended install responses and is verified against its renderer. Production `handoff.cljc` and the old inspector/codec implementations remain untouched.\n\n## Implementation details that matter to review\n\n- `holder.export/prepare` retains served answers by `[task-path resource-id]`, resolves handle aliases from the retained record, and passes `:header`, `:source-index` and `:serve-keyed` to `rows/lift-task`. `encode` reads only those answers; it never serves externally.\n- Recovery format 2 contains exactly the tag, version, pack address, structural root, base64 pack, local header, and `:yin.k/state` (gate, origins, issued, ordered issues, child metadata). There is no second snapshot body, duplicate descriptor set, current lease, epoch or holder in the pack. The origin's predecessor lease remains as the specified lineage evidence.\n- Rehydration validates the recovery address, pack address/grammar, root, header/custody agreement and state/tree shape before attachment. Fenced lower preserves the exact carried id counter, records resource-key/cursor-cell associations, detaches waits and every parked record, and gates every task exporting. Child construction disables `:make-stream` during administrative spawning, restoring the composition's constructor afterward.\n- Q3 checks the response's `:yin.link/id` against the install parent and checks its image through the manifest-named semantic derivation record: record address, tree input and image output. It adds no fetcher, resolver or initialization path.\n- The driver still mints and journals occurrences before prepare, stores recovery before the pack, and preserves the existing fence/offer/report/release brackets and crash-cut names. The source index is a required composition seam, supplied as `(fn [] {A #{tree-address}})`.\n- 7a D4 is preserved: lift serves during its walk before deciding exhaustion/protection refusals. The record retains those answers, and the retry test proves no successful serve is repeated.\n\n## Verification\n\nAll changed Clojure files were linted with `clj -M:kondo --lint <changed files>`: **0 errors, 0 warnings**. `git diff --check` is clean.\n\nUnique successful namespace coverage (excluding duplicate focused/post-mutation runs): **514 quick tests / 8,941 assertions**, plus **5 slow tests / 3,113 assertions**, all with zero failures and errors. Each touched namespace was run using `clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns>` and `-i :slow`; the two support namespaces correctly select zero tests. All five holder namespaces and all eight authority namespaces were included. The rows checkpoint suite was also run in both tiers.\n\n| Namespace | Quick tests / assertions | Slow tests / assertions |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| `yin.vm.semantic-register.layout-test` | 12 / 2396 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test` | 23 / 1213 | 3 / 2716 |\n| `yin.vm.store-write-audit-test` | 3 / 129 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.admission-test` | 23 / 240 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.completion-fold-test` | 8 / 50 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.completion-test` | 25 / 303 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.front-test` | 30 / 217 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.grant-test` | 16 / 107 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.inherited-test` | 18 / 112 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.input-test` | 20 / 125 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.reclaim-test` | 21 / 108 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint-test` | 15 / 247 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.handoff-v2-holder-test` | 5 / 338 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.holder.driver-test` | 116 / 1524 | 1 / 389 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.holder.evidence-test` | 9 / 38 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.holder.export-test` | 36 / 325 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.holder.reader-test` | 24 / 247 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.holder.writer-test` | 25 / 202 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.ledger-fixture-test` | 6 / 53 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.lift-support` | 0 / 0 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.lift-v1-test` | 21 / 208 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.rows-checkpoint-test` | 17 / 179 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.rows-custody-test` | 12 / 127 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.rows-support` | 0 / 0 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.rows-test` | 29 / 453 | 1 / 8 |\n\nThe driver has **117 deftests total** (116 quick, one slow), as required. Rows, layout, and the safepoint harness retain their assertions; no existing pinned root was changed. The store-write audit, old checkpoint suite, old direct v1 lift tests and remaining v2 holder tests also pass. After restoring every mutant, the focused export/custody/checkpoint gate passed again: **65 tests / 631 assertions**.\n\nLogs and machine-readable results: `target/7b-verification/`, `results.json`, `final-checks.json`, `final-lint.log`, and `mutants/results.json`. These are local ignored verification artifacts.\n\n### Deferred red suites, observed unchanged\n\nThese quick-tier probes were run to identify the integration boundary; they are excluded from the green acceptance totals. No fixes were made in these suites.\n\n| Namespace | Tests / assertions | Failures / errors | Follow-up |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| `yin.vm.ucf.compose-test` | 14 / 23 | 3 / 20 | 7c: row machines and source-index wiring |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.compose-rows-test` | 39 / 39 | 0 / 39 | 7c: row machines, source-index and recovery-v2 assertions |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.fencing-rows-test` | 4 / 61 | 29 / 2 | 7c: row lift/lower and rows authority inspector |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.stage-e-rows-test` | 5 / 5 | 0 / 5 | 7d: port the world and its composition seams |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.handoff-v2-authority-test` | 3 / 68 | 8 / 0 | Known 7a D1: old v2 bodies are refused by the rows-only authority |\n\nThe Stage E process suite was not run: it requires the JVM/Node peer matrix, prohibited by this brief's JVM-only/no-Node instruction. Deferred compose/Stage E slow tiers belong to 7c/7d and were not run here. `yin.repl` was not touched, per owner Q2.\n\n## Mutations\n\nEach mutant ran in a fresh foreground JVM against `holder.export-test` or `rows-custody-test`. Sources were copied to backups first; each mutant's diff and test log were saved. After each run, restoration was checked byte for byte, with `git diff --quiet --no-index <backup> <source>`, and by comparing the entire binary worktree diff with its pre-mutation diff. Plain `git diff --quiet` against HEAD cannot be clean in this intentionally uncommitted worktree; no-index verifies restoration without erasing the intended edit. All nine behavior-changing mutants were killed; none survived.\n\n| Mutant | Failures / errors | Representative catching test |\n|---|---:|---|\n| `snapshot-body` | 1 / 0 | `the-recovery-object-is-the-pack-and-holder-local-state-test` |\n| `program-execution` | 6 / 0 | `complete-export-recovery-is-fenced-and-reproducible-test` |\n| `counter-off-by-one` | 64 / 4 | `the-id-counter-is-carried-exactly-test` |\n| `parked-record-dropped` | 14 / 0 | `recovery-retains-every-parked-record-test` |\n| `restart-abort-accepted` | 1 / 0 | `a-restarted-abort-is-refused-test` |\n| `install-link-check-removed` | 4 / 0 | `the-install-response-is-verified-before-any-attachment` |\n| `install-image-check-removed` | 10 / 0 | `the-install-response-is-verified-before-any-attachment` |\n| `source-index-omitted` | 81 / 10 | `the-lift-takes-trees-from-the-composition-source-index-test` |\n| `attach-before-custody` | 30 / 0 | `every-grant-check-refuses-before-any-attachment` |\n\nThe program-execution mutant reinstated recovery waits, removed the exporting gate and called `vm/run` during rehydration; the zero-IO assertions caught the resulting operations. The attachment-order mutant attached first and then ran custody; 30 zero-attachment assertions failed. Snapshot duplication was detected by the recovery object's exact-key assertion. The Q3 checks were removed separately, not together.\n\n## Deviations\n\n1. **Five old-suite preparation tests moved to `holder.export-test`.** They exercised the retired holder wrapper, not merely the old codec. The initial partial edit copied that wrapper into test support. I removed the copy and moved the tests onto real semantic-register machines; direct v0/v1 coverage remains on the old codec. `lift_support/lift` now calls the existing codec directly, and the child hold/path assertion calls the kernel-agnostic `export/enter`.\n2. **Q3 required fixture completion beyond holder files.** `rows_support` and the harness's module-response builder now carry the manifest tree, derivation address and record. The row checkpoint resource therefore changes for install-containing cases. Existing row/layout root pins remain unchanged.\n3. **No duplicate recovery descriptor set.** The pack already carries every stream descriptor. Rehydration derives the internal retained served table from those descriptors and administrative attachments. This follows the owner's “only holder-local engine state beside the pack” instruction and avoids persisting the same facts twice.\n4. **Header validation belongs to `rehydrate-fenced`.** It compares the local recovery header with the inspected pack custody and validates `:yin.k/state` against the task tree before calling `rows/lower-task {:fenced true}`. Raw row lowering does not take a recovery envelope or implement an independent recovery-header reader.\n5. **An additional all-parked-record test** covers an inactive record alongside the selected explicit park; both must survive with no IO and an identical re-encoded pack.\n\n## Design defects / discrepancies (separate list)\n\n- **D1, inherited from 7a:** the rows-only authority rejects old bodies. Compose/fencing/Stage E still need their planned ports, and the old v2 authority test is not repaired by those ports. The latter needs an explicit owner disposition before final phase acceptance; no version dispatch was introduced.\n- **D2, inherited from 7a:** the plan's different-root/equal-baseline variant conflicts with the actual baseline, which includes the root. Driver equal variants use a different row order with the same root; different roots remain conflicts. Recovery must reproduce canonical emitted bytes, so a merely reordered pack is valid carrier syntax but inconsistent recovery. The existing semantics were preserved and tested.\n- **D3, scope discrepancy:** the plan describes `lift_v1_test` as direct old-codec coverage to keep untouched, but five tests invoked the replaced holder preparation path. Keeping them unchanged would require the forbidden legacy wrapper/shim. Moving those five laws onto rows preserves their subjects without keeping the retired implementation alive.\n- **D4, recovery shape discrepancy:** plan §3.2's sketch includes a separate descriptor set; the owner brief says the pack plus only holder-local state. The implemented format omits the redundant set. Document this final shape in 7e.\n- **D5, known 7a lift ordering fact:** streams may already be served when a custody/protection lift refusal is returned. Accounting and retries preserve that fact; no zero-serve law was invented.\n\nNo additional unresolved production defect was found in the reviewed 7b implementation. Cross-host execution remains unverified here by instruction, not implied by the JVM results.\n\n## What 7c needs\n\n- Supply `:source-index` to `compose/open!` and driver configs as a function returning the composition/linker's current `{A #{tree-address}}`. Do not add a fallback resolver or synthetic index in production.\n- Build source and receiver machines on semantic-register, and use rows for fork/exclusive bodies. The holder answers pack bytes and their BLAKE3 **pack address**; the structural root is distinct and stays in the baseline.\n- Rebase content-store/restart assertions on recovery format 2: `:yin.k/pack-bytes`, `:yin.k/pack-address`, `:yin.k/root`, header and local state; no snapshot or duplicate descriptor set. Keep recovery-first/pack-second puts and the existing journal cut names.\n- Pass grant/protection/exclusive options to row lower. Preserve the no-attachment-before-custody law. Q3 install fixtures must carry a parent-correlated link ID and the manifest-named semantic derivation/image chain.\n- Use same-root/reordered-row packs for equal variants; use different-root packs only as conflicts. Preserve the accepted REPL gap until phase 8.\n- Run the deferred compose/fencing suites and their slow tiers, then hand the world/source-index/recovery changes to 7d for the Stage E matrix. No files in those suites or in `yin.repl` were edited here.\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-13 +07\n# Slice 7c: composition and fencing on row packs\n\nImplemented in `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7c`, branch `srvm-phase7c`. No commit. All test lanes ran serially in the foreground, JVM only. No Node, Dart, remote host, or production-code change.\n\n## Result\n\nF1 and F2 were the first edits: the driver's source fence test now asserts the journal's store intents name recovery first and the pack second; Q3's install test changes only the derivation record's profile while retaining its honest input/output. Both gaps are protected by actual killed mutants.\n\nComposition fixtures now build semantic-register receivers and source machines through `rows-support`, and every composition supplies its required `:source-index` seam. F3's smuggled holder key is `:source-index (fn [] {})`. The fresh-process plain-composition test still asserts `PHASE lifted` and `REPL-NAMESPACES []`, using a semantic-register source/receiver.\n\nFencing fixtures lift with a custody header and source index, and lower row packs with exclusive, protection, and grant options. Put, retained FFI, and retained link requests preserve their operation ids through park, pack, lower, and fenced retry. The original remote toy transport remains the attachment/observation seam; there is no new code resolver.\n\nThe composition crash tests now observe recovery v2 before the pack. They assert the cut object's identity and format, its presence/absence for after/before cuts, no fence or offer over incomplete storage, and stalled recovery with no attachments or second occurrence mint. Successor mutations decode and re-emit the abstract task/custody through the existing fixture helpers, preserving valid row hashes.\n\nA second admitted encoding reverses pack row order: it has a different pack address, the same root id, and the same baseline. A counter change modifies the root and is correctly a conflicting variant. Pack addresses remain store keys and ledger variant ids; the structural root stays inside the baseline.\n\nTwo focused controls were added: the composition must consult its supplied source index and refuse an empty index; a live composed authority must refuse an unfenced fork result report without changing its projection.\n\n## Files and line counts\n\nFinal lines and additions/deletions versus this worktree's HEAD:\n\n| File | Lines | Added | Deleted | Tests (quick / slow) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/compose_test.cljc` | 1121 | 39 | 12 | 15 / 0 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/compose_rows_test.cljc` | 2597 | 50 | 11 | 40 / 1 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/fencing_rows_test.cljc` | 629 | 105 | 76 | 4 / 0 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/handoff_v2_authority_test.cljc` | 65 | 24 | 60 | 3 / 0 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver_test.cljc` | 4261 | 6 | 0 | 116 / 1 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc` | 318 | 3 | 0 | 12 / 0 |\n\nThis report is the only additional deliverable. The six touched suites contain 192 tests: 190 quick and 2 slow. `compose_rows_test` has 41 tests including its guarded slow case. No pins, resources, rows implementation, layout implementation, kernels, or REPL files were edited.\n\n## Old-codec authority disposition (7a D1)\n\nDeleted only the version-2 authority calls and their assertions from the two mixed tests. Kept all direct inspector/codec coverage, on all four existing engines: fork versus exclusive baselines, fork operation-id refusal, and halted result origin. The two mixed tests were renamed to describe their retained inspector assertions. The old inspector and codec stay untouched until phase 8; no version dispatch or compatibility shim was added. Current row authority behavior is covered by rows-custody/authority suites and the new composed unfenced-report control.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `clj -M:kondo --lint` all six changed `.cljc` files: **0 errors, 0 warnings** (`target/7c-lint-final.log`). `git diff --check` clean.\n- Every `yin.vm.ucf.*` namespace, quick tier: `clojure -M:test -e :slow -r 'yin\\.vm\\.ucf\\..*'`: **700 tests, 9340 assertions, 0 failures, 5 errors** (`target/7c-ucf-quick.log`). All five errors are in `yin.vm.ucf.stage-e-rows-test`; no other namespace fails. The five cases are `row-1-candidates-and-encodings-test`, `row-7-failure-lower-and-lost-authority-test`, `row-3-reclaim-test`, `fork-per-host-test`, and `row-5-replay-divergence-test`. Each currently refuses assembly because `stage_e_world` supplies no source index. Stage E process cases are tagged slow and excluded; no Node process/build ran.\n- Every touched namespace was selected explicitly with `-n` in both tiers. The slow run with `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1` passed **2 tests, 435 assertions**, including `completion-recovery-unknown-policy-transaction-is-reconciled-once-test` (46 assertions) and the driver's existing slow journal-cut case (389 assertions), zero failures/errors (`target/7c-slow.log`).\n- Final post-mutation focused quick verification, selecting the six touched suites plus rows, both safepoint harnesses, and semantic-register layout explicitly with `-n`: **260 tests, 6942 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors** (`target/7c-final-quick.log`).\n- Final post-mutation slow verification with the same explicit namespace selection and `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`: **8 tests, 5010 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors** (`target/7c-final-slow.log`). This includes the untouched rows and both harnesses as well as both touched slow cases.\n- Rows, the UCF safepoint harness, semantic-register safepoint harness, and semantic-register layout are verified without edits. No root-id pin moved.\n\n## Mutants\n\nEach mutation was applied alone, with a `cp`-equivalent byte backup (`shutil.copy2`), saved diff, foreground JVM run, and restoration in `finally`. After each restore, `git diff --no-index --quiet <backup> <file>` and `git diff --quiet -- src` both passed. Logs, diffs, backups, and summary: `target/7c-mutants/`; reproducible driver: `target/7c-mutants.py`. Each scoped run used `-n <namespace> -v <namespace/test>` and excluded slow tests.\n\n| Mutant | Change | Catching test | Failures / errors |\n|---|---|---|---:|\n| M1 | Store pack before recovery | driver `the-source-half-mints-fences-and-offers-before-candidacy-test` (F1) | 1 / 0 |\n| M2 | Attach before deciding custody | rows-custody `every-grant-check-refuses-before-any-attachment` | 18 / 0 |\n| M3 | Skip `accept-grant!` in row lower, through compose | compose-rows `row-2-the-source-emits-nothing-until-its-own-grant-test` | 9 / 0 |\n| M4 | Fenced lower restores gate `:running` | rows-custody `an-exclusive-blocked-root-under-fenced-skips-the-grant` | 1 / 0 |\n| M5 | Offer request carries wrong pack address | compose `a-whole-exclusive-handoff-runs-on-a-file-backed-authority-test` | 12 / 0 |\n| M6 | Swap before/after meaning in content-store cut seam | compose-rows `the-wired-compositions-content-store-cuts-test` | 1 / 1 |\n| M7 | Compose driver config ignores source index | compose `fork-runs-when-selected-and-is-labelled-fork-test` and `the-source-index-is-read-from-the-composition-test` | 6 / 0 |\n| M8 | Report origin check bypassed, admitting an unfenced halted baseline | compose-rows `a-composed-authority-refuses-an-unfenced-result-report-test` | 2 / 0 |\n| M9 | Q3 record-address conjunct removed | rows-custody `the-install-response-is-verified-before-any-attachment` (F2) | 2 / 0 |\n\nAll eight requested mutants and the extra F2 mutant were killed. M6 is a mutation of the test substrate's cut interpreter, not production code: its identity assertion fails before the swapped absence also causes decode to error. The first mutation attempt used `-v` without `-n`, which made the runner load unrelated Python suites and fail on missing generated lexer classes; those runs were discarded, stored under `target/7c-mutants-unscoped/`, and are not counted above.\n\n## Deviations\n\n1. No production seam change was necessary: existing source-index, header, grant, fenced recovery, and authority contracts support meaningful composition tests as landed.\n2. Equal-baseline variant generation varies pack row order rather than task counter, because the root id is part of the baseline. Successor mismatch cases still re-emit changed tasks/custody. This follows code semantics rather than weakening authority comparisons.\n3. Added the source-index and unfenced-report controls to make those mutant obligations explicit. Preserved the existing plain-composition subprocess contract.\n4. Per the owner ruling, `yin.repl/main.cljc` is untouched. Its old semantic kernel means its `:custody` branch remains inert until phase 8; this is an accepted gap, not a live custody proof. The no-REPL test proves the standalone semantic-register composition.\n5. No commit, including for the initial F1/F2 step, per the brief's explicit instruction.\n\n## Design defects (separate list)\n\n- **No new production/design mismatch found.** The root-inclusive baseline and recovery-first publication order are honored by the tests.\n- **Inherited D1 disposition:** authority accepts row packs only; the old-codec authority assertions no longer describe a supported path and were removed while preserving direct old-codec coverage. Authority was not redirected to the old inspector.\n- **Accepted Q2 gap:** REPL custody stays inert until its phase-8 kernel switch.\n- **Existing Q3 boundary (7b F5):** install verification checks response self-consistency (parent/link id and manifest/derivation/image), while binding content to composition-known addresses remains linker's fetch/admission responsibility. No widened admission or resolver was introduced.\n\n## What 7d needs\n\nPort `stage_e_world` source and receiver builders to semantic-register/rows-support; supply `:source-index` to each composition; replace old body-key and pending accesses with row task/custody decoding. Use pack addresses for store and offers, root ids only inside baselines. Re-emit changed task/custody variants; vary pack row order when an equal-root alternative encoding is required. Keep recovery-v2-first crash-cut meanings and fenced lower's exporting gate.\n\nThen run the Stage E in-process slow cases on the specified three hosts and its separate JVM/Node process matrix after building peers. None of that Stage E host/process acceptance is claimed here. Only 7d's Stage E suite remains red in the full UCF quick lane.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '110,270p' collab/phase7-plan.md; rg -n '\"'^#{1,5} |Q3|Q4|three engines intended|backend|Stage [A-E]'\"' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '110,270p' collab/phase7-plan.md; rg -n '\"'^#{1,5} |Q3|Q4|three engines intended|backend|Stage [A-E]'\"' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\"","aggregated_output":"## 3. Design questions phase 7 forces, with recommendation, cost, and who decides\n\n### 3.1 Exclusive and holder custody on rows: which rows carry lease, fence and holder state\n\n**Finding.** The root row's slot 16 `custody` is plain data (draft §5.5 table). UCF 7.2.1 is operative for version 3: \"no body carries an epoch; the epoch is learned only from the grant binding\". The holder state the driver needs at run time (`:yin.k/custody` on the machine: occurrence, lease, holder, epoch, arbitration, next-op-seq, `:protection`, `:input {:next :prefix}`, `:tenure`) is built by the lower from the **grant evidence**, never from the body (`accept-grant!` today). The fence (op-ids) already has slots: `:put [stream V op-id]`, `:ffi-request [... op-id]`, `:link-request [... envelope op-id]`.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides).**\n- The root `custody` slot carries exactly the draft's table for `:blocked`/`:parked` exclusive roots: `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence O :yin.k/arbitration {...} :yin.k/next-op-seq n}` plus `:yin.k/origin` on a successor; a halted exclusive root carries `{:yin.k/origin ...}` only; children nil (as now). Lease, epoch, holder, enrolled set, protection classes: **never** in a row.\n- `lift-task` gains a `:header` option (the same header `holder.export/prepare` takes today). With a header it writes the slot by kind, refuses `:op-seq-exhausted` at 2^52-1 on a blocked or parked root, `:unprotected-pending` for a retained `:put`/`:ffi-request`/`:link-request` on an enrolled target without an op-id (root or child), and `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream for an op-id on an unenrolled target; a halted root with a header but no origin is refused (not downgraded). These are the version-1 rules, moved (`handoff.cljc:623-657`, `:1513-1523`).\n- `lower-task` replaces `check-custody!` (nil only) by the version-1 custody stage: fork root under `:exclusive` → `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; exclusive root of kind `:blocked`/`:parked` requires `:grant` + `:protection` opts and runs the seven grant checks of `accept-grant!` (`handoff.cljc:3343-3420`, ported verbatim, keyed off the slot instead of body keys); every marker's protection class must match enrollment in both directions; op-id presence must equal enrollment per retained write; the restored machine gets `:yin.k/custody` from the evidence and `:yin.k/next-op-seq` from the slot **exactly** (no max); a halted exclusive root lowers with no grant, gated `:ended`. A `:fenced` option (recovery, §3.2) skips the grant and gates `:exporting`.\n- The custody stage keeps its place: all four stages over the whole tree before the first `attach!` (continuation §7.2, codex F1/F2/F4/F7).\n\n**Fork spelling (OWNER decides, §3.10 Q1).** Phase 6 writes `custody nil` for a fork root; the draft's table spells `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}` on blocked/parked fork roots and nil on halted ones. Both decide the policy from the root alone. Nil is landed and every pinned root id in `rows_test` and the harness depends on it; the explicit spelling costs about half a day of pin regeneration and makes the slot's reader one `case` on `:yin.k/policy`. I recommend **keeping nil** and amending the draft's table (fork = nil in every row), on the minimal-diff principle; the owner may prefer the draft's spelling.\n\n**Cost.** 1.5 days in 7a (lift option, three refusals, the ported grant checks, the protection stage, tests on three hosts).\n\n### 3.2 The export-recovery object re-based on rows\n\n**Finding.** Version 1 (`holder.export/freeze`) stores two bodies: the published body and a \"snapshot\" lifted with `::recovery true` (full census), because version-0 bodies omitted transitive module stores and the recovery must rehydrate everything. It also stores `:yin.k/header`, `:yin.k/descriptors` (the served set) and `:yin.k/state` (`gate`, `origins`, `issues` per wait, `issued`, children recursively). `rehydrate-fenced` restores the snapshot with `rehydrate-fenced-body` (gate `:exporting`, waits in `:yin.k/recovery-waits`, parked table emptied into the record, `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids` mapping resource ids back to carrier cursor cells), re-prepares from the retained served table and proves the published bytes are reproduced.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides).** Version 2:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/export-recovery true\n :yin.k/version         2\n :yin.k/pack-address    a        ; the pack's own address (§3.3)\n :yin.k/root            B        ; the root id inside it\n :yin.k/pack-bytes      base64\n :yin.k/header          h        ; nil for a fork\n :yin.k/descriptors     #{...}   ; the served set, as today\n :yin.k/state           {:gate g :origins n :issued n :issues [..] :children {m state}}}\n```\n\nOne body: the pack already carries the whole root store, every parked record and the module stores by reachability (continuation §2.5), so the \"snapshot extends body\" check and the fixed-point repair disappear. `rehydrate-fenced` = decode, verify `pack-address` and `root`, `lower-task` with `{:fenced true :header h}` (no grant, gate `:exporting`, waits moved to `:yin.k/recovery-waits`, parked records moved into the record, `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids` from the census), then `prepare` from the retained served table and `encode` must reproduce `pack-bytes` exactly. Engine-local state that is not canonical content (`issues`, `issued`, `origins`, `gate`) stays in `:yin.k/state` as today; `:yin.k/issue` on lowered `:put` waits is assigned sequentially in carried order (what `resume-task*` does, `handoff.cljc:3643-3650`) and overwritten from `:issues` on rehydration. The recovery address stays the BLAKE3 of the recovery bytes.\n\n**Cost.** 1.5 days in 7b (export.cljc rewrite, `:fenced` lower mode, export_test re-based).\n\n### 3.3 Checkpoint and ledger embedding of a row body root\n\n**Finding.** The authority stores the body bytes under `:yin.k/id` with `(:put-bytes-fn store)`, and both `dao.jing.mem` and `dao.jing.file` **refuse a put whose bytes do not hash to the address** (`mem.cljc:38`, `file.cljc:351`). The pack `{:yin.k/handoff true :yin.k/version 3 :yin.k/root B :yin.k/rows [...]}` does not hash to `B`. Further, `emit` builds `:yin.k/rows` as `(vec (vals rows))` of a hash map, so the pack's byte order is host-dependent today; `B` is not.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides; the draft already allows it, §10 answer 2: \"when a pack is addressed it gets its own address, distinct from the semantic root id\").**\n- `emit` sorts `:yin.k/rows` by row id (bytewise). One root → one pack on every host. Root ids do not change, so no pin moves.\n- Two identities, each used where it belongs: the **pack address** `a = BLAKE3(pack bytes)` is the storage key and the ledger's `:yin.k/id` (a snapshot variant is an encoding, as today); the **root id** `B` is the semantic identity and enters the baseline as `:yin.k/root`. Two packs with one root are variants of equal baseline (impossible after sorting, but harmless). The offer request, the fence record and the report's `:yin.k/result` carry `a`; `offer-decision`'s `(jing/segment-address? (:yin.k/id fact))` holds for `a`.\n- The **rows inspector** (`yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint/inspect address bytes`): address matches bytes (`jing/segment-bytes-match?`), bytes decode through `rows/decode` (every row hash, canonical numbering, frames), the root's custody slot by kind (the version-1 header rules of `checkpoint.cljc:183-232` on the slot's map; children nil), every op-id on `:put`/`:ffi-request`/`:link-request` pendings in root and children against the root's occurrence, origin and counter (`check-op-id!` verbatim), duplicate ids refused, and the baseline `{:yin.k/kind :yin.k/root :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/origin :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq :yin.k/ops {op-id digest}}` where the intent digest is `BLAKE3(cbor [:yin.k/append target-identity payload-slot])` with the payload **as the wire slot holds it** (`[:lit x]` or `[:row id]`, both canonical). A fork pack answers `:yin.k/fork true` as `inspect-v2` does. Every refusal is data; nothing throws (as today).\n- `admission/accepted-baseline` and `completion/successor-defect` read `:yin.k/next-op-seq` and `:yin.k/origin` from the baseline, unchanged. `ledger/offer-defect` is unchanged (`:yin.k/kind` ∈ `#{:blocked :parked}`, the baseline compared whole).\n- Halted exit: the report's `:yin.k/result` is the halted pack's address; the terminal edge names it.\n\n**Cost.** 1.5 days in 7a (inspector, sorted emit, fixtures regenerated, the three authority call sites, `ledger-v2.txt`).\n\n### 3.4 Crash cuts: which cuts are meaningful when the body is a Merkle row set\n\n**Finding.** The named cuts (`stage_e_world/cuts`) cut the progress journal around mint, serve, store intent/ack, two content-store puts (recovery first, body second), fence, offer; the authority's commit; the reply journal; and composition ticks. They are meaningful at the granularity of **one store put per object**.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides).** Store the pack as **one** content-store object. Then every cut keeps its meaning: `:export/store-put-*` cuts the recovery v2 put, `:export/body-put-*` the pack put, and a pack is either whole or absent; a torn pack is impossible by the store's atomic insert. Storing rows individually (one object per row, Merkle-addressed) would make partial checkpoints possible and require a new cut family (`:pack/missing-row-before-fence`) and a new refusal path (`rows/decode` `:missing-row` at the authority), with no benefit in phase 7. Row-level storage is a phase-8+ option for deduplication across checkpoints and is out of scope. One new cut is worth adding cheaply: `:export/pack-put-after-recovery-missing` is already covered by `store-put-before` (recovery absent, nothing fenced); no addition needed.\n\n**Cost.** None beyond keeping the two-put order in `export-fence-step`.\n\n### 3.5 Publication of successors\n\n**Finding.** The protocol (`exit-mint` → prepare → freeze → fence → report → release → closure; the successor offer refused `:awaiting-completion` then admitted) is body-agnostic except for three values: the successor header (built from the machine's `:yin.k/custody`), the report's result address and the stored bytes.\n\n**Recommendation (decided).** No protocol change. The successor's header takes `:yin.k/next-op-seq` from the machine's custody map as today; the lift writes it into the slot; the inspector's `:counter-regression` check compares slot against baseline. The report carries the successor's pack address; `reopen-exit` rebuilds from the journaled mint and the fence as today. The one wrinkle: `reopen-holder` and `rebuilt-cell` read the kind and occurrence from the stored bytes through the inspector; the rows inspector answers the same keys.\n\n**Cost.** Included in 7b.\n\n### 3.6 Authority and capability tokens versus sealed refs on rows\n\n**Finding.** Rows re-seal every stream and cursor-ref under the receiver's owner on lower (`engine/issue-ref`), authenticate refs on lift, and carry no credential (UCF 7.7.8: \"the envelope carries no credential; the holder is authenticated by the attribution the composition supplies\"). The owner ruled (2026-10-12) that ShiBi tokens are a configurable policy at the `:admit` seam, and (2026-09-27) that ShiBi is a tuple space emerging from index and query interpreters, with `dao.stream.remote` offering only a capability-agnostic seam.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides; owner to confirm scope).** Phase 7 carries **no** capability token in any row and adds no row kind for one. Authority is ledger facts, attribution is the composition's resolver, tenure is lease evidence; sealed refs are the receiver's and are minted on lower. A token-bearing admission is ShiBi work at the admission seam, after phase 8.\n\n**Cost.** None.\n\n### 3.7 `:resume-mode` under version 2\n\n**Finding.** The owner's ruling (orchestrator log, 2026-10-11) is that the version-2 stack lane stays frozen and does not carry `:resume-mode`. Rows carry `:act` and `:tail` rows; a `:tail` is the register kernel's `:return-result` delivery and an `:act` the `:write-result` one (continuation §4.2, §4.3), so the information `:resume-mode` encodes is structural in version 3.\n\n**Recommendation (decided).** No work. Phase 7 does not touch version 2; version 3 needs no such key.\n\n### 3.8 Two gaps the brief did not list but phase 7 meets\n\n- **Continuation §11 Q4 (op-id rules on lower).** Closed by the rows inspector running inside `lower-task`'s custody stage for exclusive roots (below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent without origin, never the body's own occurrence). Fork roots refuse any op-id (`:fork-op-id`), as `inspect-v2` does. Decided, in 7a.\n- **Continuation §11 Q3 (install verification).** `check-install!` verifies status, manifest name, exports and that the image loads, not link-id = parent nor image address = the manifest's. Adding both is about a quarter day in 7b. **OWNER decides** (§3.10 Q3); recommend yes, because the recovery object now trusts the pack for install children.\n- **Continuation §11 Q1/Q10 (the `:running` wire phase).** Unchanged by phase 7; Stage E's installer scenarios use phase `:parked` only. Leave open.\n\n### 3.9 Which kernels take part\n\nStage E runs the **semantic-register kernel only** (the reference lane). The de Bruijn kernels are covered by the cross-engine laws of the harness (61/350, accepted) and are not Stage E holders or sources; the holder driver never lowers through a layout. This follows the owner's matrix ruling and avoids three times the process matrix.\n\n### 3.10 The owner's questions (blocking)\n\n- **Q1, fork spelling in the custody slot.** nil (landed; recommended) or the draft's `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}` (half a day of pin regeneration; the draft is amended either way).\n- **Q2, the REPL custody gap.** `yin.repl` composes the **old** `yin.vm.semantic` kernel (`repl.cljc:144`) and wires `compose` (`repl/main.cljc:979-1011`). After phase 7 the driver reads only packs, so the REPL's custody path cannot lift its machine until phase 8 renames the semantic-register kernel into `yin.vm.semantic`. Options: (a) accept the gap until 8 (recommended: `the-plain-composition-loads-and-drives-with-no-yin-repl-test` is re-based on a semantic-register receiver, and the REPL's `:custody` branch is marked inert until 8); (b) pull the REPL's kernel switch into phase 7 (an extra slice, two to three days, touching `yin.repl` and its tests, which 4b/5 did not plan).\n- **Q3, the install checks of continuation §11 Q3** in 7b (recommended yes, a quarter day).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Slicing\n\nEach slice: files, dependencies, acceptance tests, hosts, days (opus implementation + review rounds at the observed 2-4 codex rounds per slice, about half a day per round including fixes).\n\n### 7a: custody on rows, the rows inspector, the authority's fixtures\n\n**Create.** `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows/checkpoint.cljc` (the inspector of §3.3); `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_support.cljc` (semantic-register machine builders mirroring `lift_support`: `new-machine` over `sr/create-vm` with `tu/make-stream`, deterministic one-slot streams, `parked-reader`, `parked-writer`, `parked-explicit`, `parked-installer`, `parked-linker`, `halted-machine`, `halted-with-cursors`, `parked-module-store-cursors`, `with-op-id`, `serve-table`, `header`, `source-index` capture through `linearize/project`); `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc` + `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` (the five bases lifted by `rows/lift-task` under a header, the mutations as raw row edits with rehash where they touch a row and as root-slot edits otherwise, three anchors with full pack bytes); `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_test.cljc`; `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc` (the lift and lower custody rules of §3.1: header by kind, the three lift refusals, the seven grant checks with zero attachments, protection in both directions, exact counter restore, halted exclusive lowers ended, fork under `:exclusive` refused).\n\n**Change.** `rows.cljc` (`emit` sorts rows by id; `lift-task` `:header`; `lower-task` custody stage with `:grant`/`:protection`/`:exclusive`/`:fenced` opts; `check-custody!` replaced); `authority/grant.cljc`, `authority/completion.cljc`, `authority/admission.cljc` (inspector call); `ledger.cljc` (none expected; `:yin.k/root` in the baseline is opaque to the fold); `ledger_fixtures.cljc` (`park`/`halted` from rows packs; `path` → `ledger-v2.txt`), `ledger_fixture_test`, `crash_cut_test`, `grant_test`, `completion_test`, `inherited_test`, `front_test` (fixture bodies only).\n\n**Depends on.** Nothing; owner's Q1.\n\n**Acceptance.** `rows_checkpoint_test` (every base accepted with its baseline; every mutation refused at its path; the file equals `render` on three hosts), `rows_custody_test`, `ledger_fixture_test` (every host rebuilds every frame byte for byte; the projection digest pinned), `crash_cut_test`, the eight authority tests green, `rows_test` and the safepoint harness unchanged (no root id moves), `store_write_audit_test` allowlist updated if the row table changes.\n\n**Hosts.** JVM, Node, Dart.\n\n**Days.** 2.5 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) = **4**.\n\n### 7b: the holder's export record, recovery v2, the driver's lower\n\n**Change.** `holder/export.cljc` (`prepare`/`encode` over `rows/lift-task` with `:header` and a `:serve-keyed` adaptor; `freeze` → recovery v2; `rehydrate-fenced` over `lower-task {:fenced true}`); `holder/driver.cljc` (`lower`, `step-validating`, `rebuilt-cell`, `reopen-holder`, `reopen-exit`, `source-mint`/`exit-mint` unchanged in shape; `:export-version` removed); `compose.cljc` (`holder-config-keys`/`driver-config` drop `:export-version`); `holder/export_test`, `holder/driver_test`, `holder/writer_test`, `holder/reader_test` (machines from `rows_support`; rec1 rows replaced; the v2 fork-lift deftest deleted); `handoff_v2_holder_test` (one deftest deleted).\n\n**Depends on.** 7a; owner's Q3 for the install checks (a quarter day inside this slice).\n\n**Acceptance.** `export_test` (recovery v2 freezes and rehydrates fenced with zero program IO, the published pack reproduced byte for byte, aliases and distinct cursor cells preserved, install children rehydrated without initialization or IO, a restarted abort refused), `driver_test` whole (117 deftests), `writer_test`, `reader_test`, `evidence_test`; the law \"zero attachments before the custody stage refuses\" pinned by attach counts.\n\n**Hosts.** JVM, Node, Dart.\n\n**Days.** 3 implementation + 2 review (4 rounds; the driver is 2.9k lines and its tests 4.3k) = **5**.\n\n### 7c: the composition and the compose and fencing rows\n\n**Change.** `fencing_rows_test` (rows lift under header, `lower-task` with grant opts), `compose_test` (semantic-register `:receiver`/`:machine`; the plain-composition test per Q2), `compose_rows_test` (41 deftests; `offer-variant`-style helpers re-emit packs; content-store cuts see recovery v2 then the pack); `yin.repl/main.cljc` only if Q2 answers (b).\n\n**Depends on.** 7b; owner's Q2.\n\n**Acceptance.** All three files green on three hosts; the slow deftest `completion-recovery-unknown-policy-transaction-is-reconciled-once-test` under `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1` on each lane.\n\n**Days.** 1.5 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) = **3**.\n\n### 7d: Stage E on the semantic-register kernel, three hosts, four process pairs\n\n**Change.** `stage_e_world.cljc` (`parked-reader-over`, `reading-writer` over `rows_support`; `holder-seams` supplies `:source-index`; `offer-variant!` re-emits; `snapshot`'s `:fences` reads pack digests), `stage_e_scenarios.cljc` (checks that name `:yin.k/id-counter` or body keys adjusted; row 1's \"two encodings\" assertion keeps its meaning), `stage_e_peer.cljc` (none expected), `stage_e_process_test.clj` (none expected; the Node peer build picks up the new namespaces), `stage_e_rows_test.cljc` (none expected).\n\n**Depends on.** 7c (the world port can begin on 7b and run in-process while 7c is in review).\n\n**Acceptance.** `stage_e_rows_test` on JVM, Node and Dart (slow tier, `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`); `stage_e_process_test` on the JVM over `[[:jvm :jvm] [:jvm :node] [:node :jvm] [:node :node]]`, every life its own process, every cut a SIGKILL, `target/stage-e-durability.edn` appended; the fast-lane gate `the-cross-host-handoff-across-processes-test` first. Row 10's canonical-byte agreement across hosts now holds for packs as well (sorted rows).\n\n**Hosts.** JVM, Node, Dart (in-process); JVM↔Node (processes).\n\n**Days.** 2 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) + 0.5 for the slow matrix runs (the process matrix is about an hour per full pass on the remote hosts, per build-n-test) = **4**.\n\n### 7e: documentation\n\n**Change.** `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §2.5, §2.6 (custody slot by kind), §7.1-7.2 (the `:header` lift, the custody and fenced lower stages), §8.4 (phase 7 met), §10 (a 10.7 inventory), §11 (Q3, Q4 closed; Q1/Q10 left); `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` §5.5 (the custody table as ruled), §7 (pack address versus root id, sorted rows), §9 (the fenced lower); `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` 7.7.4 (recovery v2 paragraph replacing the version-1 one), 7.11.1 clauses 1-5 and 10 (stage letters updated); `docs/orchestrator-log.md` entry.\n\n**Depends on.** The owner's answers; concurrent with 7c/7d.\n\n**Days.** 1 + 1 review round = **1.5**.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Risks and what could force a redesign\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:1:# UCF transported state as 