er-vm.continuation.md:2585:- **6c Q3, closures in the free environment (information; a decision\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md:2594:## 12. Portability notes for the implementer\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1:## §7. The Universal Continuation Format (Proposed)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:25:# Universal Continuation Format — protocol draft\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:89:## §7.1 Stance: a continuation is a value, not a session\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:128:## §7.2 Vocabulary and envelope\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:185:### 7.2.1 The handoff body: the task wire grammar, versions 0 and 1\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:366:## §7.3 Code identity — content-addressed semantic profiles (blocker 5)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:368:### 7.3.1 The problem restated as data\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:374:### 7.3.2 The canonical instruction vector and its address\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:502:### 7.3.3 The contract stamp\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:562:### 7.3.4 References to code inside a continuation\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:644:## §7.4 Safepoints — where a canonical configuration exists (blocker 1)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:646:### 7.4.1 Only safepoints lift, and the table is the machine's\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:752:### 7.4.2 Safepoint maps for foreign engines: static facts only\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:812:### 7.4.3 The pending wait travels as data — complete, and resumable elsewhere\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1122:## §7.5 Recursive portable encoding (blocker 2)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1124:### 7.5.1 A disjoint tagged grammar\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1197:backends split into named kernels (`:yin.ast/code`, `:yin.semantic/code`)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1264:### 7.5.2 Primitives: a name is a binding, not a meaning\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1327:### 7.5.3 Sharing, cursor cells, and cycles\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1433:### 7.5.4 Failure is total and names its place\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1458:## §7.6 Dependency closure and context (blocker 3)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1460:### 7.6.1 Declared, not hand-written — and discovered conservatively\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1480:#### 7.6.1a `:yin.k/trees`: the source association (amendment 2026-10-11)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1571:### 7.6.2 The store slice, restored as an isolated store\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1642:### 7.6.3 The migration unit is one task\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1674:### 7.6.4 Modules\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1687:### 7.6.5 The satisfaction check\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1715:## §7.7 Ownership arbitration — custody as a lease over datoms (blocker 4)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1717:### 7.7.1 The race, named\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1732:### 7.7.2 Custody facts, and their dispatch keys\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1811:### 7.7.3 The authority: what the grantor possesses\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1857:### 7.7.4 The exporting state: fencing the source before publication\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2039:### 7.7.5 Fencing effects: epochs, operation ids, and what exactly-once costs\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2128:### 7.7.6 Successors and chains\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2155:### 7.7.7 Restart\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2193:### 7.7.8 Fenced custody, version 1: sequence, envelope, epoch binding\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2587:## §7.8 Lifecycle: lift on park, lower on resume\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2649:## §7.9 Outcome algebra\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2803:## §7.10 Invariant compliance\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2823:## §7.11 Acceptance blockers for the implementation phase\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2900:### Holder driver scheduling and retained inboxes (D15a)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2993:### 7.11.1 Blocker-closure acceptance matrix\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:3055:  refusal case. Separately prepare the three engines intended to\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '299,380p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; sed -n '1450,1675p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; sed -n '1999,2013p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; sed -n '2429,2571p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; sed -n '362,467p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '688,708p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '800,892p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '299,380p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; sed -n '1450,1675p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; sed -n '1999,2013p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; sed -n '2429,2571p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; sed -n '362,467p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '688,708p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '800,892p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md\"","aggregated_output":"### 2.5 Captures, parked records, module contexts, the parked table\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §2.4; evaluator §1.6; draft §5.1, §5.4).\n\n- A **reified continuation** value is `[:row B]`, `B` a `:reified\n  [act-cref]` row; the act has no pending, its `pc−1` is a\n  `:current-continuation`, `rd` is that instruction's destination, the\n  window is `saved(pc, rd)`.\n- The **parked table** is `[:parked-table P₁ state-cref₁ P₂ state-cref₂ …]`\n  sorted by `P`; a state is an `:act` whose `pc−1` is `:park` (an\n  explicit park), **or** FFI bookkeeping under a call id `P` that a live\n  `:ffi`/`:ffi-request` pending of the same task carries: an `:act` whose\n  `pc−1` is `:ffi-call`, or a `:tail` whose site is the parking tail\n  `:call`. An active `parked-id` never selects a bookkeeping record (v2\n  amendment §7.3, kept).\n- A **module context** is `E`'s `store-of`; the module store travels as\n  `[:module-store M store-cref]`; every `store-of` resolves to a carried\n  module store (draft §8 step 3).\n- **Install children** are whole `:task` rows in the child role (draft\n  §5.4, §5.5), unchanged.\n\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13), what travels and what does not**\n(engineer deviations 4, 6, 7; codex round 1 accepted all three):\n\n- **The store slice is the whole root store.** `lift-task` walks every\n  entry of `(:store m)` (`store-node`), and every parked record travels\n  (`parked-table` over all of `(:parked m)`), each judged by P4. No\n  reachability pruning of the store is performed. *Why:* the kernel\n  holds no liveness information for store entries; the conservative\n  slice is correct and the draft's \"exactly the reachable records\" was\n  never implemented.\n- **Module stores travel by reachability.** A `:module-store` row is\n  emitted for every `store-of` an `E` or closure names, transitively\n  through the values of stores already discovered (`module-store-nodes`);\n  a named store the kernel lacks is `:yin.k/non-portable\n  :missing-module-store`.\n- **Linked registry entries do not travel.** The row table has no slot\n  for the receiver's module registry; only module stores do. A name a\n  carried closure resolves through the source's registry is a\n  **composition prerequisite** of the receiver: row 17's receiver is\n  given the source's registry value, and without it the relift refuses\n  `:yin.k/unsatisfied {:obligations #{host.mod/f}}`. The receiver's\n  registry is kept as given; there is no isolated-receiver pruning.\n- **Custody is nil at the root in phase 6.** The lift emits `custody nil`\n  (fork), and the lower refuses any non-nil custody as `:yin.k/unsupported\n  :custody`; the exclusive and holder path is phase 7 (§8.4).\n\n### 2.6 The root (replacing draft §5.5 in two slots)\n\n**PROPOSED**, with the reviewer's correction of the free environment:\n\n```clojure\n[T :task\n   version          ; 3\n   contract         ; {:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}\n   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n   role             ; :root | :child\n   id-counter       ; N\n   free-env-cref    ; NEW: a :bindings row, the task's initial free environment (§6.3)\n   store-cref\n   module-stores    ; crefs sorted by M\n   parked-cref      ; :parked-table\n   cursor-cells-cref       ; :cursor-cells census\n   requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map plus :yin.k/trees (§6.1)\n   code             ; plain: {A canonical-vector …}, \"v4\" vectors verified to hash to their key\n   installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, in wait order     (:blocked, :parked)\n   parked-id        ; P or nil                                (:parked only)\n   result           ; V or nil                                (:halted only)\n   custody]         ; plain, draft §5.5's table\n```\n\nNo `layout` slot and no native image: those were the positional\nprofiles' (v2 amendment §2, §4.1). The custody table of the draft §5.5\nstands, with its version-0 comparison reworded (§10.3 R10).\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13):** of that table only the fork rows are\nreachable in phase 6; the lift writes `custody nil` and the lower's step\n10 (`check-custody!`) refuses any non-nil value `:yin.k/unsupported\n:custody` (§2.5, §7.2). *Why:* the exclusive and holder path is phase\n7's; carrying a custody arm no reader admits would be an untested slot.\n\n### 2.7 The bijection with the keyed frame\n### 7.1 Lift\n\n**DECIDED** (draft §9, UCF §7.8): refuse unless quiescent; refuse the\nholds (`unliftable-hold`, `handoff.cljc:588`) as `:yin.k/non-portable\n:reason-mismatch`; classify kind; run the **dependency closure over the\n`\"v4\"` vectors**, which needs the phase-5 scanners\n(`scan/requirements`, `scan/free-names`, `scan/free-occurrences` over the\n§2.3 table: the current `completion/code-facts` reads stack-table\npositions and cannot be used), and refuse `:yin.k/unsatisfied` unless\n`:complete`, `:yin.k/trees` included; number cursor cells (draft §6); walk\nwaits, parked records, the store slice, module stores, the free\nenvironment (from the kernel's `:free-env` field or the lift's explicit\n`:free-env` argument, §6.3, never from a current `E`), the result,\ninstall children; run `frame/defect` over every\nframe; emit rows and the census. A de Bruijn source walks the frames\n`layout/lift-act`/`lift-tail` reconstruct.\n\n**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), the lift from a de Bruijn kernel as\n`layout/lift-task` runs it (engineer deviation 2, codex: acceptable).**\nA de Bruijn source is not walked by a second lift: `layout/lift-task k\nspace template serve! opts` is `(rows/lift-task (canonical-task k space\ntemplate) serve! opts)`, where `canonical-task` presents the quiescent\nkernel as a semantic-register machine built on `template` (a fresh\nmachine of the kernel's composition), so the ten steps below run\nunchanged over it. `canonical-task`, in order: refuses\n`:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` when the kernel has an active continuation;\nrefuses `:yin.k/unsupported :installs` when it holds install children;\nrefuses `:yin.k/non-portable :free-env-value` for a closure or\ncontinuation in its `:free-env` (§6.3); then, under a lift context\n(`:kind` by `kernel-kind`, the kernel, the space, the kernel's\n`:free-env`, the template's owner), sets `:code` to `{A {:vector :address\n:analysis}}` over the space's segments with identity aliases, converts\nthe store, the module stores and the value through `canonical-value`,\neach parked record through `lift-state` (typed `:parked-continuation`\nwith its id) and each wait entry through `lift-state`, and carries\n`:resources`, `:capability-secret`, `:free-env` (also as `:env`),\n`:ready-queue`, `:id-counter`, `:halted?` and `:blocked?`. Any\n`:yin.k/non-portable :ambiguous-image` or `:unknown-image` of\n`image-segment` (§4.1), `:not-at-safepoint` of `safepoint!`,\n`:register-layout` of `virt` (§4.2), `:stack-layout`, `:stack-base`,\n`:frame-depth`, `:closure-origin` or `:closure-binders` is raised here,\nbefore `rows/lift-task` serves anything; the tests that pin\n`:ambiguous-image`, `:free-env-value` and `:not-at-safepoint` assert\nzero serves. *Why:* one carrier, one lift; the layout only\nreconstructs.\n\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13): the order as `lift-task*` runs it (B4),\nand the discharge rule.** The paragraph above put the dependency closure\nbefore the numbering and the walk, but the carried segments, contexts\nand module stores are known only once the walk has found them, so the\nclosure cannot run first. The merged order is:\n\n1. **Quiescence and safepoint.** Refuse `:yin.k/not-quiescent` on a\n   non-empty ready queue; refuse the holds (`unliftable-hold`: `:observe`,\n   `:held`, `:unminted-cursor`, `:pending-close`,\n   `:link-cursor-not-installed`) as `:yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch`;\n   refuse `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` when `:control` is set.\n2. **Kind.** `:parked` when the value is a parked continuation the parked\n   table holds, else `:blocked` on a non-empty wait set, else `:halted`\n   with an empty queue; anything else is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`.\n3. **Free environment** (§6.3): the option, else the kernel field, else\n   `:missing-free-env`.\n4. **The walk.** Waits (state and pending), the parked table, the whole\n   store, the result (halted only), install children (each a recursive\n   `lift-task*` in the child role with its own accumulator), then the\n   module stores by reachability. Every value is classified `[:lit x]`,\n   `[:row n]` or `[:cursor-cell source]`; references are authenticated\n   against the kernel's resource table before encoding\n   (`:forged-resource-reference`), a foreign-owned value is\n   `:foreign-value`, a heap cell `:cell`, a host object `:host-object`.\n   The walk records every **segment** reached and every **context**: a\n   closure's `(A, entry, E)`, an `act`'s `(A, pc, E)`, a return frame's\n   `(A, pc, E)`. A `tail` state contributes its `K` frames' contexts and\n   none for its own dead activation.\n5. **Code.** `{A vector}` for the segments reached, from the kernel's\n   code table through its aliases; an unaddressed segment is\n   `:yin.k/non-portable :unaddressed-segment`, an address the kernel\n   cannot produce a vector for is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`.\n6. **Dependency closure** (`discharge!`), **per context, over live\n   code.** For each recorded context `(A, at, E)`, the live pcs are\n   `(live-pcs v at)`: the closure of `at` under `analysis/successors`\n   plus, for every reachable `:closure` instruction, that closure's body\n   from its entry (reachable code can still construct it). Every free\n   occurrence (`scan/free-occurrences`) at a live pc must be **answered\n   in that context**, in `resolve-var`'s order: a reserved name; the\n   context's own `E`; the **one** store `engine/active-store` selects for\n   that `E` (the root store when `E` has no `store-of`, else the module\n   store it names, both of which travel); a profiled primitive (a\n   `:host`-class profile refuses `:yin.k/non-portable\n   :host-state-primitive` at that point, so a host primitive shadowed by\n   `E` or the store lifts); an installing module's export (`m/x` built\n   with `(symbol (name m) (name x))`); a module the registry resolves for\n   a namespaced name. Any unanswered name is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`,\n   `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`, `:yin.k/missing {:obligations #{…}}`.\n   *Why per context:* codex F3, the union of all module-store keys\n   answered names the running closure could not resolve. *Why live\n   code:* codex F6, a numeric or whole-body scan refused valid\n   continuations whose unreachable branches named a missing symbol.\n   The parser admits no backward jump (§3.1 of the frozen design), so no\n   carried vector exercises the backward edge; `live-pcs` is public and\n   is tested directly on a hand-built vector with a `:jump` to an earlier\n   pc. Discharge runs at lift only; the lower does not re-discharge free\n   names against the receiver (§7.2).\n7. **Trees** (§6.1): every carried segment has a non-empty entry in the\n   `:source-index` option, else `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with `:yin.k/trees`.\n8. **The task.** `requires` is `{:yin.k/cursor-profiles … :yin.k/segments\n   … :yin.k/trees …}`; the root is assembled with `custody nil` (§2.5).\n9. **Frames.** `validate-frames` over the assembled abstract task (X1,\n   every wait, parked state and reified value, children recursively); a\n   defect is `:yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state` carrying it. The\n   lift does not decode its own output a second time.\n10. **Emit** (`emit`): canonical cursor-cell numbering and rows.\n\n**Refusals are thrown.** Every refusal of lift and lower is an `ex-info`\nwhose ex-data carries `:yin.k/status`, a `:yin.k/kind` where one is\nnamed, and the defect's data, the same convention as `decode` and `emit`;\nnothing returns a `{:status …}` map (engineer deviation 2, accepted).\n\n### 7.2 Lower\n\n**DECIDED** (draft §8 in order; §3.3): gate, structure, graph, hashes,\ncanonical form, canonical numbering, **frames**, pending and operations,\ndependency closure (`requires`/`code` agree with the census;\n`:yin.k/trees` complete per §6.1), custody, composition, restore. Restore\nbuilds the receiving kernel's state: semantic-register: `code` through\n`load-vector`/`attach-image` under `code/contract`, the free environment\nas base `:env`, each `:wait` to an engine entry (payload plus the\npending's engine fields, `lower-frame!`'s shapes, `handoff.cljc:3182-3246`,\n`:deliver` carried), parked records into `:parked`, the result into\n`:value`; de Bruijn: derive and attach the image for each A, set\n`:free-env`, and `layout/lower-act`/`lower-tail` each frame (the stack\nlane's `tail` needs 6c-pre). Restoration delivers nothing, runs nothing,\nmints nothing.\n\n**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), the lower into a de Bruijn kernel as\n`layout/lower-task` runs it (engineer deviation 2, codex: acceptable;\nD4 by R6).** `layout/lower-task scratch k wire attach! opts`, in order:\n(i) `rows/decode` of the wire under the budget (steps 1-7 of the\ncarrier); (ii) `lower-defect` on the decoded task, judged by the\nreceiver's `kernel-kind`, **before any attach**, thrown as the carrier's\nrefusals are; (iii) `rows/lower-task` of the same wire into `scratch`, a\nfresh semantic-register machine of the same composition (the carrier's\nstages, attachment and restore, §7.2 above, unchanged); (iv)\n`kernel-task` of the restored machine into `k`. `lower-defect`'s clauses\nrun in this order and the first that holds is the refusal: two carried A\nwhose vectors derive one image of the receiver's kind,\n`:yin.k/unsupported :ambiguous-image {:yin.k/image ident :yin.k/segments\n#{A …}}` (§4.1; a vector that derives no image is left to the carrier's\nown checks); install children, `:yin.k/unsupported :installs`; a closure\nor continuation in the free environment, `:yin.k/unsupported\n:free-env-value {:yin.k/path …}` (§6.3); and, for the register kernel\nonly, a `:prim` row reachable through a wait's or a parked state's\nwindow, lexical binding, closure capture, reified state or guest\ncollection, `:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers {:yin.k/path\n…}` (§4.2). A primitive function is an admitted carrier value (`:prim`);\nthe restriction is the register kernel's, not the carrier's, and does not\nreach the free environment. `kernel-task` then builds the receiver: the\nspace of the machine's vectors (`machine-space`, with the map from the\nscratch machine's segment ids to A), every image derived and attached in\nA order (`attach-space`: `stack/attach-image` under `vm/stack-contract`\nor `register/attach-image` under `vm/register-contract`), and under a\nlower context over that space the store, module stores, parked records\n(`lower-state`, typed `:parked-continuation`), wait entries\n(`lower-state`) and the value converted through `positional-value`; the\nfree environment set as `:free-env`; `:resources`, `:id-counter`,\n`:halted?` and `:blocked?` carried, `:ready-queue` empty. It delivers\nnothing and runs nothing. The scratch machine's `:env` and\n`:capability-secret` are not read: refs verify only when the scratch\nreceiver and the kernel share a composition, which the harness\nguarantees and a production caller must. The two `assoc` sites of\n`canonical-task` and `kernel-task` are on the store-write audit's\nallowlist (`store_write_audit_test.clj`) with the reason of\n`rows/restore-task`'s entry: both copy a store whose every key already\npassed the one program-write gate and convert values only, so routing\nthem through `store-put` would re-check what the carrier's step checked.\n*Why:* restoring through the carrier's own `lower-task` reuses its\ntwelve steps and every validation rather than writing a second restore\n(engineer deviation 2); the defects a kernel cannot hold must be named\nbefore the first `attach!`, as every carrier defect is (§7.2 above;\ncodex M8, which moved `lower-defect` after `rows/lower-task`, is killed\nby the attach-count assertions).\n\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13): the lower's staged checks as\n`lower-task` runs them, and its refusal kinds.** Steps 1-7 are `decode`\n(gate, structure, graph, hashes, canonical form, numbering, frames,\n§3.3). Steps 8-10 then run as four stages, **each over the whole task\ntree (root and every install child, pre-order) before the next**, and\n**every one of them before the first `attach!`**; a defect in a child's\nlater stage therefore cannot be masked by the root's earlier one, and\nzero streams are attached on any refusal (codex F1, F2, F4, F7; mutants\nthat attach early are killed by the attach-count law):\n\n| Stage | Function | Checks, in order | Refusal |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| stores | `check-stores!` | no key of the root store or of any module store is a reserved name (`vm/reserved-name?`), refused here rather than by restore's `store-put` | `:yin.k/undecodable :reserved-name` (`:yin.k/role :store-key`) |\n| pendings and install records | `check-pendings!` | the task's role slot equals its position (`:root`/`:child`); every `:install` pending names a carried install row; kind consistency (`:halted` has no waits, `:blocked` has waits, `:parked` names a key of its parked table); then every install record by `check-install!` (§5.2: phase ∈ `#{:running :parked}`, the retained response and image contract, the child `:blocked`) | `:role`, `:incomplete-install`, `:kind`, `:parked-id`, `:install-phase`, `:install-response`, `:install-child`, all `:yin.k/undecodable` |\n| dependencies | `check-dependencies!` | `requires` is a map whose `:yin.k/segments` equals the key set of `code` (`:requires`); every segment has a non-empty `:yin.k/trees` entry; every `store-of` of every `:closure` and `:env` row of **this task** (children excluded, each checked in its own stage pass) names one of this task's `:module-store` rows (§2.6), so a parent's or sibling's store of the same key cannot answer (codex F7, F8); every vector attaches in the receiving kernel under its A (`attaches?`: `module/attach-module` into a throwaway copy, the result discarded) (`:segment`); every `:closure` row names a `:closure` tuple of its vector at its entry (`:closure-origin`) with the same binders (`:closure-binders`); every `:prim` names a primitive the receiver holds | `:yin.k/undecodable :requires`, `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with `:yin.k/trees` or **`:yin.k/stores`** (`:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`), `:yin.k/undecodable :segment`, `:closure-origin`, `:closure-binders`, `:yin.k/unsatisfied {:yin.k/name p}` |\n| custody | `check-custody!` | the root's custody slot is nil (phase 6 carries fork only) | `:yin.k/unsupported :custody` |\n\nThen **attachment** (step 11, `attach-streams!`): one `attach!` per\ndistinct stream identity of the whole tree, `:yin.k/unsatisfied\n{:dao.stream/identity …}` when the composition declines. Then **restore**\n(step 12, `restore-task`): the carried vectors attached by A into a\nreceiver emptied of code, so frame and closure addresses map through the\nreceiver's own aliases and no source-local segment number travels; fresh\nresource keys per stream identity and per cursor cell; values re-sealed\nunder the receiver's owner; waits in `lower-frame!`'s entry shapes, a\n`tail` with `{:deliver :return}` and no window, env or pc; parked records\nwith a cycle guard; the store through `engine/store-put`; module stores;\ninstall children spawned by the engine's spawn discipline and then\nrestored recursively (the spawn's initial control is replaced, no\n`vm/run`); the free environment as both `:env` and `:free-env`; the id\ncounter `max(local, carried)`. Restore meets no carrier defect, by\nconstruction of the stages above. The lower does **not** re-discharge\nfree names against the receiver: a name a closure resolves through the\nregistry is the composition prerequisite of §2.5, and the receiver's\nregistry is kept as given. The kinds `:role :custody :incomplete-install\n:kind :parked-id :requires :segment :install-phase :install-response\n:install-child :closure-origin :closure-binders` and the `:yin.k/stores`\ncensus are the engineer's and the fix rounds' naming; the earlier text\nnamed none of them (deviation 9, accepted with the requirement that every\ndefect be a named transport outcome before attachment).\n\n---\n\n## 8. Slices, gates, entry conditions\n### 8.4 Phase 7's entry conditions\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §10 phase 7). 6a-6c green on three hosts; the row\ncarrier the only writer and reader the semantic-register kernel has; a\ndocumented seam for the holder, checkpoint, ledger and authority paths\n(phase 7 wires it; phase 6 edits none of them); the Stage D/E scenarios\n(`stage_e_scenarios.cljc`, `checkpoint_fixtures.cljc`) listed with, for\neach, the §5.2 row that exercises its parking shape; the export-recovery\nobject (UCF §7.7.4, format version 1 over the old codec) re-based on the\nrow carrier by phase 7, since it embeds a body; and the free-environment\nretention seam (§6.3) either landed by the 4b track or the explicit\n`:free-env` argument threaded through the holder's export path.\n\n### 8.5 Ordering and concurrency\n\n### 10.6 Amendments after slice 6b (revision 4, 2026-10-13)\n\nEach item names the clause, what changed, and why; the clause itself\ncarries the same marker. Sources are the engineer report's design bugs\n(B1-B4) and deviations (1-10) and the three codex reviews' rulings.\n\n| # | Clause | What changed | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| B1 | §5.2 rows 10-12, the install paragraph; §8.2 item 3 | row 10 is child `:blocked`, phase `:parked`; row 11 is the `:running` wire phase set by hand on row 10's machine; the lower's install contract (phase set, response and image, child `:blocked`) is stated | `advance-install` retains a blocked child at `:parked` and links a halted or parked one in the same sweep; the promised states were unreachable |\n| B2 | §5.2 wrapper paragraph, row 21 | the mechanical wrapper rule has one stated exception: row 21 under `:depth-3` answers `[3 [2 [1 1]]]` | `nest`'s parameter is `x` and shadows the free `x` before `f` is created; the reference run itself answers 1 |\n| B3 | §5.2 refusal rows; §8.2 item 7 | the `:tail` fourth slot is `:row-shape` at structure step 2, and the tamper is a raw row edit with rehash | the row table fixes `:tail` at three slots before S1 runs; `emit` drops extra abstract slots silently |\n| B4 | §7.1 | the lift's order is quiescence, kind, free environment, walk, code, dependency closure, trees, task, frames, emit | segments and contexts are known only after the walk |\n| D1 | §6.1, §8.2 depends-on | `:source-index` is a lift input from the composition | the kernel holds no source index |\n| D2 | §7.1 | refusals are thrown `ex-info` | consistency with `decode`/`emit` |\n| D3 | §2.5, §2.6, §7.2 | custody nil at the root; non-nil is `:yin.k/unsupported :custody` at step 10 | exclusive/holder is phase 7 |\n| D4 | §2.5, §5.2 row 17 | linked registry entries do not travel; the receiver's registry is a composition prerequisite and is kept as given | no registry slot in the row table |\n| D5 | §7.1, §7.2 | discharge is per context over live code (`live-pcs`), answered by `E`, the one active store, primitive, installing export, registry; lift only | codex F3 (union over stores was unsound) and F6 (whole-body scan refused valid states) |\n| D6 | §2.5 | the store slice is the whole root store; every parked record travels; module stores by reachability | conservative and what the code does |\n| D7 | §6.3 | the retention seam landed; the lift reads the kernel's `free-env` field by default, the option overrides, E2 catches a disagreeing override | 4b stage 3 landed before 6b |\n| D8 | §7.2 | the lower's four stages, each across the whole tree before the next, all before attachment; the refusal-kind table including `:yin.k/stores` | codex F1, F2, F4, F7, F8: every carrier defect must be a named refusal before any attach |\n| D9 | §5.2 row 18 | the fixture's module store is symbol-keyed and hand-built | codex asked for the synthetic label |\n| D10 | §9 | 4b stage 2 landed; rows 3-4, 7-12, 17, 24 run; three-host gate still open | honesty |\n\n**Note on `handoff.cljc` (no edit).** The engineer report said\n`export-task` builds installing names as `(symbol (str m \"/\" x))`, a\nnil-namespace symbol that never equals `m/x`. Read at\n`handoff.cljc:1480-1488`, the code is `(symbol (str (name m) \"/\" (name\nx)))`. The one-argument `symbol` of Clojure, ClojureScript and the\npinned ClojureDart core splits its string at `/` into namespace and\nname, so this yields the qualified `host.mod/f` with namespace\n`\"host.mod\"`, equal to `'host.mod/f`; codex round 1 evaluated it on the\nJVM and found the same. The claim is withdrawn; the old form is not a\nbug. `rows.cljc`'s `installing-names` uses the two-argument `(symbol\n(name mn) (name x))`, which is equivalent. `handoff.cljc` is not edited\nby this phase (§8.5).\n\n### 10.7 Amendments after slice 6c (revision 5, 2026-10-13)\n\nEach item names the clause, what changed, and why; the clause itself\ncarries the marker \"**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13)**\". Sources are the\nengineer report's deviations (1-8) and design defects (D1-D5), the codex\nreview (F1-F6, D1-D5) and the Architect's rulings (R1-R6), whose\nwording is applied verbatim where a ruling says so.\n\n| # | Clause | What changed | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| R1 | §4.1 | A stays the identity under image collision; a de Bruijn receiver refuses `:yin.k/unsupported :ambiguous-image` before attach, a de Bruijn source refuses `:yin.k/non-portable :ambiguous-image` before serving; no enumeration order selects an A; the harness scopes `kernel-space` to the vectors a world projected | codex F2: `(fn [x] x)` and `(fn [y] y)` have distinct A, one R and one H; both kernels dedupe attaches by checksum; editing their offset tables for a phase-8 deletion was not worth a slice |\n| A1 | §4.1 | the public API as landed: `segment`, `space`, `lift-state`, `lower-state`, `canonical-task`, `kernel-task`, `lower-defect`, `lift-task`, `lower-task` and the rest; `lift-act`/`lift-tail`/`lower-act`/`lower-tail` superseded | engineer deviation 1, accepted by codex: one dispatching entry per direction |\n| R5 | §4.2, §8.3 item 12 | the unique-preimage rule for `virt`: live-out* first, else the sole named preimage for validation only, else `:yin.k/non-portable :register-layout`; item 12 permits that refusal for an ambiguous tamper | engineer deviation 7, codex D3 and M9 (take the first preimage survived) |\n| D4 | §4.2, §7.2, §8.3 item 6 | a primitive is an admitted carrier value; the register kernel refuses one in its payload `:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers` with the path before attach; \"is no carrier value\" withdrawn; four refusal fixtures | engineer deviation 8, codex D4: the limit is the receiver's, not the carrier's |\n| S1 | §4.3 | 6c-pre landed; the tail lowering shapes stated as `lower-state` emits them | the seam was a specification; it is code |\n| D2 | §4.3 | the retained-FFI response-pair seam specified as slice 6c-post-D2, gated by the owner, not a prerequisite of 6c; `unlowerable` empties and the counts rise to 366/96 when it lands | engineer D2, codex probe 3: the kernels' re-park names their own call pair; a kernel edit that dies at phase 8 |\n| D5 | §5.2 row 21 | the expectation on every lane: `E` binds `x` to 1 under `:depth-3`, the free environment keeps 99, pinned separately | codex D5; the cross-engine lanes could have hidden a lane agreeing on one and not the other |\n| R2 | §5.3, §5.6 item 8, §8.3 items 10-11 | the native lanes run the shared fragment: 61 law-4 cases over three lanes, 350 law-5 transfers (92/16 in the quick tier); tail acceptance rows 6 and 20 on the native lanes; the reference lane keeps 89/534 | engineer deviations 3-4, codex F3; link and install rows need 2-3 days of code for kernels deleted at phase 8; ACCEPTED by the owner on 2026-10-13 (6c Q1) |\n| D1 | §5.5 | positions carry identities; the renaming operation, injective correspondence, separate descriptor equivalence and five negative controls; three distinct lanes asserted per case | engineer D1, codex F4, M11 and M12 survived, probe 4 |\n| R3 | §6.3, §7.2 | a closure or continuation in the free environment is refused on the native lanes both ways (`:free-env-value`), admitted by the carrier and the reference lane; nested values included | codex F1: no finite canonical form through the shared free environment; refuse, do not convert |\n| L1 | §7.1 | the lift from a de Bruijn kernel is `rows/lift-task` over `canonical-task`; the refusals it raises before serving | engineer deviation 2 |\n| L2 | §7.2 | the lower is decode, `lower-defect` (collision, installs, free-env procedure, register primitives, in that order, before any attach), `rows/lower-task` into a scratch machine, `kernel-task`; the audit allowlist entries | engineer deviation 2; codex M8; the audit's precedent of `restore-task` |\n| T1 | §8.3 items 14-17 | the fix round's tests named as acceptance | R1, R3, R4, F6, the audit |\n| C1 | §8.5 | 6c edited no kernel; 6c-post-D2 is the one named kernel edit, its own slice | honesty |\n| V1 | §9 | counts as asserted; no three-host record in the sources read; the limits and their shelf life | honesty |\n\n---\n\n## 11. Owner questions\n\nRevisions 2-3 left none: the reviewer accepted the engine-less profile\nand rejected the merged-`E` free environment. **Revision 4 (after 6b)\nlists the design choices the merged code made that no ruling covers.**\nEach is stated as the code behaves today; none is decided by this\ndocument.\n\n- **Q1, install children, the parked-child scenario.** The engine never\n  retains an install child at a quiescent safepoint except `:blocked` at\n  phase `:parked` (§5.2 B1). The carrier still admits phase `:running`\n  (never engine-reachable at a safepoint) and the lower refuses any\n  child kind but `:blocked`. Should the row table and both checks\n  narrow to the one reachable state (phase `:parked`, child `:blocked`),\n  or does the owner want a reachable parked-child install scenario,\n  which is an engine change (`advance-install`), with row 11 rewritten\n  to it?\n- **Q2, census agreement on lower.** The draft §8 step 9 requires the\n  carried `requires`/`code` to agree **exactly** with a census recomputed\n  from the rows (a declared segment not reached, or a reached segment\n  not declared, is `:yin.k/undecodable`). `check-dependencies!` checks\n  only that `:yin.k/segments` equals the key set of `code`; a vector\n  carried but referenced by no row is accepted and attached. Accept the\n  narrower check, or require the recomputed census?\n- **Q3, install response verification.** The draft §5.4 asks the\n  receiver to verify that the install's link id equals `parent`, that\n  the image hashes to the address it is delivered under and is the image\n  the manifest names, and that format, contract and the child's profile\n  agree. `check-install!` verifies status, manifest name, export shape\n  and that the image loads; not the link id, not the image's address\n  against the manifest. Accept the narrower contract for phase 6, or\n  extend it before phase 7 (which re-bases the export-recovery object)?\n- **Q4, operation ids and correlation on lower.** The draft §8 step 8's\n  op-id rules (below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent\n  without origin) and its correlation agreement (cursor-cell stream,\n  envelope ids, module names) are not implemented; op-ids travel\n  verbatim, and pending compatibility is only P2's reason check. With\n  fork-only custody there is no `next-op-seq`; is this phase 7's with\n  the custody arm, or a 6-era gap to close?\n- **Q5, no re-discharge on lower.** Free names are discharged at lift\n  only (§7.1); the lower checks carried segments, trees, store census,\n  closures and primitives, and a name a closure resolves through the\n  registry is a composition prerequisite (§2.5). A receiver lacking that\n  registry entry accepts the carrier and fails at run time\n  (`Unable to resolve symbol`). Accept, or require the lower to\n  re-discharge registry-answered names against the receiver's registry\n  before attachment?\n- **Q6, conservatism of the live-code rule.** `live-pcs` follows both\n  successor edges of every branch and enters every reachable closure\n  body, with no constant propagation. The harness's `(if false (fn []\n  ghost) (next-of c))` case transports only because the lift happens at\n  a safepoint **after** the branch (the resume pc is past it, so the\n  dead arm is unreachable by edges); the same program lifted at a\n  safepoint **before** the branch would refuse `ghost` although the arm\n  can never run. Is \"reachable by control-flow edges, never by value\"\n  the intended precision, accepting the false refusals it implies for\n  constant-dead code?\n- **Q7, host-class primitives behind a shadow.** `:host-state-primitive`\n  refuses only when the name reaches the primitive step of the answering\n  order; the same name bound in `E` or the active store lifts (codex\n  round 2 confirmed). Intended, or should a host-class primitive name\n  refuse regardless of shadowing?\n- **Q8, preflight attachment cost.** `attaches?` validates and analyses\n  every carried vector (and every install image) by attaching it into a\n  throwaway receiver, and restore attaches it again. Codex found it pure\n  and only duplicated CPU. Accept, or ask the kernel for a validate-only\n  entry point?\n- **Q9, Law 2 outside a fresh receiver.** The id counter is adopted as\n  `max(local, carried)`; a receiver whose construction mints more ids\n  than the carrier relifts to a different root (draft §9 already scopes\n  the law to the fresh-receiver context). No harness case has a\n  non-fresh receiver. Add one as a documented non-law, or leave it?\n- **Q10, the `:running` wire phase's future.** If Q1 narrows the phase\n  set, row 11 disappears from the matrix and the `:install` row's phase\n  slot becomes a constant; if not, row 11 stays a synthetic case. Which?\n\n**Revision 5 (after 6c) adds the three questions the rulings left\n(`collab/1792360000000-architect-6c-rulings.report.md`); they are\nnumbered on their own, as 6c Q1-Q3, so the 6b numbering above stands.**\n### 5.4 Task context (content rows)\n\n| Kind | Slots |\n|---|---|\n| `:store` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …` sorted by key; the isolated slice of UCF §7.6.2 |\n| `:module-store` | `manifest-M  store-cref` |\n| `:parked-table` | `P₁ state-cref₁ P₂ state-cref₂ …` sorted by P (each state an `:act` or a `:tail`) |\n| `:install` | `name-S  phase  parent-D  response-D  child-cref` |\n| `:cursor-cells` | the census, §3 |\n| `:cursor-cell-body` | `stream-cref  position` (position in the `:dao.stream.remote/v1` portable cursor domain) |\n\nValidation of a parked state reads decoded task context, never an\nengine: the table key `P` and the set of call ids the task's live\n`:ffi`/`:ffi-request` pendings carry are the `task` input of\n`frame/defect` (continuation §3.1), under which an `:act` at a `:park`\nsite is an explicit park and an `:act` at an `:ffi-call` site or a\n`:tail` at a tail call under a live call id is FFI bookkeeping.\n\nResource entries never appear in `:store`; streams and cursor cells lower into\nthe receiver's private resource table beside the program store (linker\nM4, `yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3). `:module-store` rows are the per-module\nsnapshots of §7.6.2's M4 paragraph. The parked table holds exactly the\nreachable records. An `:install` row carries the complete entry of\n§7.4.3 r5: phase `:running` or `:parked`, parent link id (nil where the\ncontract permits), the response verbatim as structural data, and the\nchild as a cref to a whole `:task` row in the child role. \"Verified\"\nmeans verified by the **receiver**, never trusted because it decodes:\nthe response's manifest names the module the row is keyed by, its link\nid equals `parent`, its image is well formed, hashes to the address it\nis delivered under, is the image the manifest names, and its format,\ncontract and the child's profile agree (`yin.vm.linker.md` §5, §9;\namendment §2). A failure is `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the row.\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13; continuation §5.2 B1, §7.2):** the\nmerged lower (`rows/check-install!`) verifies, before any attachment:\nthe phase is one of `#{:running :parked}` (`:install-phase`); the\nresponse is a map with `:status :ok`, a `:manifest` whose\n`:yin.module/name` is the row's module and whose `:yin.module/exports`\nis a collection of symbols, and an `:image` map whose `:value` the\nreceiving kernel's loader admits (`:install-response`); and the carried\nchild task's kind is `:blocked` (`:install-child`), the only kind the\nengine retains at a safepoint (a halted or parked child is linked in the\nsweep it halts in, so phase `:running` is a wire phase the codec accepts\nbut the engine never exposes at a safepoint). The link-id-equals-parent\nand image-address-equals-manifest checks of the paragraph above are\n**not** implemented (continuation §11 Q3).\n\n### 5.5 The root\n\n```clojure\n[T :task\n   version          ; 3                       (gated first, §8)\n   contract         ; {:yin.code/contract \"v4\" :yin.k/version 1}, the one profile map (§2)\n   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n   role             ; :root | :child\n   id-counter       ; N                       (UCF §7.6.3)\n   free-env-cref    ; a :bindings row, the task's initial free environment (continuation §6.3)\n   store-cref\n   module-stores    ; crefs sorted by M\n   parked-cref      ; :parked-table\n   cursor-cells-cref       ; :cursor-cells census\n   requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map plus :yin.k/trees (continuation §6.1)\n   code             ; plain: {A canonical-vector …}, \"v4\" vectors verified to hash to their key\n   installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, IN WAIT ORDER   (:blocked, :parked)\n   parked-id        ; P or nil                              (:parked only)\n   result           ; V or nil                              (:halted only)\n   custody]         ; plain, see below\n```\n\n(Root replaced 2026-10-11 by continuation §2.6: `free-env-cref` added,\n`contract` is the one profile map, `code` holds `\"v4\"` canonical vectors,\n`requires` gains `:yin.k/trees`.) No `layout` slot and no native image:\nthose were the positional profiles' (v2 amendment §2, §4.1).\n\nKind rules are §7.2.1's: `:blocked` has nonempty frames and neither\nparked-id nor result; `:parked` has frames (possibly empty), a parked-id\nnaming a key of the parked table, no result; `:halted` has a result and\nneither frames nor parked-id. Frames restore in carried order and are\nnever collapsed to one; a task with an empty ready queue may hold several\nordered waits.\n\n`custody` is the custody arm carried as plain data, under the rules of\n§7.2.1 and amendment §3, by role, kind and policy:\n\n| role | kind | policy | `custody` |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| root | blocked / parked | exclusive | `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq}` plus `:yin.k/origin` on every successor, absent on a first export |\n| root | blocked / parked | fork | `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}` |\n| root | halted | exclusive | `{:yin.k/origin …}` only: a result is not a lease subject |\n| root | halted | fork | nil: a fork halt has no custody data and a first fork halt has no origin to carry |\n| child | any | — | nil; validated with its root's occurrence, origin and counter passed down |\n\nThe explicit fork policy spelling projects onto the amendment's\nempty-header fork representation: a fork root proposes nothing and is\nnever upgraded to custody; an exclusive-only receiver refuses it as\n`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. Admission semantics are the governing ones\nunchanged; only the spelling is new. No body carries an epoch.\n\nThe root is a content row and **its id is the transported state's\naddress**, the `B` of UCF §7.3.4 for this carrier. Occurrence and origin\nare inside it, as the governing rule keeps them (§10.1). The code map is\nplain data in the root rather than a row kind: each instruction vector\nkeeps its own address `A` computed exactly as UCF §7.3.2 computes it, and\nthe carrier verifies, never recomputes, that address.\n\n## 6. Canonical cursor-cell numbering\n## 7. Equality and the content address\n\nThe transported state is a DAG rooted at the `:task` row. Its address is\nthe root id, which commits to every reachable content row, to every cursor cell\nid through the references, and to every cursor cell's contents through the\ncensus. Two transported states are equal iff their root ids are equal,\nand under §6 that holds iff they were lifted from states equal up to the\nrenaming of cursor-cell ids that §6 makes canonical. No physical enumeration of\nthe row set needs an order, because the root commits to everything and\nthe pack, if one is addressed, has its own canonical encoding (§10.2).\nJing supplies canonical bytes and the digest of each body; this document\nsupplies the order within each body and the numbering of cursor cells. Jing is\nasked to sort nothing it does not already sort.\n\nOccurrence and origin are inside the hash, as in versions 1 and 2 where\nthe body address is the hash of the whole body. Snapshot variants of one\noccurrence may therefore have distinct addresses while remaining one\noccurrence, exactly as UCF §7.3.4 intends.\n\n## 8. Validation on lower\n\n    operation inspector and admission checks of §7.7.8; an authority\n    never adds missing header keys.\n\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13; continuation §7.2).** The merged\n`rows/lower-task` runs steps 8-10 as four stages, **each over the whole\ntask tree before the next, and all before the first attachment** (step\n11): (a) stores: no reserved name keys the root store or a module store\n(`:yin.k/undecodable :reserved-name`); (b) pendings and install\nrecords: role, every `:install` pending names a carried install, kind\nconsistency, parked id, every install record as §5.4 as amended\n(`:role :incomplete-install :kind :parked-id :install-phase\n:install-response :install-child`); (c) dependencies: `requires`'\n`:yin.k/segments` equals `code`'s key set (`:requires`), every segment\nhas a non-empty `:yin.k/trees` entry, every `store-of` of this task's\nclosures and environments names one of **this task's** module-store\nrows (`:yin.k/unsatisfied`, `:yin.k/missing {:yin.k/stores …}`), every\nvector attaches in the receiving kernel under its A (`:segment`), every\nclosure row names a closure tuple of its vector with the same binders\n(`:closure-origin`, `:closure-binders`), every `:prim` is the receiver's\n(`:yin.k/unsatisfied`); (d) custody: nil only (`:yin.k/unsupported\n:custody`). Of step 8's text, the op-id rules and the correlation\nagreement are not implemented (fork-only custody has no `next-op-seq`;\ncontinuation §11 Q4); of step 9's, the census is not recomputed from the\nrows (continuation §11 Q2), and `requires` beyond segments, trees and\nprimitives (modules, streams, cursor profiles) is not evaluated on\nlower; free names are discharged at lift only (continuation §7.1).\nRefusals are thrown `ex-info` with `:yin.k/status` and a `:yin.k/kind`.\n11. **Composition.** Protection, resource, primitive and grant inputs of\n    the receiving composition.\n12. **Restore.** Reconstruct isolated code spaces, fresh values, the\n    isolated store, module-store instances (first link wins), one\n    private resource entry per cursor cell, the parked table, children first\n    (one native task instance **per install entry**, even where two\n    entries share a child row), then waits in order; adopt `max(local,\n    carried)` for the id counter; publish the machine only after every\n    child succeeds; run, poll, mint, append, close or initialize nothing\n    during lower.\n\nDiagnostics use the established families and no new top-level outcome:\n`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, `:yin.k/undecodable` with `:yin.k/path` as\n`[row-id slot-index …]` and a `:yin.k/kind` where one is named above\n(`:reason-mismatch`, `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`,\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`), `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`,\n`:yin.k/unsatisfied`, and on the lift side `:yin.k/non-portable` with\nthe three kinds this carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string`,\n`:metadata` and `:unresolved-cursor-cells`.\n\n## 9. Lift, lower, and the laws\n\n**Lift** (reference machine): refuse unless quiescent; number cursor cells (§6);\nwalk waits, parked records, the slice, module stores, the initial free\nenvironment (continuation §6.3, §7.1), the result and each install\nchild, emitting rows and the census; refuse by path as UCF\n§7.5.4 and §7.2.1 specify. **Amended after 6b (2026-10-13; continuation\n§7.1 B4):** the merged order is quiescence and safepoint, kind, free\nenvironment, the walk (which fixes the segments, contexts and module\nstores), code, the per-context dependency closure over live code, tree\ncoverage from the composition's `:source-index`, frame validation of\nthe assembled task, then canonical numbering and emit; the slice is the\nwhole root store and every parked record; custody is nil. The nested carrier's value table\n(`:yin.k/values`) has no counterpart, sharing being Merkle; its cursor cell\ntable becomes the census.\n\n**Lower**: §8 in order, then restore.\n\nThe laws, with the domains the Architect's finding 12 requires:\n\n- **Canonical round trip.** For every valid, canonical (§6-numbered)\n  version-3 state `S` under the one profile of §2 **for which `lower(S)`\n  succeeds** (adequate composition, resource and admission inputs; a\n  refusal is not a counterexample), and with no execution between lower\n  and lift, `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id under the **fresh-receiver\n  reconstruction context**: the receiver's id counter is 0 so\n  `max(local, carried)` is the carried value; occurrence, origin and the\n  custody arm are supplied to the lift from the restored task's own\n  record, not minted; cursor-cell bodies and op-ids are carried verbatim; the\n  lift's §6 numbering undoes resource remapping. Outside that context\n  (a receiver with a larger counter, or any step taken) the law does not\n  apply and native equivalence is the claim.\n- **Native observational equivalence.** `lower(lift(m))` need not equal\n  `m` as native state (fresh ids, seals, private keys); under equivalent\n  external inputs it must preserve the next observable step, the wait\n  set and its order, the result, and the effect trace.\n- **Cross-engine.** Running the corpus to each safepoint on an engine\n  that implements canonical semantic-VM execution yields, under\n  controlled composition identities (occurrence, origin, resource\n  descriptors fixed by the harness), the same version-3 row set the\n  reference machine yields.\n\nThe UCF §7.11 harness gains one assertion per law. The Stage D/E corpus,\nre-encoded as version-3 rows, is the oracle for the first law.\n\n## 10. Questions the review answered, and what remains\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1857,2039p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '2823,2899p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '2993,3150p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1857,2039p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '2823,2899p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '2993,3150p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\"","aggregated_output":"### 7.7.4 The exporting state: fencing the source before publication\n\nEmitter-as-candidate was the first draft's whole answer to the\nsource-wakeup race, and it does not close the publication window. The\nlifecycle published first and made the emitter a candidate afterward, but\nnothing ever removed the emitter's *local* eligibility: its driver may\nstill poll the wait set — and polling a blocked writer **performs the\nappend** (the engine, a `:put` entry retries `append!` on every\npoll) — may restore a woken entry from the ready queue, may satisfy a\ndirect `:resume`, and may retry a blocked write, all after the value is in\nother machines' hands. Delaying register restoration is too late; the\nappend is the effect.\n\nUCF therefore defines an explicit **exporting** transition that precedes\nany publication, and the lift driver performs it as one step over the\nmachine value:\n\n1. **Quiesce**: the ready-queue must be empty (§7.6.3).\n2. **Detach the wait set**: every wait entry moves from the machine's\n   wait-set into the export record. The machine's wait-set is now empty,\n   so *there is nothing to poll* — no poll, no append, no effect. The\n   entries are not lost: the export record retains them verbatim; they are\n   the source of the `:yin.k/pending` variants of §7.4.3.\n3. **Neutralize control**: the record's control and K are already absent\n   (the machine is parked or blocked); direct `:resume` resolves against\n   `:parked`, and the parked records referenced by reachable code travel\n   inside the export record — a local `:resume` against them is refused\n   while exporting (the restore function observes the exporting machine\n   and declines), because the parked id now names a checkpoint that may be\n   granted elsewhere.\n4. **Publish, then offer**: append segments and the value body; record\n   `:yin.k/offered` on the arbitration medium. Only now do readers exist,\n   and the source was fenced before any of them did.\n\nThe exporting machine is pure data with an empty wait-set and a retained\nexport record — no engine change, no callback, no timer; the drivers of the\nworld observe the state as they observe `ready-for-ingress?` (§3.1), and a\ndriver that polls an exporting machine's wait set anyway is a host assembly\ndefect of the same kind as wiring a refusing deposit destination.\n\n**Failure recovery and idempotent retry.** If publication fails — a full\ncarrier, an unreachable arbitration medium — the machine stays exporting\nand the composition retries; a retry re-encodes or reuses the encoded value\nand **reuses the occurrence** (§7.3.4), so no retry mints a second runnable\ncheckpoint. Abandoning the export reinstates the export record into the\nmachine (wait entries return to the wait-set, parked records to `:parked`)\nand the task resumes as a purely local one — the sole paths back to local\nexecution are *abort export* (before the offer is recorded) and *a grant to\nthe emitter itself* (after); there is no third.\n\n(M-next D8/D9, as built.) The exporting machine carries one gate key,\n`:yin.k/gate`, with the modes `:running`, `:exporting` and `:ended`:\nunder `:running` internal computation proceeds and stream observations\nand effects are deferred to the driver; under `:exporting` and `:ended`\nnothing is observed, no install child is advanced, a direct resume is\nrefused, and every public apply refuses a late result. The root's mode\nis stamped on each install child at its creation and before each run;\na child carries the mode only. A machine entering exporting refuses\nthe custody holds -- a wait entry with reason `:observe`, an entry or\ncursor cell carrying `:yin.k/held`, a cursor cell carrying `:yin.k/unminted`,\na machine with a nonempty `:yin.k/closes` queue, and a\n`:link-request` entry without `:cursor` -- as `:yin.k/non-portable`,\nkind `:reason-mismatch`, with the holding task's path; the lift\nrefuses the same holds on its own behalf. A task is not at a liftable\nsafepoint while it holds any of these.\n\nThe lift's custody input is one **header** argument -- supplied to\n`holder.export/prepare [machine record serve! header]`, stored in the\nexport record as `:header`, and passed to `export-task` through its\n`:header` option -- `nil` or:\n\n    {:yin.k/occurrence  O\n     :yin.k/arbitration {:dao.stream/identity i\n                         :dao.stream/descriptor d}\n     :yin.k/origin      {:yin.k/occurrence P\n                         :dao.lease/lease L\n                         :yin.k/emitter a}  ; absent on a first export\n     :yin.k/next-op-seq n\n     :yin.k/enrolled    #{stream-identity ...}}\n\nA `nil` header lifts today's version-0 fork; a header lifts version 1,\nexclusive, in the canonical codec, with `:yin.k/policy\n:yin.k/exclusive` added by the lift. The occurrence is minted once by\nthe driver and persisted before prepare is called; `:yin.k/enrolled`\nis the composition's declaration from the ledger reader; it is never\nencoded into the UCF body, but the header stays in the retained export\nrecord. The live record can contain authentic closures and is not itself\na canonical data object. `holder.export/freeze [machine prepared-record]`\nencodes a separate content-addressed export-recovery object, tagged\n`:yin.k/export-recovery` with format version 1, using the handoff value/cursor-cell\ncodec. It returns recovery bytes/address beside the exact handoff body\nbytes/address; both are stored durably before the journal's fenced event.\nRecovery retains the local enrolled declaration, prior gate, ordered waits,\nparked continuations, issue stamps, descriptors and dependency closure.\nThe enrolled declaration still never travels in a published handoff body.\n\n`holder.export/rehydrate-fenced` validates the recovery object and its\nembedded body before administrative attachment. It creates authentic\nfresh-receiver-owned values and preserves resource aliases and distinct\ncursor cells without program reads, cursor minting, writes, closes, initialization\nor execution. Every task stays exporting, with waits outside scheduler\nqueues. The prior gate is retained only as data; recovery is neither grant\nadmission nor permission to abort back into execution. Runtime composition\nfunctions and handles are the environment, not the canonical journal.\nIncomplete preparations cannot manufacture a missing snapshot on restart.\nRecovery also retains a complete codec snapshot when legacy fork bytes\nomit transitive dependencies; the published version-0 bytes stay unchanged.\nFor version 1, the module-store fixed point is a correctness repair:\ncanonical ordering uses a separate scratch census and did not populate\nthe emitted dependency table. Bodies that previously omitted transitive\nmodule stores now contain them and have different content addresses; this\nis not a new handoff version or closure format. The transitive-store test\npins the old one-pass body and corrected body bytes by their addresses.\n\nThe driver's exit persists recovery and body, journals the fence, attempts\nthe stable successor offer, reports that exact successor/body, carries\nrelease of the matching origin lease, observes the authoritative closure\nand successor edge, then retries the same offer until admitted. The\nrecovery object is stored first and a store that refuses stops the step\nbefore the body is attempted; a retry re-brackets from the recovery\nobject, idempotent through the store. An\n`:awaiting-completion` offer answer does not block reporting. A halt reports\nits result body and observes the terminal edge, with no successor offer.\nEvery external attempt has a durable intent before delivery and an\nacknowledgment afterward; an unmatched intent retains unknown delivery.\nA non-admitted report answer either retries -- `:suspended` resends the\nidentical request -- or ends the run (r3 1.6): the terminal refusals\ngate the machine `:ended`, append one diagnostic and carry the cleanup\nrelease, which closes nothing by itself. A legal abort journals\none terminal `:aborted` record before local execution is handed back,\nand the journal fold treats that record as closing the export. A holder\nrechecks tenure across that append -- dao.lease's own `holding?`, with\nthe grant's max cap and its stopped latches -- and tenure that died\nunder it ends the run instead of restoring the machine; the cleanup\nrelease opens with the next step, after the terminal record.\nReopened holders do not regain tenure from persisted clock readings:\nthey reconcile, release and re-enter candidacy, never resume execution.\n\nKind and\nheader agree at lift: blocked and parked roots carry occurrence,\narbitration, counter and policy, and origin when present; a halted\nroot carries origin only, and a header-bearing halt without an origin\nis refused -- it is not automatically downgraded (a halt with no\norigin at all is a version-0 result under a `nil` header); install\nchildren carry no header. Before answering `:ok`, a version-1 root\nlift runs `checkpoint/inspect` on its own bytes and address, then\n`handoff/validate-body` on the decoded body; install children do not\nindependently undergo the root custody inspection. Any refusal\nreturns as the lift's refusal and nothing is published. A lift under a\nheader refuses `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:op-seq-exhausted`, at the\n2^52-1 counter of a blocked or parked root (a halted root ignores that\nheader value), kind `:unprotected-pending` for a retained `:put`,\n`:ffi-request` or `:link-request` entry, in the root or an install\nchild, with no operation id whose target identity is enrolled, and\n`:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream for an operation id on an\nunenrolled target.\n\nAbort is permitted only before any possibly accepted offer attempt, or\nupon authoritative evidence that this occurrence has never been\nadmitted and cannot still become admitted from an outstanding attempt;\na refusal of one request is not such evidence. As implemented,\n`holder.export/abort` reads the composition's persisted `attempts`\nsequence -- each attempt's append outcome under `:append` -- and\naccepts abort only with no attempts or when every attempt's outcome is\nin the closed set `#{:dao.stream/full :dao.stream/invalid-value\n:dao.stream/closed :dao.stream/refused}`; `transport-error` and a\nmissing answer refuse. A holder exporting a successor may abort only\nunder current valid tenure, implemented as `{:now n :bound b :live\ntrue}` with `:live` supplied by ledger evidence that the lease is the\noccurrence's active lease at its epoch; at or past the bound, and\nwithout the evidence, abort is refused. After the lease has ended its\nold local machine is never restored.\n\n(M-next D15a, as built.) The driver steps this state through a\ncustody seam, not `:phase` alone: `control-step` carries custody\nprogress and the established offer/report/release/closure brackets,\n`program-step` prepares and captures the export, `stop` latches the\ndriver, and `owed-control-write?` answers the control cadence. A\ncandidate that accepted a grant but has not lowered it stays in\n`:activating` and still stops at its own lease bound. The inbox\nretention machine and the unavailable-lane semantics are in 7.11.\n\n### 7.7.5 Fencing effects: epochs, operation ids, and what exactly-once costs\n## §7.11 Acceptance blockers for the implementation phase\n\nThe warning at the top of this file is retained until each of these is\nclosed with a test in the parity or conformance suite. The first four are\nthe review's named architectural obligations; they are blockers to\n*accepting the design*, not merely work items.\n\n- **Authority grounding and wiring.** The arbitration `dao.space`, its\n  transactor as grantor, the occurrence ledger with epochs, and the\n  attribution resolver `dao.lease.md` requires, are composition duties; the\n  first composition to ship UCF is `yin.repl.core`'s handoff demo, and\n  its wiring becomes the worked example. A composition without a\n  transactable space offers `:yin.k/fork` only.\n- **Execution identity.** Occurrence minting, the `:yin.k/origin` chain,\n  retry idempotence (one occurrence per park), and successor completion as\n  a grantor ledger transition need conformance tests, including the crash\n  windows of §7.7.5.\n- **Pending-state completeness.** Every pending variant of §7.4.3 must\n  round-trip and resume *elsewhere*: retained writes, sent and retained FFI\n  calls with both endpoints and the kept response cursor, and cursor cells\n  through the aliasing scenarios of `check-wait-set`. This depends on\n  `dao.stream.remote.md` and its **portable cursor profile**\n  `:dao.stream.remote/v1`, which preserves the source stream's kept cursor\n  and `gap` outcomes. The M4 string-backed stream test below is the\n  required cross-host proof.\n- **Enforceable fencing.** Epoch-checked, atomic-with-commitment admission\n  and durable op-id plus intent dedup at enrolled consumers are design\n  items for `dao.space.transactor.md`. A consumer without a shared atomic\n  boundary declares at-least-once or fail-stop behavior; it cannot claim\n  exactly-once custody from the lease alone.\n- **Canonical byte encoding.** `:yin.code/hash` and `:yin.k/id` are exactly\n  as portable as `dao.jing/segment-key`. Canonical CBOR landed in\n  3ddaa21b (recorded in `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` section 5); the\n  cross-host address checks below still must pass.\n- **Contract revision publication.** `:yin.code/contract \"v3\"` needs a\n  published revision history naming the complete execution contract\n  (§7.3.3): the tuple grammar — mnemonic set, per-mnemonic arity and operand\n  kinds, saturation/defaults table — the opcode table and transitions, the\n  resolution and last-value-wins rules, the effect outcome map, and the\n  scheduler semantics. The index is in `yin.vm.semantic.md` 2.4 and the\n  history landed in dbae125b (`yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md`); runtime profile\n  checks below remain open.\n- **Primitive profile publication.** The standard `yin.vm/primitives`\n  map published with profiles (S7.5.2), including the `:effectful`\n  declaration for `require` (`yin/def` is syntax, not a primitive);\n  reverse-lookup uniqueness asserted at `create-vm`.\n- **Definition frames under Rule R.** The UCF frame encoding of a\n  pending definition (a define continuation awaiting its value), stamp\n  comparison at continuation lowering, module-store snapshot lowering\n  through `engine/store-put`, and the lift and lower round trip of a\n  parked definition are M4 obligations and do not pass today.\n- **Discovery completeness.** The fixed point of §7.6.1 implemented and\n  tested against the `:store-get`/`:store-put`, dynamic-callee, and\n  cross-activation-`E` cases; module manifests publishing store footprints\n  so `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete` disappears.\n- **Isolated-store lowering.** Conformance tests that the resumed task's\n  store contains exactly the slice and the resumer's pair, and that a\n  receiver-side binding of a name in the slice's name set cannot change\n  resolution.\n- **Safepoint conformance harness.** A test that runs every corpus segment\n  on the reference machine to each safepoint of the complete §7.4.1 table —\n  effect-producing calls included — lifts, lowers into a fresh VM, and\n  checks the parity suite's result; the same harness is what a foreign\n  engine runs to claim conformance, including `lift ∘ lower = id` on\n  canonical frames, the static `def`/`use`/`live` and\n  `lexically-required` checks, the frame validator on every frame, and\n  **cross-engine canonical equality**: one program at one safepoint lifts\n  to row sets with equal root ids after the harness's specified\n  re-emission (`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §5.5) from\n  the semantic-register kernel and from the stack and register kernels\n  through their layouts, the stack kernel with its tail-completion seam;\n  and the §7.3.4 image-equality assertion (the two AST projection lanes\n  and the direct vector load yield one image and address).\n- **Exporting-state integration.** The lift driver's exporting transition\n  and its failure/retry paths, tested specifically against the\n  poll-a-blocked-writer-appends hazard of §7.7.4.\n\n### 7.11.1 Blocker-closure acceptance matrix\n\nThese are observable test contracts, not a declaration that the blockers\nare closed. M3 and M4 tests run on JVM, CLJS (Node), and CLJD. A named\nrefusal must be a data outcome before a code image is loaded, a machine is\nrestored, or an effect is committed, as applicable. The M4 lift driver\nmust consume the UCF table amendments in linker section 11 item 12 before\nits round-trip tests run. Full UCF acceptance waits for every row,\nincluding the post-M5 gates; passing a linker milestone alone is not a\nclaim that all five blockers have closed.\n\n**Code identity (7.3; blocker 5).**\n\n- Invariant: A resumer executes the instruction stream named by the\n  continuation's address and contract stamp, or refuses before loading it.\n- Setup: On each host, make equal resolved vectors from batches with\n  different entity ids, order, provenance, and omitted defaults; make\n  unequal vectors by reversing repeated single-valued operands. Include\n  a `:gensym` prefix of `false` beside absent and explicit `\"id\"` prefixes.\n  Verify the published `v2` decision on the direct vector load and on\n  the vector each of the two AST lanes (row lane, datom lane) produces;\n  there is no projected vector load (§7.3.4).\n  Include an index hit, carried code, a `dao.jing` miss/hit path, a valid\n  vector under a wrong index entry, a correctly addressed malformed\n  vector, a missing address, an old stamp, and two valid images claiming\n  one live local id.\n- Action and expected outcome: M3 `step`/`fetch` transfers and verifies\n  each format over ring buffers; the two equal vectors address equally,\n  the unequal vectors do not, and a `:sha256` address still verifies under\n  its named algorithm. M3 also passes linker section 9's full refusal\n  matrix, staged-response, invalid-request, and traffic-equivalence\n  tests without a function or handle on the link stream. M4 lower selects\n  by stamp then address, verifies hash and grammar, and gives B0-equal\n  execution on all three hosts.\n  Wrong index content is linker `:hash-mismatch` at M3 or\n  `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` at UCF lower; malformed code is\n  `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the pc; a miss is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`;\n  an incompatible stamp is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. No check may\n  redirect an existing local id or bypass the common validator. The\n  falsy-prefix case either normalizes identically on the direct load and\n  on both AST lanes or is refused as inadmissible before an address is\n  minted.\n- Landing and order: M3 lands the stream and format-identity gate;\n  M4 closes UCF identity after M3 and the published normalization\n  decision, before any lifted image is run.\n  The already landed `v2` history (dbae125b), canonical CBOR\n  (3ddaa21b), and Phase 1 vector/stamp (f51077f2) supply the names and\n  bytes, not the cross-host lowering proof. Landed linker design\n  (1f7990d5, sections 5 and 9) specifies M2's four format records and\n  refusal tests; the records must pass those tests before M3 starts.\n\n**Safepoint reconstruction (7.4; blocker 1).**\n\n- Invariant: At every declared parking transition, lifting and lowering\n  preserve the next observable step and its wait, while other pcs refuse.\n- Setup: Run the corpus to every 7.4.1 row: explicit park, blocked\n  stream read, blocked write, sent FFI, retained FFI, ordinary and tail\n  effectful calls, and halt; also exercise M4's `:link-request`,\n  `:link-response`, and `:install` pending variants (linker section 11\n  item 12). Exercise distinct activation depths and captured environments\n  at one pc, a nonempty ready queue, a nonsafepoint pc, and a kept response\n  cursor shared by two waiters. Omit the portable cursor profile for the\n  refusal case. Separately prepare the three engines intended to\n  reconstruct canonical frames (the semantic-register kernel; the\n  register kernel; the stack kernel after its tail-completion seam) with\n  a nontrivial call stack, a captured closure, and a blocked effect at\n  the handoff point.\n- Action and expected outcome: On each host, lift a parked task and lower\n  into a fresh VM, then compare its result and effect trace with the\n  reference machine. Before the full corpus, lift that handoff from one\n  engine and lower it in the other; the canonical row set after\n  re-emission, the pending wait, next result and effect trace must agree\n  across all three and in every lowering direction. Static\n  `:yin.safepoint/kinds`, `def`/`use`/`live`, and lexical reads agree with\n  the reference trace. The observed parked record, wait entry, and effect\n  outcome determine `:yin.k/reason` and `:yin.k/pending`, never the\n  static kind alone.\n  Test both FFI states and each call effect. `lift(lower(frame))` must\n  reproduce the canonical frame. An undeclared pc gives\n  `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`; queued work gives `:yin.k/not-quiescent`.\n  Insufficient pending evidence refuses before publication, as does a\n  missing portable cursor profile. Halt yields a result, not a frame.\n- Reflection acceptance (`dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md`, slice 8): Park a\n  continuation holding a string-backed local `dao.stream` with a kept\n  cursor, then migrate to another network node. The exporter\n  mechanically serves that stream by entering it in its table; the cursor cell\n  descriptor is a remote descriptor and the cursor cell carries the kept\n  cursor, and the receiver holds a reflection on the original logical\n  stream. Reads resume at the kept position. Force retention loss and\n  verify the reflection returns `:dao.stream/gap` with the source's\n  successor cursor, without replay or silent skip. Repeat with a source\n  that cannot honor the profile: lift refuses `:yin.k/unsatisfied`; a\n  reflection marked gone on lower is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the\n  stream. The exporter's entry and any relay pair the route depends on\n  are lease-governed, and a resumer that arrives after reclaim observes\n  `not-found`.\n- Landing and order: M4 covers the early two-engine gate, the table,\n  reference-machine parity, and the cross-host reflection proof after code\n  identity; the kept-cursor proof no longer waits for post-M5 hardening.\n  M4 must land the mirror step and reflection of `dao.stream.remote.md`,\n  not just reuse a native network stream.\n  Before export, specify the no-wait explicit-park representation and any\n  `:call-effect` pending shape; test them or remove that reason from the\n  wire contract. The `:reasons` Option B ruling in\n  `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` section 8 is landed design, not a runtime\n  fix: M4 replaces it with static `:kinds` and tests dynamic reasons.\n  A foreign engine claiming conformance runs the same frame parity harness.\n\n**Recursive portable encoding (7.5; blocker 2).**\n\n- Invariant: Every reachable portable value returns with its meaning and\n  aliasing intact, and every unsupported or malformed value refuses as a\n  whole with a precise path before execution.\n- Setup: Park frames with nested literal maps that look like UCF tags,\n  map keys containing values, closures, reified and parked continuations,\n  profiled primitives, shared subvalues, stream refs, and two cursor refs\n  that share a cursor cell beside two independent cursor cells at the same position.\n  Add a host object, unnamed or ambiguous function, host-state primitive,\n  cyclic value, forged resource ref, missing table ref, cyclic ref data,\n  extra table entry, and a correctly hashed malformed marker.\n- Action and expected outcome: Lift, transfer, and lower on each host;\n  literals remain literals, repeated refs share one value, independent\n  cursor cells advance independently, and shared cursor cells advance in wait\n  order. The lift refuses unsupported leaves as `:yin.k/non-portable`\n  with path and kind and publishes no partial value; decode refuses\n  malformed markers and refs as `:yin.k/undecodable` with path before\n  restoring state. Forged resource refs refuse before lift, while valid\n  refs re-seal on lower into the receiver's private resource table. A\n  same-named primitive with a different profile is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`,\n  never substituted by name alone.\n- Landing and order: M4 supplies the recursive codec, published standard\n  primitive profiles (including effectful `yin/def` and `require`), and\n  linker section 11 item 12's `:yin.k/binding`, `:yin.k/store-of`,\n  private-resource, and sealed-reference variants before lift uses them.\n  M4 also proves the string-backed stream reflection and kept-cursor case in\n  the safepoint row before cross-host values are accepted. Phase 1\n  canonicalization and M2 format records do not encode frames or values.\n\n**Ownership arbitration (7.7; blocker 4).**\n\n- Invariant: One occurrence has at most one admitted holder, and each\n  enrolled protected effect commits at most once across re-grants.\n- Setup: Copy one checkpoint, including two valid encodings with one\n  occurrence id, to two readers and leave the source's blocked writer\n  wakeable. Use a transactable arbitration space, a grantor ledger,\n  enrolled transactional consumer, and durable op-id intent and result\n  records. Inject publication failure, retry, stale lease, consumer\n  partition, and crashes after the successor append, resumed report, and\n  release. After a crash, evict a kept-cursor value so the re-granted run\n  observes `gap` and attempts a different effect at the same sequence.\n- Action and expected outcome: M4's exporting transition detaches waits\n  before publication; polling or direct resume of the exporting source\n  cannot append, and retry preserves the occurrence. With arbitration,\n  only a granted holder runs; competitors get `:yin.k/not-holder` or\n  `:yin.k/awaiting-grant`. Reclaim advances the epoch; stale effects fail\n  at commitment. At the enrolled consumer, epoch check, operation-intent\n  check, dedup record, and side-effect commitment share one atomic\n  boundary: equal id and intent return the stored result without a second\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
