{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '3170,3260p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '1640,1770p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; sed -n '870,916p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc; sed -n '1554,1573p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc; cat collab/1792480000000-engineer-7c-compose-fencing.report.md\"","aggregated_output":"  address with different captured environments, a transitive module\n  closure, and a parked-id reference. Give the receiver a conflicting\n  store binding and a different FFI pair. Omit in turn a segment, module\n  footprint, primitive profile, stream attachment, and parked record.\n- Action and expected outcome: Lift computes the fixed point over\n  address-context pairs and the reachable store slice; lowering checks\n  every requirement before restoring. The resumed task reads the carried\n  binding, uses the receiver's pair only for future calls, routes pending\n  calls to their carried endpoints, and shares no task-local module store.\n  A missing segment reports `:blocked`; an undeclared footprint reports\n  `:incomplete`; missing requirements yield `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with\n  the computed discovery state and missing set. A missing active-value\n  parked ref refuses lift as `:yin.k/non-portable` with\n  `:yin.k/kind :foreign-parked-ref`. No partial lower occurs.\n- Landing and order: M4 must test linker obligations, manifest store\n  footprints, transitive child-install slices, and isolated lowering\n  after encoding and code identity; linker section 11 criteria 15 and 23\n  are the minimum module cases. The full UCF fixed point, scheduler\n  parked records and fresh-name state, and all missing-dependency modes\n  close in post-M5 hardening. M2 format records verify link payloads;\n  they do not prove the task's transitive runtime closure.\n\n**Fenced custody, version 1 (7.2.1, 7.4.3, 7.7.8, 7.9; amendment r5).**\n\nThis block adds obligations; it changes no row above. The safepoint\nand portable-encoding rows keep their M4 kept-cursor and reflection\ngate with its meaning and its evidence as written. The stage-1\nhandoff tests that landed with M-next A (80b59233,\n`test/yin/vm/ucf/handoff_test.cljc`) are recorded by\n`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.1.3 and by `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` I-5;\nthey are not moved here and nothing below relies on them as custody\nevidence. The ownership row keeps its own contract; the rows below\nare the wire-level clauses that row's tests must also exercise.\n\n- Invariant: A version-1 body, envelope, binding, and admission\n  outcome mean the same on JVM, Node, and Dart, and every breach of\n  their grammar is a data outcome before a machine is restored or an\n  effect is committed.\n- What M-next B claims: the grammar is published. It lands no code,\n  passes no test, and closes no row. `handoff-version` is still 0.\n- How each clause is tested on three hosts: by canonical byte\n  fixtures decoded on each host, so a rule about integer kind or key\n  presence is checked on the bytes and not on a host number type;\n  and by the ordered host pairs of `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.1.1\n  where a value crosses. Outcomes are compared as data.\n\nClauses, with the stage that owes the evidence\n(`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.3):\n\n1. Version gate (7.2.1). Fixtures: version 2, absent, a float `1.0`,\n   a version-0 child in a version-1 root. Assert\n   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` with the found and supported versions,\n   or `:yin.k/undecodable` for the mixed tree, with zero attach\n   calls, zero proposals, and no machine: recursive validation has\n   no side effect. The integral float is refused on Node as on the\n   others. A version-0-only reader refuses a version-1 body. A\n   version-0 body still lowers as a fork on a reader that speaks\n   both; a composition requiring exclusive refuses it. Stage: D.\n2. Custody header (7.2.1). Fixtures omit each required key, give a\n   fork policy, a nil occurrence, an origin equal to the body's\n   occurrence, and header keys other than the origin on a halted\n   root. Root and embedded child are distinguished: any header key\n   on a child, a halted child with an origin included, is refused,\n   and a halted child without one validates. Assert\n   `:yin.k/undecodable` with the path. Stage: D.\n3. Sequence state (7.7.8). Assign-before-append, one increment,\n   retention across `full`, unknown effect, and `:suspended`; the\n   counter restored exactly; children drawing from the root.\n   Stage: D for assignment and restoration; C for durable input\n   replay; E for crash and regrant through the composition.\n4. Carried ids (7.4.3, 7.7.8). An id crosses park, bytes, and lower\n   unchanged on `:put`, `:ffi-request`, and `:link-request`, and the\n   retry is fenced. Fixtures: an id on `:next`, a sequence at or\n   above the counter, a duplicate id, an id in a body without an\n   origin, an id naming the body's own occurrence. Assert\n   `:yin.k/undecodable`. Protection mismatch in each direction is\n   `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream; a first exclusive export\n   over an attempted write is `:unprotected-pending`. Stage: D for\n   these structural checks. Stage: C for authoritative membership\n   and ancestry: an ancestor id the granted checkpoint never\n   carried is refused, a carried one is admitted, a child's carried\n   id counts, and an orphan successor gives no ancestry.\n5. Explicit park and install completeness (7.4.3), both versions.\n   A `:park` or `:call-effect` reason and an `:install` pending\n   without its entry refuse at decode; a `:parked` body's frames\n   restore in order; a response that fails verification and a phase\n   outside `:running` and `:parked` refuse; each of the two phases\n   crosses with each child kind `:blocked`, `:parked` and `:halted`,\n   a `:running` phase over a blocked child and an explicitly parked\n   child included, and lower preserves both fields; a runnable\n   child refuses export; a restored child continues from its saved\n(defn- lift-task*\n  [m serve! opts role]\n  (when (seq (:ready-queue m))\n    (outcome! :yin.k/not-quiescent nil {:yin.k/ready (count (:ready-queue m))}))\n  (when-some [h (unliftable-hold m)]\n    (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :reason-mismatch {:yin.k/hold h}))\n  (when (some? (:control m))\n    (outcome! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc (get-in m [:control :pc])}))\n  (let [value (:value m)\n        active? (and (map? value) (= :parked-continuation (:type value)) (contains? (:parked m) (:id value)))\n        kind (cond active? :parked\n                   (seq (:wait-set m)) :blocked\n                   (engine/halted-with-empty-queue? m) :halted\n                   :else (outcome! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {}))\n        ;; §6.3: the explicit argument overrides the kernel's retained field\n        free-env (cond (some? (:free-env opts)) (:free-env opts)\n                       (some? (:free-env m)) (:free-env m)\n                       :else (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :missing-free-env {}))\n        ctx {:m m :serve! serve! :serve-keyed (:serve-keyed opts) :path (or (:path opts) [])\n             :acc (atom {:streams {} :cells {} :stores #{} :segments #{} :contexts #{}})}\n        free-node (bindings-node ctx free-env)\n        waits (mapv (fn [e] (node :wait [(state-node ctx e) (pending-node ctx e)])) (:wait-set m))\n        parked (node :parked-table (mapcat (fn [[p rec]] [p (state-node ctx rec)]) (:parked m)))\n        store (store-node ctx (:store m))\n        result (when (= :halted kind) (lift-value ctx value))\n        installs (mapv (fn [[mn inst]] (install-node ctx mn inst opts)) (sort-by #(str (key %)) (:installs m)))\n        module-stores (module-store-nodes ctx)\n        acc @(:acc ctx)\n        segments (:segments acc)\n        code (into {} (map (fn [a]\n                             (if-some [v (get-in m [:code (get (:code-aliases m) a) :vector])]\n                               [a v]\n                               (outcome! :yin.k/unsatisfied nil {:yin.k/segment a}))))\n                   segments)\n        _ (discharge! ctx code)\n        index (or (:source-index opts) {})\n        missing-trees (into #{} (remove #(seq (get index %))) segments)\n        _ (when (seq missing-trees)\n            (outcome! :yin.k/unsatisfied nil {:yin.k/discovery :incomplete :yin.k/missing {:yin.k/trees missing-trees}}))\n        requires {:yin.k/cursor-profiles (if (seq (:cells acc)) remote/cursor-profiles #{})\n                  :yin.k/segments segments\n                  :yin.k/trees (select-keys index segments)}\n        task (node :task [3 profile kind role (or (:id-counter m) 0) free-node store module-stores parked\n                          (node :cursor-cells (mapcat identity (:cells acc))) requires code installs waits\n                          (when (= :parked kind) (:id value)) result nil])]\n    ;; §3.3: a frame the evaluator itself minted that fails is inconsistent\n    (when-let [d (validate-frames task)]\n      (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :inconsistent-state {:yin.k/defect d}))\n    task))\n\n\n(declare task-tree)\n\n\n(defn- header-custody\n  \"The root's custody slot from the caller's header, by kind: blocked or\n   parked takes the occurrence, arbitration, counter and the exclusive\n   policy, and the origin when the header names one; halted the origin\n   alone (an empty map without one, which the grammar refuses). A nil\n   value is an absent key (handoff.cljc's header-of).\"\n  [header kind]\n  (into {}\n        (filter (comp some? val))\n        (if (= :halted kind)\n          (select-keys header [:yin.k/origin])\n          (assoc (select-keys header [:yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq :yin.k/origin])\n                 :yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive))))\n\n\n(defn- protect!\n  \"Every retained write of the tree under the header's enrolled set (UCF\n   7.4.3, 7.7.8; handoff.cljc's protected!): an enrolled target without an\n   operation id is :unprotected-pending, an id on an unenrolled target is\n   :yin.k/unsatisfied naming the stream.\"\n  [task enrolled]\n  (doseq [[t _] (task-tree task :root)\n          w (nth (:slots t) 13)]\n    (let [ss (:slots (second (:slots w)))]\n      (when-let [[ti _ oi] (get write-slots (first ss))]\n        (let [identity (first (:slots (nth ss ti)))\n              enrolled? (contains? enrolled identity)\n              carried? (some? (nth ss oi))]\n          (cond\n            (and enrolled? (not carried?))\n            (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :unprotected-pending {:dao.stream/identity identity})\n            (and carried? (not enrolled?))\n            (outcome! :yin.k/unsatisfied nil {:dao.stream/identity identity})\n            :else nil))))))\n\n\n(defn lift-task\n  \"The lift of §7.1: quiescent semantic-register machine m as a version-3\n   carrier, or a refusal thrown as ex-data {:yin.k/status :yin.k/kind}.\n   serve! is the exporter's remote-table entry (yin.vm.ucf.remote).\n   opts: :free-env, the task's initial free environment (default: the\n   kernel's retained :free-env field; the explicit value overrides it);\n   :source-index, {A #{tree-address}} the composition knows (§6.1), which\n   must cover every carried segment; :budget, the numbering budget;\n   :header, the exclusive custody header (occurrence, arbitration,\n   next-op-seq, origin when the task has a predecessor, and\n   :yin.k/enrolled, the enrolled stream identities, a lift input that\n   never travels); :serve-keyed, (f [task-path resource-id] handle), which\n   replaces serve! and is asked once per resource of each task, the task\n   path being the install names from the root ([] for the root itself),\n   so a caller can retain each answer under its key (holder.export's\n   record). No header is the fork: custody nil. A header writes the\n   root's slot by kind (header-custody), refuses :op-seq-exhausted at\n   2^52-1 on a blocked or parked root, and protects every retained write\n   of the tree (protect!). Before emitting, the lift runs custody-baseline\n   over the task, so it never emits a carrier the inspector refuses.\"\n  ([m serve!] (lift-task m serve! {}))\n  ([m serve! opts]\n   (let [opts (or opts {})\n         header (:header opts)\n         task (lift-task* m serve! opts :root)\n         kind (nth (:slots task) 2)\n         task (if (nil? header)\n                task\n                (let [n (:yin.k/next-op-seq header)]\n                  (when (and (not= :halted kind) (cbor/numeric? n) (cbor/num= n max-exact))\n                    (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :op-seq-exhausted {:yin.k/next-op-seq n}))\n                  (protect! task (or (:yin.k/enrolled header) #{}))\n                  (assoc-in task [:slots 16] (header-custody header kind))))]\n     (custody-baseline task)\n     (emit task (merge default-budget (:budget opts))))))\n\n\n(def ^:private fixed-resource-keys\n  #{vm/call-in-stream-key vm/call-out-stream-key vm/call-out-cursor-key\n    module/link-request-resource module/link-response-resource})\n\n            {:export (:role cell) :step :prepared}))\n      (-> state\n          (assoc-in [k :record] (:record r))\n          (unsatisfied :export-refused\n                       (select-keys r [:yin.k/status :yin.k/reason\n                                       :yin.k/kind :yin.k/path]))))))\n\n\n(defn- export-fence-step\n  \"Freeze the complete record and persist both addressed objects before\n   journaling the fence: the recovery object first, then the pack, and a\n   store that refuses stops the step before the next object is\n   attempted. The journal contains canonical progress only; the runtime\n   environment retains composition functions and handles.\"\n  [state k]\n  (when (= :exit k) (require-tenure! state))\n  (let [cell (get state k)\n        e (export/freeze (:m cell) (:record cell))]\n    (if (not= :ok (:status e))\n      (unsatisfied state :export-refused\n                   (select-keys e [:yin.k/status :yin.k/reason\n                                   :yin.k/kind :yin.k/path]))\n      (let [bytes (:pack-bytes e)\n            address (:pack-address e)\n            record-bytes (:bytes e)\n            record-address (:address e)\n            store-object\n            (fn [[object-address object-bytes]]\n              (let [request-id [:yin.k/store (:occurrence cell) object-address]]\n                (journal! state {:yin.k/journal :yin.k/intent :yin.k/action :yin.k/store\n                                 :yin.k/occurrence (:occurrence cell)\n                                 :yin.k/request {:yin.k/request-id request-id\n                                                 :yin.k/address object-address}})\n                (when (= :exit k) (require-tenure! state))\n                (let [stored? (store-put! state object-address object-bytes)]\n                  (when stored?\n                    (journal! state {:yin.k/journal :yin.k/ack :yin.k/action :yin.k/store\n                                     :yin.k/occurrence (:occurrence cell)\n                                     :yin.k/request-id request-id\n                                     :yin.k/answer {:yin.k/status :stored}}))\n                  stored?)))]\n        (if-not (and (store-object [record-address record-bytes])\n                     (store-object [address bytes]))\n          (unsatisfied state :content-unavailable {:yin.k/address address})\n          (do\n            (journal! state\n                      {:yin.k/journal :yin.k/fenced\n  \"`rows/lower-task` of the state's checkpoint bytes into the receiver\n   under `opts`: `{:status :ok :kind k :vm m}`, or the lower's refusal as\n   data (undecodable bytes are `:yin.k/undecodable`).  Every refusal is\n   decided before the first attachment.\"\n  [state opts]\n  (let [attach! (get-in state [:seams :attach!])\n        wire (try (cbor/decode (:bytes state))\n                  (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _ ::undecodable))]\n    (if (or (= ::undecodable wire) (not (map? wire)))\n      {:yin.k/status :yin.k/undecodable}\n      (try\n        (let [m (rows/lower-task (assoc (:receiver state) :attach-stream attach!) wire attach! opts)]\n          {:status :ok :vm m})\n        (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n          (let [d (ex-data e)]\n            (if (and (map? d) (contains? d :yin.k/status)) d (throw e))))))))\n\n\n(defn- lower\n  \"D10's lower with the accepted grant's inputs, over the receiver:\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-13 +07\n# Slice 7c: composition and fencing on row packs\n\nImplemented in `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7c`, branch `srvm-phase7c`. No commit. All test lanes ran serially in the foreground, JVM only. No Node, Dart, remote host, or production-code change.\n\n## Result\n\nF1 and F2 were the first edits: the driver's source fence test now asserts the journal's store intents name recovery first and the pack second; Q3's install test changes only the derivation record's profile while retaining its honest input/output. Both gaps are protected by actual killed mutants.\n\nComposition fixtures now build semantic-register receivers and source machines through `rows-support`, and every composition supplies its required `:source-index` seam. F3's smuggled holder key is `:source-index (fn [] {})`. The fresh-process plain-composition test still asserts `PHASE lifted` and `REPL-NAMESPACES []`, using a semantic-register source/receiver.\n\nFencing fixtures lift with a custody header and source index, and lower row packs with exclusive, protection, and grant options. Put, retained FFI, and retained link requests preserve their operation ids through park, pack, lower, and fenced retry. The original remote toy transport remains the attachment/observation seam; there is no new code resolver.\n\nThe composition crash tests now observe recovery v2 before the pack. They assert the cut object's identity and format, its presence/absence for after/before cuts, no fence or offer over incomplete storage, and stalled recovery with no attachments or second occurrence mint. Successor mutations decode and re-emit the abstract task/custody through the existing fixture helpers, preserving valid row hashes.\n\nA second admitted encoding reverses pack row order: it has a different pack address, the same root id, and the same baseline. A counter change modifies the root and is correctly a conflicting variant. Pack addresses remain store keys and ledger variant ids; the structural root stays inside the baseline.\n\nTwo focused controls were added: the composition must consult its supplied source index and refuse an empty index; a live composed authority must refuse an unfenced fork result report without changing its projection.\n\n## Files and line counts\n\nFinal lines and additions/deletions versus this worktree's HEAD:\n\n| File | Lines | Added | Deleted | Tests (quick / slow) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/compose_test.cljc` | 1121 | 39 | 12 | 15 / 0 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/compose_rows_test.cljc` | 2597 | 50 | 11 | 40 / 1 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/fencing_rows_test.cljc` | 629 | 105 | 76 | 4 / 0 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/handoff_v2_authority_test.cljc` | 65 | 24 | 60 | 3 / 0 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver_test.cljc` | 4261 | 6 | 0 | 116 / 1 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc` | 318 | 3 | 0 | 12 / 0 |\n\nThis report is the only additional deliverable. The six touched suites contain 192 tests: 190 quick and 2 slow. `compose_rows_test` has 41 tests including its guarded slow case. No pins, resources, rows implementation, layout implementation, kernels, or REPL files were edited.\n\n## Old-codec authority disposition (7a D1)\n\nDeleted only the version-2 authority calls and their assertions from the two mixed tests. Kept all direct inspector/codec coverage, on all four existing engines: fork versus exclusive baselines, fork operation-id refusal, and halted result origin. The two mixed tests were renamed to describe their retained inspector assertions. The old inspector and codec stay untouched until phase 8; no version dispatch or compatibility shim was added. Current row authority behavior is covered by rows-custody/authority suites and the new composed unfenced-report control.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `clj -M:kondo --lint` all six changed `.cljc` files: **0 errors, 0 warnings** (`target/7c-lint-final.log`). `git diff --check` clean.\n- Every `yin.vm.ucf.*` namespace, quick tier: `clojure -M:test -e :slow -r 'yin\\.vm\\.ucf\\..*'`: **700 tests, 9340 assertions, 0 failures, 5 errors** (`target/7c-ucf-quick.log`). All five errors are in `yin.vm.ucf.stage-e-rows-test`; no other namespace fails. The five cases are `row-1-candidates-and-encodings-test`, `row-7-failure-lower-and-lost-authority-test`, `row-3-reclaim-test`, `fork-per-host-test`, and `row-5-replay-divergence-test`. Each currently refuses assembly because `stage_e_world` supplies no source index. Stage E process cases are tagged slow and excluded; no Node process/build ran.\n- Every touched namespace was selected explicitly with `-n` in both tiers. The slow run with `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1` passed **2 tests, 435 assertions**, including `completion-recovery-unknown-policy-transaction-is-reconciled-once-test` (46 assertions) and the driver's existing slow journal-cut case (389 assertions), zero failures/errors (`target/7c-slow.log`).\n- Final post-mutation focused quick verification, selecting the six touched suites plus rows, both safepoint harnesses, and semantic-register layout explicitly with `-n`: **260 tests, 6942 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors** (`target/7c-final-quick.log`).\n- Final post-mutation slow verification with the same explicit namespace selection and `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`: **8 tests, 5010 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors** (`target/7c-final-slow.log`). This includes the untouched rows and both harnesses as well as both touched slow cases.\n- Rows, the UCF safepoint harness, semantic-register safepoint harness, and semantic-register layout are verified without edits. No root-id pin moved.\n\n## Mutants\n\nEach mutation was applied alone, with a `cp`-equivalent byte backup (`shutil.copy2`), saved diff, foreground JVM run, and restoration in `finally`. After each restore, `git diff --no-index --quiet <backup> <file>` and `git diff --quiet -- src` both passed. Logs, diffs, backups, and summary: `target/7c-mutants/`; reproducible driver: `target/7c-mutants.py`. Each scoped run used `-n <namespace> -v <namespace/test>` and excluded slow tests.\n\n| Mutant | Change | Catching test | Failures / errors |\n|---|---|---|---:|\n| M1 | Store pack before recovery | driver `the-source-half-mints-fences-and-offers-before-candidacy-test` (F1) | 1 / 0 |\n| M2 | Attach before deciding custody | rows-custody `every-grant-check-refuses-before-any-attachment` | 18 / 0 |\n| M3 | Skip `accept-grant!` in row lower, through compose | compose-rows `row-2-the-source-emits-nothing-until-its-own-grant-test` | 9 / 0 |\n| M4 | Fenced lower restores gate `:running` | rows-custody `an-exclusive-blocked-root-under-fenced-skips-the-grant` | 1 / 0 |\n| M5 | Offer request carries wrong pack address | compose `a-whole-exclusive-handoff-runs-on-a-file-backed-authority-test` | 12 / 0 |\n| M6 | Swap before/after meaning in content-store cut seam | compose-rows `the-wired-compositions-content-store-cuts-test` | 1 / 1 |\n| M7 | Compose driver config ignores source index | compose `fork-runs-when-selected-and-is-labelled-fork-test` and `the-source-index-is-read-from-the-composition-test` | 6 / 0 |\n| M8 | Report origin check bypassed, admitting an unfenced halted baseline | compose-rows `a-composed-authority-refuses-an-unfenced-result-report-test` | 2 / 0 |\n| M9 | Q3 record-address conjunct removed | rows-custody `the-install-response-is-verified-before-any-attachment` (F2) | 2 / 0 |\n\nAll eight requested mutants and the extra F2 mutant were killed. M6 is a mutation of the test substrate's cut interpreter, not production code: its identity assertion fails before the swapped absence also causes decode to error. The first mutation attempt used `-v` without `-n`, which made the runner load unrelated Python suites and fail on missing generated lexer classes; those runs were discarded, stored under `target/7c-mutants-unscoped/`, and are not counted above.\n\n## Deviations\n\n1. No production seam change was necessary: existing source-index, header, grant, fenced recovery, and authority contracts support meaningful composition tests as landed.\n2. Equal-baseline variant generation varies pack row order rather than task counter, because the root id is part of the baseline. Successor mismatch cases still re-emit changed tasks/custody. This follows code semantics rather than weakening authority comparisons.\n3. Added the source-index and unfenced-report controls to make those mutant obligations explicit. Preserved the existing plain-composition subprocess contract.\n4. Per the owner ruling, `yin.repl/main.cljc` is untouched. Its old semantic kernel means its `:custody` branch remains inert until phase 8; this is an accepted gap, not a live custody proof. The no-REPL test proves the standalone semantic-register composition.\n5. No commit, including for the initial F1/F2 step, per the brief's explicit instruction.\n\n## Design defects (separate list)\n\n- **No new production/design mismatch found.** The root-inclusive baseline and recovery-first publication order are honored by the tests.\n- **Inherited D1 disposition:** authority accepts row packs only; the old-codec authority assertions no longer describe a supported path and were removed while preserving direct old-codec coverage. Authority was not redirected to the old inspector.\n- **Accepted Q2 gap:** REPL custody stays inert until its phase-8 kernel switch.\n- **Existing Q3 boundary (7b F5):** install verification checks response self-consistency (parent/link id and manifest/derivation/image), while binding content to composition-known addresses remains linker's fetch/admission responsibility. No widened admission or resolver was introduced.\n\n## What 7d needs\n\nPort `stage_e_world` source and receiver builders to semantic-register/rows-support; supply `:source-index` to each composition; replace old body-key and pending accesses with row task/custody decoding. Use pack addresses for store and offers, root ids only inside baselines. Re-emit changed task/custody variants; vary pack row order when an equal-root alternative encoding is required. Keep recovery-v2-first crash-cut meanings and fenced lower's exporting gate.\n\nThen run the Stage E in-process slow cases on the specified three hosts and its separate JVM/Node process matrix after building peers. None of that Stage E host/process acceptance is claimed here. Only 7d's Stage E suite remains red in the full UCF quick lane.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"agent_message","text":"The code confirms that lower checks install link/parent agreement and the manifest/derivation/image chain before attachment. Fenced recovery also restores the exact carried counter and keeps waits and parked records outside the scheduler. I’ll document those checks and close Q4’s implemented operation-id rules while leaving correlation agreement explicitly open."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1752,1769p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; sed -n '1638,1675p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; sed -n '800,827p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '3250,3277p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '1146,1189p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1752,1769p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; sed -n '1638,1675p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; sed -n '800,827p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '3250,3277p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '1146,1189p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md\"","aggregated_output":"   (let [opts (or opts {})\n         header (:header opts)\n         task (lift-task* m serve! opts :root)\n         kind (nth (:slots task) 2)\n         task (if (nil? header)\n                task\n                (let [n (:yin.k/next-op-seq header)]\n                  (when (and (not= :halted kind) (cbor/numeric? n) (cbor/num= n max-exact))\n                    (outcome! :yin.k/non-portable :op-seq-exhausted {:yin.k/next-op-seq n}))\n                  (protect! task (or (:yin.k/enrolled header) #{}))\n                  (assoc-in task [:slots 16] (header-custody header kind))))]\n     (custody-baseline task)\n     (emit task (merge default-budget (:budget opts))))))\n\n\n(def ^:private fixed-resource-keys\n  #{vm/call-in-stream-key vm/call-out-stream-key vm/call-out-cursor-key\n    module/link-request-resource module/link-response-resource})\nlater stage therefore cannot be masked by the root's earlier one, and\nzero streams are attached on any refusal (codex F1, F2, F4, F7; mutants\nthat attach early are killed by the attach-count law):\n\n| Stage | Function | Checks, in order | Refusal |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| stores | `check-stores!` | no key of the root store or of any module store is a reserved name (`vm/reserved-name?`), refused here rather than by restore's `store-put` | `:yin.k/undecodable :reserved-name` (`:yin.k/role :store-key`) |\n| pendings and install records | `check-pendings!` | the task's role slot equals its position (`:root`/`:child`); every `:install` pending names a carried install row; kind consistency (`:halted` has no waits, `:blocked` has waits, `:parked` names a key of its parked table); then every install record by `check-install!` (§5.2: phase ∈ `#{:running :parked}`, the retained response and image contract, the child `:blocked`) | `:role`, `:incomplete-install`, `:kind`, `:parked-id`, `:install-phase`, `:install-response`, `:install-child`, all `:yin.k/undecodable` |\n| dependencies | `check-dependencies!` | `requires` is a map whose `:yin.k/segments` equals the key set of `code` (`:requires`); every segment has a non-empty `:yin.k/trees` entry; every `store-of` of every `:closure` and `:env` row of **this task** (children excluded, each checked in its own stage pass) names one of this task's `:module-store` rows (§2.6), so a parent's or sibling's store of the same key cannot answer (codex F7, F8); every vector attaches in the receiving kernel under its A (`attaches?`: `module/attach-module` into a throwaway copy, the result discarded) (`:segment`); every `:closure` row names a `:closure` tuple of its vector at its entry (`:closure-origin`) with the same binders (`:closure-binders`); every `:prim` names a primitive the receiver holds | `:yin.k/undecodable :requires`, `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with `:yin.k/trees` or **`:yin.k/stores`** (`:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`), `:yin.k/undecodable :segment`, `:closure-origin`, `:closure-binders`, `:yin.k/unsatisfied {:yin.k/name p}` |\n| custody | `check-custody!` | the root's custody slot is nil (phase 6 carries fork only) | `:yin.k/unsupported :custody` |\n\nThen **attachment** (step 11, `attach-streams!`): one `attach!` per\ndistinct stream identity of the whole tree, `:yin.k/unsatisfied\n{:dao.stream/identity …}` when the composition declines. Then **restore**\n(step 12, `restore-task`): the carried vectors attached by A into a\nreceiver emptied of code, so frame and closure addresses map through the\nreceiver's own aliases and no source-local segment number travels; fresh\nresource keys per stream identity and per cursor cell; values re-sealed\nunder the receiver's owner; waits in `lower-frame!`'s entry shapes, a\n`tail` with `{:deliver :return}` and no window, env or pc; parked records\nwith a cycle guard; the store through `engine/store-put`; module stores;\ninstall children spawned by the engine's spawn discipline and then\nrestored recursively (the spawn's initial control is replaced, no\n`vm/run`); the free environment as both `:env` and `:free-env`; the id\ncounter `max(local, carried)`. Restore meets no carrier defect, by\nconstruction of the stages above. The lower does **not** re-discharge\nfree names against the receiver: a name a closure resolves through the\nregistry is the composition prerequisite of §2.5, and the receiver's\nregistry is kept as given. The kinds `:role :custody :incomplete-install\n:kind :parked-id :requires :segment :install-phase :install-response\n:install-child :closure-origin :closure-binders` and the `:yin.k/stores`\ncensus are the engineer's and the fix rounds' naming; the earlier text\nnamed none of them (deviation 9, accepted with the requirement that every\ndefect be a named transport outcome before attachment).\n\n---\n\n## 8. Slices, gates, entry conditions\n    operation inspector and admission checks of §7.7.8; an authority\n    never adds missing header keys.\n\n**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13; continuation §7.2).** The merged\n`rows/lower-task` runs steps 8-10 as four stages, **each over the whole\ntask tree before the next, and all before the first attachment** (step\n11): (a) stores: no reserved name keys the root store or a module store\n(`:yin.k/undecodable :reserved-name`); (b) pendings and install\nrecords: role, every `:install` pending names a carried install, kind\nconsistency, parked id, every install record as §5.4 as amended\n(`:role :incomplete-install :kind :parked-id :install-phase\n:install-response :install-child`); (c) dependencies: `requires`'\n`:yin.k/segments` equals `code`'s key set (`:requires`), every segment\nhas a non-empty `:yin.k/trees` entry, every `store-of` of this task's\nclosures and environments names one of **this task's** module-store\nrows (`:yin.k/unsatisfied`, `:yin.k/missing {:yin.k/stores …}`), every\nvector attaches in the receiving kernel under its A (`:segment`), every\nclosure row names a closure tuple of its vector with the same binders\n(`:closure-origin`, `:closure-binders`), every `:prim` is the receiver's\n(`:yin.k/unsatisfied`); (d) custody: nil only (`:yin.k/unsupported\n:custody`). Of step 8's text, the op-id rules and the correlation\nagreement are not implemented (fork-only custody has no `next-op-seq`;\ncontinuation §11 Q4); of step 9's, the census is not recomputed from the\nrows (continuation §11 Q2), and `requires` beyond segments, trees and\nprimitives (modules, streams, cursor profiles) is not evaluated on\nlower; free names are discharged at lift only (continuation §7.1).\nRefusals are thrown `ex-info` with `:yin.k/status` and a `:yin.k/kind`.\n11. **Composition.** Protection, resource, primitive and grant inputs of\n   carried is refused, a carried one is admitted, a child's carried\n   id counts, and an orphan successor gives no ancestry.\n5. Explicit park and install completeness (7.4.3), both versions.\n   A `:park` or `:call-effect` reason and an `:install` pending\n   without its entry refuse at decode; a `:parked` body's frames\n   restore in order; a response that fails verification and a phase\n   outside `:running` and `:parked` refuse; each of the two phases\n   crosses with each child kind `:blocked`, `:parked` and `:halted`,\n   a `:running` phase over a blocked child and an explicitly parked\n   child included, and lower preserves both fields; a runnable\n   child refuses export; a restored child continues from its saved\n   state without rerunning initialization or replaying the link\n   request. Version 1 adds: phase and parent required, and a\n   child's carried ids checked in the root's context. The two\n   version-0 defects of `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.3 are fixed with\n   version-0 tests. Stage: D.\n6. Envelope (7.7.8). Fixtures drop each key, change the dispatch\n   value, give a wrong author, an unbound lease, and a foreign id,\n   and nest an envelope-shaped program value. Assert each defective\n   form commits nothing, records nothing, and yields the structured\n   diagnostic with its defect and provenance and no admission\n   outcome; absent attribution is `:wrong-author` and an intent\n   that cannot be canonically encoded is `:malformed`; a failed or\n   backpressured diagnostic append admits nothing and is itself a\n   driver outcome; the nested value arrives as payload; no envelope key\n   appears in any body. Stage: C for the consumer, D for the writer.\n7. Epoch binding (7.7.8). First grant binds 0; each reclaim raises\n   the epoch by one in the lapse's transaction; a successor starts\n### 5.3 Lanes\n\nThe `:semantic-register` lane's wait entries are canonical frames plus\nengine fields. The de Bruijn lanes load `(:image (derive/stack-image v))`\nand `(:image (derive/register-image v))` of `v = (:vector (project P))`\nthrough `dvm/create-vm`/`rvm/create-vm`, run to the safepoint and lift\nthrough `layout/lift-act`/`lift-tail`; the stack lane's tail rows (6,\n9, 12, 20) require the seam of §4.3 and are gated on 6c-pre.\n\n**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), the shared fragment (R2; count\namendment ACCEPTED by the owner on 2026-10-13, §11 6c Q1).** The de\nBruijn lanes execute the shared fragment of the matrix: rows 1-6, 13-16,\n18-19, 21-22 and 24 under the four wrappers, and row 20. Rows 7-12 and\n17 are not built on them: a de Bruijn kernel links a native image while\nmodule responses carry A, and §4 defines no layout for install children.\nRow 4 is lifted from a de Bruijn source but not lowered into a de Bruijn\nreceiver until the retained-FFI seam (§4, 6c-post-D2) lands: the\nkernel's re-park names its own call pair. These lanes are a cross-engine\ncheck of the canonical frame and are removed with their kernels at phase\n8; the reference lane runs the whole matrix. *What the code does:* the\nharness's `*lane*` (`:semantic-register`, `:stack`, `:register`) selects\nthe kernel `machine` builds; `load-program` on a de Bruijn lane attaches\n`v`'s derived image beside what the kernel holds and starts it at the\nimage's offset (`kernel-load`), so parked records keep naming their\nrows; `lane-lift` is `rows/lift-task` on the reference lane and\n`layout/lift-task` over the world's `kernel-space` otherwise;\n`lane-lower` is `rows/lower-task` or `layout/lower-task` with a fresh\nscratch machine. `link-rows` is `#{7 8 9 10 11 12 17}`, `lane-cases`\nfilters `matrix-cases` by it (61 cases), `unlowerable` is `#{[4 :stack]\n[4 :register]}`. Every world's layout lookups run inside `scoped`: a\n`binding`-scoped atom `*projected*` records the vectors that world\nprojected and `kernel-space` reads that set, so an alpha-equivalent A an\nunrelated fixture projected widens no lookup; `kernel-space` throws when\nunbound, and only `file-wide` reads the whole file's `code-index`, in\nthe one fixture that shows the contamination (§8.3 item 14). *Why:*\nlink rows need the harness to answer a `require` in\nthe kernel's own format (H or R) while module responses carry A, install\nchildren are whole kernels the layout would lift and lower recursively,\nand the retained-FFI re-park is a kernel edit (§4.3); 2-3 days of\nharness and layout code for kernels deleted at phase 8 was not worth\nit (R2). The sentence above on `lift-act`/`lift-tail` is superseded by\n§4.1: the lift is `lift-state`, dispatching on the kernel's kind.\n\n### 5.4 Laws\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat > /tmp/amend7e.py <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nC=Path('docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md')\nT=Path('docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md')\nU=Path('docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md')\nM='**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)**'\ndef rep(s,a,b):\n    assert a in s,a[:100]\n    return s.replace(a,b,1)\ndef span(s,a,b,new):\n    start=s.index(a); end=s.index(b,start)\n    return s[:start]+new+s[end:]\ns=C.read_text()\ns=span(s,'- **Custody is nil at the root in phase 6.**','### 2.6',f'''- {M} — Reason: phase 7 admits exclusive custody on rows.\n  Fork custody is nil on every task row. An exclusive blocked or parked\n  root carries policy, occurrence, arbitration and next-op-seq, with\n  origin on a successor; a halted exclusive root carries origin only.\n  Every child has nil custody and is checked in the root's context.\n\n''')\ns=span(s,'No \"'`layout` slot and no native image:'\"','### 2.7',f'''No \"'`layout` slot and no native image: those were the positional\nprofiles'\"' (v2 amendment §2, §4.1).\n\n{M} — Reason: the owner chose nil fork custody and phase 7 enforces the slot grammar.\nThe custody slot admits only \"'`:yin.k/policy`, `:yin.k/occurrence`,\n`:yin.k/arbitration`, `:yin.k/next-op-seq` and `:yin.k/origin`.\nFork roots of every kind and all children carry nil. Exclusive blocked\nor parked roots require the first four keys, with policy\n`:yin.k/exclusive`, and carry origin when present; halted exclusive\nroots carry origin only. A header-bearing halt without origin refuses.\nNo current lease, epoch or holder enters the body; the predecessor lease\ninside origin is lineage evidence.\n\n'\"''')\ns=s.replace('These lanes are a cross-engine\\\\ncheck of the canonical frame and are removed with their kernels at phase\\\\n8; the reference lane runs the whole matrix.',f'''{M} — Reason: the owner retains the de Bruijn kernels as backends after phase 8.\nThese lanes check the canonical frame on the documented backend fragment\n(§4.6); they remain after phase 8 and are not widened. The canonical\nlane runs the whole matrix.''')\ns=s.replace('harness and layout code for kernels deleted at phase 8 was not worth\\\\nit (R2).','harness and layout code was excluded by the fragment ruling (R2,\\\\nupdated by §4.6).')\ns=s.replace('a kernel edit that dies at phase 8','a kernel edit outside the retained backend fragment (§4.6)')\ns=s.replace('link and install rows need 2-3 days of code for kernels deleted at phase 8','link and install rows need 2-3 days of code outside the retained backend fragment (§4.6)')\ns=rep(s,'## 5. The safepoint harness',f'''### 4.6 The retained backend fragment\n\n{M} — Reason: the owner retains both de Bruijn kernels as semantic-register VM backends.\nThe stack and register kernels are backends of the semantic-register VM,\nchecked through the layouts on the shared fragment in §5.3. They remain\nthrough and after phase 8; the fragment is not widened. It excludes link\nand install rows 7-12 and 17, and row 4 is not lowered into either\nbackend while the retained-FFI seam remains absent. A closure or\ncontinuation in the free environment, ambiguous images, install children,\nand the register payload's unsupported primitives refuse as documented\nin §4.1-4.4 and §7.2, before attachment on lower. Stage E uses the\ncanonical semantic-register kernel only. A is the only code identity;\nnative images are derived forms, and the source AST remains retrievable\nthrough the linker's source-index, fetch and admission path (§6.1).\n\n## 5. The safepoint harness''')\ns=rep(s,'8. **The task.** \"'`requires`'\"',f'''{M} — Reason: \"'`lift-task` now takes `:header` and runs custody checks after the walk.\nThe header is nil for a fork; otherwise it contains occurrence,\narbitration, next-op-seq, optional origin and the local `:yin.k/enrolled`\nset. That set never enters the pack. The linker/composition supplies\n`:source-index` as `{{A #{{tree-address}}}}`; code and trees use its existing\nfetch and admission path, with A the only code identity and the AST\nretained. The holder retains serve answers by `[task-path resource-id]`;\nencode uses those answers without serving externally.\n\n8. **The task.** `requires`'\"''')\ns=rep(s,'the root is assembled with \"'`custody nil` (§2.5).'\"','the abstract task is initially assembled with nil custody.')\ns=rep(s,'10. **Emit** (\"'`emit`): canonical cursor-cell numbering and rows.'\"', '''10. **Header and custody, then emit.** After \"'`lift-task*` has validated\n    frames, a non-nil header refuses `:op-seq-exhausted` at 2''^52-1 for\n    blocked/parked roots, checks every retained write of the tree against\n    enrollment (`:unprotected-pending` when an enrolled target lacks an\n    op-id; `:yin.k/unsatisfied` when an id targets an unenrolled stream),\n    and writes the root'\"'s custody slot by kind. \"'`custody-baseline` then\n    validates the whole tree, including fork op-id refusal. `emit`\n    numbers cursor cells canonically and sorts rows by the string of\n    their id. The BLAKE3 pack address hashes the encoded whole pack;\n    the root id hashes the structural task row. The walk can already\n    have served streams before a custody/protection refusal; retained\n    successful answers are reused on retry.'\"''')\ns=rep(s,'''**Amended after 6b (2026-10-13): the lower's staged checks as\n\"'`lower-task` runs them, and its refusal kinds.**'\"''',f'''{M} — Reason: phase 7 adds install verification and one tree-wide custody stage.\nThe lower's staged checks as \"'`lower-task` runs them, and its refusal kinds.'\"''')\ns=rep(s,'''Steps 8-10 then run as four stages, **each over the whole task\ntree (root and every install child, pre-order) before the next**, and''','''Steps 8-10 run stores, pendings/install records and dependencies,\n**each over the whole task tree (root and every install child,