{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '109,132p' collab/phase7-plan.md; sed -n '916,966p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc; sed -n '1814,1822p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; sed -n '1880,1920p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; sed -n '178,198p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/compose.cljc; sed -n '3306,3320p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; rg -n '## .*##|lower\\\\. the|dies at phase 8|kernels die|deleted at phase 8|three engines intended' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '109,132p' collab/phase7-plan.md; sed -n '916,966p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc; sed -n '1814,1822p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; sed -n '1880,1920p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc; sed -n '178,198p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/compose.cljc; sed -n '3306,3320p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; rg -n '## .*##|lower\\\\. the|dies at phase 8|kernels die|deleted at phase 8|three engines intended' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\"","aggregated_output":"\n## 3. Design questions phase 7 forces, with recommendation, cost, and who decides\n\n### 3.1 Exclusive and holder custody on rows: which rows carry lease, fence and holder state\n\n**Finding.** The root row's slot 16 `custody` is plain data (draft §5.5 table). UCF 7.2.1 is operative for version 3: \"no body carries an epoch; the epoch is learned only from the grant binding\". The holder state the driver needs at run time (`:yin.k/custody` on the machine: occurrence, lease, holder, epoch, arbitration, next-op-seq, `:protection`, `:input {:next :prefix}`, `:tenure`) is built by the lower from the **grant evidence**, never from the body (`accept-grant!` today). The fence (op-ids) already has slots: `:put [stream V op-id]`, `:ffi-request [... op-id]`, `:link-request [... envelope op-id]`.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides).**\n- The root `custody` slot carries exactly the draft's table for `:blocked`/`:parked` exclusive roots: `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence O :yin.k/arbitration {...} :yin.k/next-op-seq n}` plus `:yin.k/origin` on a successor; a halted exclusive root carries `{:yin.k/origin ...}` only; children nil (as now). Lease, epoch, holder, enrolled set, protection classes: **never** in a row.\n- `lift-task` gains a `:header` option (the same header `holder.export/prepare` takes today). With a header it writes the slot by kind, refuses `:op-seq-exhausted` at 2^52-1 on a blocked or parked root, `:unprotected-pending` for a retained `:put`/`:ffi-request`/`:link-request` on an enrolled target without an op-id (root or child), and `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream for an op-id on an unenrolled target; a halted root with a header but no origin is refused (not downgraded). These are the version-1 rules, moved (`handoff.cljc:623-657`, `:1513-1523`).\n- `lower-task` replaces `check-custody!` (nil only) by the version-1 custody stage: fork root under `:exclusive` → `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; exclusive root of kind `:blocked`/`:parked` requires `:grant` + `:protection` opts and runs the seven grant checks of `accept-grant!` (`handoff.cljc:3343-3420`, ported verbatim, keyed off the slot instead of body keys); every marker's protection class must match enrollment in both directions; op-id presence must equal enrollment per retained write; the restored machine gets `:yin.k/custody` from the evidence and `:yin.k/next-op-seq` from the slot **exactly** (no max); a halted exclusive root lowers with no grant, gated `:ended`. A `:fenced` option (recovery, §3.2) skips the grant and gates `:exporting`.\n- The custody stage keeps its place: all four stages over the whole tree before the first `attach!` (continuation §7.2, codex F1/F2/F4/F7).\n\n**Fork spelling (OWNER decides, §3.10 Q1).** Phase 6 writes `custody nil` for a fork root; the draft's table spells `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}` on blocked/parked fork roots and nil on halted ones. Both decide the policy from the root alone. Nil is landed and every pinned root id in `rows_test` and the harness depends on it; the explicit spelling costs about half a day of pin regeneration and makes the slot's reader one `case` on `:yin.k/policy`. I recommend **keeping nil** and amending the draft's table (fork = nil in every row), on the minimal-diff principle; the owner may prefer the draft's spelling.\n\n**Cost.** 1.5 days in 7a (lift option, three refusals, the ported grant checks, the protection stage, tests on three hosts).\n\n### 3.2 The export-recovery object re-based on rows\n\n**Finding.** Version 1 (`holder.export/freeze`) stores two bodies: the published body and a \"snapshot\" lifted with `::recovery true` (full census), because version-0 bodies omitted transitive module stores and the recovery must rehydrate everything. It also stores `:yin.k/header`, `:yin.k/descriptors` (the served set) and `:yin.k/state` (`gate`, `origins`, `issues` per wait, `issued`, children recursively). `rehydrate-fenced` restores the snapshot with `rehydrate-fenced-body` (gate `:exporting`, waits in `:yin.k/recovery-waits`, parked table emptied into the record, `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids` mapping resource ids back to carrier cursor cells), re-prepares from the retained served table and proves the published bytes are reproduced.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides).** Version 2:\n\n```clojure\n(defn lower-defect\n  \"What a kernel of `kind` cannot hold, judged on decoded task `task`\n   before anything is attached: two carried A of one image (§4.1), install\n   children (not carried by this phase's layouts), a closure or\n   continuation in the free environment (§6.3), and for the register\n   kernel a primitive function in the payload data of a wait or parked\n   state (a window, a lexical binding, or reached through them), which\n   its restore refuses as `:continuation-registers` (`vm/machine-data?`).\n   nil when it can.\"\n  [task kind]\n  (let [slots (:slots task)\n        code (nth slots 11)\n        collision (image-collision code kind)\n        free (free-env-procedure task)]\n    (cond\n      collision\n      (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :ambiguous-image\n             :yin.k/image (first collision) :yin.k/segments (second collision))\n\n      (seq (nth slots 12))\n      (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :installs)\n\n      free\n      (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :free-env-value :yin.k/path free)\n\n      (= :register kind)\n      (when-let [path (or (some (fn [[i w]] (state-prim code (first (:slots w)) [:frames i]))\n                                (map-indexed vector (nth slots 13)))\n                          (some (fn [[p st]] (state-prim code st [:parked p]))\n                                (partition 2 (:slots (nth slots 8)))))]\n        (assoc {} :yin.k/status :yin.k/unsupported :yin.k/kind :continuation-registers :yin.k/path path))\n\n      :else nil)))\n\n\n(defn lower-task\n  \"§7.2 into a de Bruijn kernel: decode `wire`, refuse what kernel `k`\n   cannot hold (`lower-defect`) before any attach, then `rows/lower-task`\n   into `scratch` (a fresh semantic-register machine of the same\n   composition: the carrier's steps 1-11 and restore) and `kernel-task`\n   into `k`. Throws the first refusal as ex-data.\"\n  ([scratch k wire attach!] (lower-task scratch k wire attach! {}))\n  ([scratch k wire attach! opts]\n   (let [task (rows/decode wire (merge rows/default-budget (:budget opts)))]\n     (when (= :task (:kind task))\n       (when-let [d (lower-defect task (kernel-kind k))]\n         (throw (ex-info \"Layout refused\" d))))\n     (kernel-task (rows/lower-task scratch wire attach! opts) k))))\n(defn- attaches?\n  \"The receiving kernel admits vector v (its loader's validation), under\n   address a when a is given.\"\n  [recv v a]\n  (try (let [r (module/attach-module recv v)] (or (nil? a) (contains? (:code-aliases r) a)))\n       (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _e false)))\n\n\n(defn- check-install!\n  (let [[a entry params] (:slots c)\n        v (get code a)\n        _ (when (nil? v) (outcome! :yin.k/undecodable :segment {:yin.k/segment a}))\n        tuple (first (filter #(and (= :closure (nth % 0)) (= entry (nth % 3))) v))]\n    (cond\n      (nil? tuple) (outcome! :yin.k/undecodable :closure-origin {:yin.k/segment a :yin.k/entry entry})\n      (not= params (nth tuple 2)) (outcome! :yin.k/undecodable :closure-binders {:yin.k/segment a :yin.k/entry entry})\n      :else nil)))\n\n\n(defn- check-dependencies!\n  \"Step 9, dependency closure: requires and code agree, every segment has\n   its trees (§6.1), every closure's and frame's store-of names a module\n   store of this task (not a parent's or sibling's of the same key), every\n   segment is a vector the receiver's kernel admits under its A, every\n   closure names a closure of its code, every :prim is the receiver's.\"\n  [recv task _role]\n  (let [[_ _ _ _ _ _ _ module-stores _ _ requires code] (:slots task)]\n    (when-not (and (map? requires) (= (set (:yin.k/segments requires)) (set (keys code))))\n      (outcome! :yin.k/undecodable :requires {}))\n    (let [trees (:yin.k/trees requires)\n          missing (into #{} (remove #(seq (get trees %))) (keys code))]\n      (when (seq missing)\n        (outcome! :yin.k/unsatisfied nil {:yin.k/discovery :incomplete :yin.k/missing {:yin.k/trees missing}})))\n    ;; §2.6: every store-of resolves to a module store this task carries\n    (let [census (into #{} (map #(first (:slots %))) module-stores)\n          missing (into #{} (comp (keep #(nth (:slots %) (if (= :closure (:kind %)) 4 1)))\n                                  (remove census))\n                        (concat (rows-of task :closure true) (rows-of task :env true)))]\n      (when (seq missing)\n        (outcome! :yin.k/unsatisfied nil {:yin.k/discovery :incomplete :yin.k/missing {:yin.k/stores missing}})))\n    (doseq [[a v] (sort-by #(str (key %)) code)]\n      (when-not (attaches? recv v a)\n        (outcome! :yin.k/undecodable :segment {:yin.k/segment a})))\n    (run! #(check-closure! code %) (rows-of task :closure true))\n    (doseq [p (rows-of task :prim true)]\n      (when-not (contains? (:primitives recv) (first (:slots p)))\n        (outcome! :yin.k/unsatisfied nil {:yin.k/name (first (:slots p))})))))\n\n\n(defn- accept-grant!\n(defn- check-common!\n  [config]\n  (check-interval! (:units config) (:renewal-interval config))\n  (check! (and (map? (:store config))\n               (fn? (:put-bytes-fn (:store config)))\n               (fn? (:get-bytes-fn (:store config))))\n          \"a content store: a dao.jing byte-store handle\" {})\n  (check! (some? (:diagnostics config)) \"a diagnostic stream: a writer\" {})\n  (check! (some? (:medium config)) \"a carrier medium name\" {})\n  (check! (fn? (:clock config)) \"a lease clock: (fn [] reading)\" {})\n  (check! (fn? (:source-index config))\n          \"a source index: (fn [] {A #{tree-address}})\" {}))\n\n\n(defn- log-handle\n  \"A fresh memory log: the composition's tick and lease-fact media.\"\n  []\n  (:dao.stream/handle\n    (memory-log/create! {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/memory-log})))\n\n\n                 ;; version 2 is a fork exactly when it carries no custody\n                 ;; header key, and only a root is a custody subject\n                 (and (not install-child?)\n                      (not-any? #(contains? body %)\n                                [:yin.k/policy :yin.k/occurrence\n                                 :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/origin\n                                 :yin.k/next-op-seq]))\n                 (not= 1 v)))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/profile-mismatch\n               {:yin.k/version v\n                :yin.k/supported #{1 2}\n                :yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive}))))\n\n\n(defn- custody-inspect!\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md')\ns=p.read_text().replace('VM, checked on their documented fragment and refusing the unsupported\\\\n  remainder before attach (continuation design §4.6). They are retained','VM, checked on their documented fragment and required to refuse the\\\\n  unsupported remainder before attach (continuation design §4.6). They are retained')\np.write_text(s)\np=Path('docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md')\ns=p.read_text().replace('backend while the retained-FFI seam remains absent. A closure or','backend while the retained-FFI seam remains absent. This row-4 exclusion\\\\nis a harness limit, not a general pre-attach rejection in \"'`lower-defect`'\"\\\\n(§11 6c Q2); the owner\\\\'s refuse-the-remainder requirement is not evidence\\\\nof such a gate. A closure or')\np.write_text(s)\nPY\ncat > collab/1792530000000-engineer-7e-docs.report.md <<'EOF'\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-14 +07\n# Slice 7e: phase-7 documentation\n\n## Plain summary (15 lines)\nUpdated only the three authorized design documents and this report.\nEvery amended clause carries the phase-7 date marker and a reason.\nFork custody is nil on every task row.\nExclusive root custody is stated by kind and restricted to five keys.\nCurrent lease, epoch and holder remain outside published packs.\nLift now documents the header, protection checks and retained serves.\nLower documents three per-tree stages followed by custody once and attach.\nInstall verification states the actual parent and manifest/derivation/image checks.\nFenced recovery restores exact counters and keeps all waits and parked records detached.\nRecovery format 2 holds one pack, its identities, local header and local state.\nPack storage identity is distinguished from structural root identity.\nEqual-baseline variants reorder the same root; counter variants conflict.\nPhase-7 entry readiness is distinguished from pending host acceptance.\nThe two de Bruijn backends remain after phase 8 on their documented fragment.\nThe report leaves correlation, backend limits and remaining owner questions visible.\n\n## Edits by file and section\n\n- \"'`docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`: §2.5-2.6 custody by kind/five keys; §7.1 header, actual lift order, sorted emit and pack/root identities; §7.2 three tree passes, custody once, install verification, grant checkpoint and fenced restore; §8.4 entry conditions met with 7d review/host limits; new §10.8 inventory; §11 closes 6b Q3 as implemented and Q4'\"'s op-id arm, preserves correlation and all other open questions, records 7a D2. New §4.6 makes the requested UCF pointer resolve; §4.3, §5.3, §10.7 and 6c Q1/Q2 remove stale kernel-deletion rationale while preserving the fragment and open seam question.\n- \"'`docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`: §5.4 actual install checks and all parked records; §5.5 nil fork custody and five-key restriction; §7 BLAKE3 pack address versus root, sorted emission versus permissive row-order decoding; §8 actual staged lower/op-id rules and remaining correlation/census limitations; §9 header and administrative fenced lowering.\n- `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`: §7.7.4 header/source-index seam, recovery format 2 exact shape, no snapshot body, fenced rehydration and actual lift checks; §7.11 backend/fragment wording; §7.11.1 canonical kernel and its two backends, continuation §4.6 pointer, clauses 1-5/10 on rows, stage letters and `yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint` citation.\n- This report holds the orchestrator-log text; `docs/orchestrator-log.md` was not edited.\n\n## Sources and verification\n\nRead `collab/phase7-plan.md` §3.1-3.9 and slices, `collab/7a-report.md` including D3, the timestamped 7b/7c reports, `collab/7c-review.md` and the 7d report. `collab/7a-review.md` is absent in this worktree. Checked the relevant implementations in `rows.cljc`, `rows/checkpoint.cljc`, `handoff.cljc`, `holder/export.cljc`, `holder/driver.cljc`, `compose.cljc`, and additionally `semantic_register/layout.cljc` for the backend pre-attach limit.\n\nVerification was foreground-only: reviewed the three document diffs against code, checked amendment markers and section references, checked balanced Markdown fences and authorized file scope, and ran `git diff --check`. No runtime tests were run for these documentation-only changes. No code, tests, pins, prompt, plan or orchestrator log was edited; no commit or remote action was made. Reported JVM results belong to 7d, not to this documentation slice. A non-Codex review remains the owner'\"'s next step.\n\n## Design defects: discrepancies between plan and code\n\n1. **7a D2 / 7c F3:** plan §1.1's id-counter variant cannot have an equal baseline after changing the root; the baseline includes \"'`:yin.k/root`, so it is `:variant-conflict`. The equal-baseline alternative keeps the root and reverses row order. `decode` admits unsorted packs; one root/one pack holds for sorted `emit`, not every admitted encoding. The documents state code and the owner ruling; the plan is outside this slice'\"'s edit scope.\n2. **Recovery sketch:** plan §3.2 includes a redundant \"'`:yin.k/descriptors` set and passes `:header` to raw fenced lower. Format 2 has only tag/version, pack address/root/base64 pack, local header and local state. `rehydrate-fenced` checks header/state before `rows/lower-task {:fenced true}`; attached pack descriptors rebuild the retained served table. No second snapshot body or compatibility reader exists.\n3. **Custody staging:** plan §3.1 says four stages over the tree. Code runs stores, pendings/install records and dependencies as three passes, then `check-custody!` once for the tree. All precede attachment. Grammar resides in `rows/custody-baseline`; the rows inspector wraps it, avoiding a namespace cycle.\n4. **Lift ordering (7a D4):** serve operations happen during the walk before exhaustion/protection/custody refusal. The holder retains successful serves and reuses them on retry; no zero-serve promise was added.\n5. **7d source-index seam:** the plan'\"'s slice says holder-seams supplies source-index. Code requires a function on \"'`compose/open!` and forbids holder override; compose forwards it to the driver. Trees are associations, not a new identity or resolver. A alone identifies code; source-index/fetch/admission keeps the AST retrievable.\n6. **Install verification boundary:** Q3 is implemented through response self-consistency: non-nil parent/link-id equality, manifest-named derivation hash, tree input and image-A output, plus image admission. Code does not add an independent claimed-image-address field check or fetch/admission path; composition-known address binding stays with the linker. The stronger old prose is identified as intended contract and the actual checks are stated.\n7. **Retained backends:** old plan/revision rationale said the de Bruijn kernels die at phase 8. The binding owner ruling keeps them as backends, without widening the fragment. `layout/lower-defect` checks image collision, installs, free-env procedures and register payload primitives before attach; it does not implement a universal fragment-membership gate. In particular, retained-FFI row 4 is excluded by the harness, not rejected there before attach. UCF states refuse-the-remainder as the owner'\"'s requirement, and continuation §4.6 states this code limit rather than claiming the missing gate exists. 6c Q2 remains open.\n8. **Acceptance inventory/evidence:** the plan's Stage E inventory says 12 tests in each suite; 7d reports 13 each. Its JVM suites and clean JVM/JVM process gate do not prove Node/Dart or killed four-pair JVM/Node acceptance. No new production defect was established by this documentation work.\n\n## Open questions\n\n6b Q1/Q10 (install wire phase/parked child), Q2 (recomputed census), Q5 (registry re-discharge), Q6 (live-code conservatism), Q7 (primitive shadowing), Q8 (preflight cost), Q9 (non-fresh receiver), and Q4's broader correlation agreement remain open. 6c Q2/Q3 retain their open status; 6c Q1's accepted 61/350 matrix remains normative. The row-4 backend refusal gap above is evidence for Q2, not an implemented seam or fragment expansion. REPL custody is an accepted gap until phase 8. Tri-host Stage E, killed process acceptance and the non-Codex documentation review remain owner/orchestrator work.\n\n## Orchestrator-log text (10 lines; owner to paste)\n\n\"'```text\n2026-10-14 — Slice 7e documents the phase-7 row carrier against code and 7a-7d reports.\nOnly continuation, transport-tuples and UCF designs changed; orchestrator log was left to its owner.\nCustody is nil for every fork row; exclusive roots use the five-key slot by kind.\nLift header/protection order and retained serves are stated as implemented.\nLower runs three tree passes, custody once, then attachment; Q3 install checks are documented.\nFenced recovery keeps all tasks exporting, exact counters and detached waits/parked records.\nRecovery format 2 stores one pack plus local header/state, before fencing both stored objects.\nPack BLAKE3 address is storage identity; root is in baseline, so counter variants conflict.\nBackends remain after phase 8 on their fragment; correlation and backend refusal gaps stay visible.\nDocs checks pass; non-Codex review and the remaining Stage E host/process gates are still required.\n```\n\nREADY\nEOF'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
